window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["theory-of-computation"]=[{"id": "TOC-U1-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Over the alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span>, the minimal DFA that accepts exactly those strings which contain an <b>even</b> number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s has how many states?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "Infinitely many"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA cannot count; it can only remember a finite amount of information. For the parity of the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s the only thing that matters is whether the count seen so far is even or odd — two facts, hence at most two states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">E</span> = \"even number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s so far\" (start state, and final because the empty string has zero <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s). Let <span class=\"mm\">O</span> = \"odd number so far\".</li><li><span class=\"mm\">δ(E,a) = O</span>, <span class=\"mm\">δ(E,b) = E</span> — a <span class=\"mm\">b</span> does not change the parity.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">δ(O,a) = E</span>, <span class=\"mm\">δ(O,b) = O</span>.</li><li>Both states are reachable (<span class=\"mm\">ε</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">E</span>, <span class=\"mm\">a</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">O</span>) and distinguishable (<span class=\"mm\">E</span> is final, <span class=\"mm\">O</span> is not). So no further merging is possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 2 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates add a third \"dead\" state, imagining that a misplaced <span class=\"mm\">b</span> must be rejected. It must not: <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s are simply ignored, so the transition on <span class=\"mm\">b</span> stays inside the two parity states. Equally wrong is \"infinitely many\", which forgets that a DFA only needs the parity, not the count.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In DFA minimisation by the table-filling method, two states <span class=\"mm\">p</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q</span> of a complete DFA are <b>distinguishable</b> exactly when:</p>", "options": ["there is a string w ∈ Σ* such that exactly one of δ̂(p,w) and δ̂(q,w) is a final state.", "p and q are both final states.", "p and q are both non-final states.", "no string takes p and q to different states."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> States are equivalent when no future input can tell them apart, i.e. when for every string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> the two runs agree on acceptance. Distinguishable is the negation of that: some string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> makes exactly one run end in a final state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take <span class=\"mm\">w = ε</span>. If one state is final and the other is not, they are already distinguishable.</li><li>Otherwise try longer strings: <span class=\"mm\">p</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q</span> are distinguishable if for some symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span> the states <span class=\"mm\">δ(p,a)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">δ(q,a)</span> are distinguishable.</li><li>Table filling propagates this rule until no new pair is marked; unmarked pairs are merged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options (B) and (C) are necessary conditions only for <span class=\"mm\">w = ε</span>: two final states, or two non-final states, may still be told apart by a longer string. Option (D) describes <i>equivalence</i>, the opposite of distinguishability.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA accepts a string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> if and only if:</p>", "options": ["at least one computation path labelled w leads from the start state to a final state.", "every computation path labelled w leads from the start state to a final state.", "the NFA has no ε-transitions on the path.", "exactly one computation path labelled w reaches a final state."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nondeterminism is existential acceptance: the NFA guesses, and one successful guess is enough.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Running an NFA on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> produces a set of reachable states rather than a single state.</li><li>The string is accepted when that set meets the set of final states.</li><li>Dead-end branches are simply abandoned; they do not veto acceptance.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is universal acceptance, which is <i>not</i> how an NFA accepts — an NFA with one accepting and one dead branch still accepts. Option (D) confuses \"at least one\" with \"exactly one\".</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> states over <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span> is converted to an equivalent DFA by the subset construction. The number of states of the resulting DFA is at most:</p>", "options": ["2^n", "n²", "n", "n + 1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In the subset construction each DFA state is a <i>set</i> of NFA states, and there are <span class=\"mm\">2^n</span> such sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The start state of the DFA is <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(q₀)</span>.</li><li>For a DFA state <span class=\"mm\">S</span> and symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, the successor is the <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-closure of the union of <span class=\"mm\">δ(p,a)</span> over all <span class=\"mm\">p ∈ S</span>.</li><li>Every DFA state is a subset of the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> NFA states, so there can be no more than <span class=\"mm\">2^n</span> of them; only the reachable subsets actually appear.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — at most <span class=\"mm\">2^n</span> states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <span class=\"mm\">2^n</span> is an upper bound, not the usual size: most NFAs produce far fewer reachable subsets, and the DFA can then be minimised further. Candidates who read the bound as \"exactly <span class=\"mm\">2^n</span>\" lose the question.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The language denoted by the regular expression <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*abb</span> is:</p>", "options": ["the set of all strings over {a,b} that end in abb.", "the set of all strings over {a,b} that contain abb as a substring.", "the set of all strings over {a,b} that begin with abb.", "the set of all strings over {a,b} that contain exactly one occurrence of abb."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Concatenation in a regular expression fixes order: <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span> consumes an arbitrary prefix, and the trailing <span class=\"mm\">abb</span> must be the last three symbols of the string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Any string of the form <span class=\"mm\">x·abb</span> with <span class=\"mm\">x ∈ {a,b}*</span> is generated: choose <span class=\"mm\">x</span> from <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span> and append <span class=\"mm\">abb</span>.</li><li>Conversely, every generated string literally ends in <span class=\"mm\">abb</span>.</li><li>So the language is exactly the strings with suffix <span class=\"mm\">abb</span>, e.g. <span class=\"mm\">abb</span>, <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>, <span class=\"mm\">abbabb</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*abb(a+b)*</span> — with the trailing star — is the \"contains\" language (option B). Dropping the final <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span> pins <span class=\"mm\">abb</span> to the end.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which regular expression denotes exactly the set of all strings over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span> that contain at least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span>?</p>", "options": ["(a+b)*a(a+b)*", "a*", "(a+b)*", "b*ab*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> \"At least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\" is expressed by isolating one required <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and letting arbitrary text surround it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span> before the required <span class=\"mm\">a</span> generates any prefix.</li><li>The single <span class=\"mm\">a</span> is the witness that at least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> occurs.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span> after it generates any suffix, including more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*a(a+b)*</span> accepts <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">ba</span>, <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>, <span class=\"mm\">aab</span>, <span class=\"mm\">bbaab</span> … and rejects only <span class=\"mm\">b*</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) <span class=\"mm\">b*ab*</span> allows only <i>one</i> <span class=\"mm\">a</span>; option (C) allows strings with no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> at all; option (B) <span class=\"mm\">a*</span> forces every symbol to be <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements is <b>FALSE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every NFA has an equivalent DFA.", "Every DFA is a special case of an NFA.", "A language accepted by an NFA with ε-transitions need not be regular.", "NFAs and DFAs accept exactly the same class of languages."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Adding nondeterminism and <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves does not add expressive power: both machines characterise the regular languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions are eliminated by taking <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-closures; the subset construction then yields a DFA for the same language.</li><li>Hence every <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-NFA language is regular — statement (C) is false, and it is the required answer.</li><li>(A), (B) and (D) are the standard equivalences.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — the false statement.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students expect that <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves buy extra power, because they look like spontaneous computation. They only shorten the description; the accepted class is unchanged.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The pumping lemma for regular languages is used to:</p>", "options": ["prove that certain languages are not regular.", "prove that a given language is regular.", "minimise a given DFA.", "convert an NFA into an equivalent DFA."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The lemma is a necessary condition: every regular language satisfies it. Therefore failing it proves non-regularity, while satisfying it proves nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is regular with a DFA of <span class=\"mm\">p</span> states, every <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ p</span> can be split <span class=\"mm\">w = xyz</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span>, <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">xy^i z ∈ L</span> for all <span class=\"mm\">i ≥ 0</span>.</li><li>So a language that violates this for <i>every</i> valid split is not regular.</li><li>No converse is available: <span class=\"mm\">{a^n b^n : n ≥ 0}</span> fails it, but a non-regular language can still satisfy it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the pumping lemma to claim a language <i>is</i> regular (option B). To prove regularity you must exhibit a DFA, an NFA or a regular expression.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L = {w ∈ {0,1}* : #₀(w) ≡ 0 (mod 3) and #₁(w) ≡ 0 (mod 2)}</span>. The minimal DFA accepting <span class=\"mm\">L</span> has how many states?</p>", "options": ["5", "6", "8", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A conjunction of two independent modular conditions is recognised by the <i>product</i> of the two machines; the state count multiplies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The condition <span class=\"mm\">#₀ ≡ 0 mod 3</span> needs 3 states (the residues 0, 1, 2).</li><li>The condition <span class=\"mm\">#₁ ≡ 0 mod 2</span> needs 2 states (even, odd).</li><li>The product machine tracks the pair <span class=\"mm\">(i, j)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">i = #₀ mod 3</span> and <span class=\"mm\">j = #₁ mod 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">3 × 2 = 6</span> states, and every pair is reachable.</li><li>All six are pairwise distinguishable (residue classes of a mod-3 counter and a mod-2 counter are distinct), so the product is already minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 6 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the counts (<span class=\"mm\">3 + 2 = 5</span>) instead of multiplying them, or forgetting that the two conditions are independent and must be tracked simultaneously.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Taking a DFA to be <b>complete</b> (a transition is defined for every state and symbol), the minimal DFA accepting <span class=\"mm\">L(a(a+b)*)</span> has how many states?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a(a+b)*</span> is \"every string that starts with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\". Three facts must be remembered: nothing read yet, a good prefix, a hopeless prefix.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> (start, non-final): on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> go to <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span>, on <span class=\"mm\">b</span> go to <span class=\"mm\">q_d</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> (final): the first symbol was <span class=\"mm\">a</span>; every later symbol keeps the string valid, so <span class=\"mm\">δ(q₁,a) = δ(q₁,b) = q₁</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q_d</span> (dead, non-final): the first symbol was <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; nothing can repair it, so <span class=\"mm\">δ(q_d,a) = δ(q_d,b) = q_d</span>.</li><li>The three states are reachable and pairwise distinguishable (<span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q_d</span> differ on <span class=\"mm\">a</span>; <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> is the only final state), so 3 is minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2, which is correct only for an <i>incomplete</i> transition graph where <span class=\"mm\">q_d</span> is simply omitted. GATE convention uses complete DFAs, and the minimal complete DFA has 3 states.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">To prove that <span class=\"mm\">L = {a^n b^n : n ≥ 0}</span> is not regular using the pumping lemma, which argument is correct?</p>", "options": ["Assume the pumping length is p and take w = a^p b^p. Then |xy| ≤ p forces y = a^k with k ≥ 1, so xy²z = a^{p+k} b^p ∉ L.", "Assume the pumping length is p and take w = (ab)^p. Pumping y = ab gives (ab)^{p+1} ∉ L.", "Assume the pumping length is p and take w = a^p b^p. Then y must contain both an a and a b, so xy²z ∉ L.", "Assume the pumping length is p and take w = b^p a^p. Then y = a^k gives xy²z ∉ L."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A valid pumping-lemma proof must pick <b>one</b> string <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ p</span> such that <i>every</i> legal split <span class=\"mm\">w = xyz</span> yields a pumped string outside <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Choose <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p b^p ∈ L</span>; <span class=\"mm\">|w| = 2p ≥ p</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> confines <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> to the leading block of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, so <span class=\"mm\">y = a^k</span> for some <span class=\"mm\">k ≥ 1</span>.</li><li>Then <span class=\"mm\">xy²z = a^{p+k} b^p</span>, which has more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s than <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s and is not in <span class=\"mm\">L</span> — contradiction for every legal split.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) fails because the lemma does not let you <i>choose</i> <span class=\"mm\">y</span>: with <span class=\"mm\">w = (ab)^p</span> a legal split may take <span class=\"mm\">y = a</span>, which pumps into a string still in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. Option (C) misreads <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span>: <span class=\"mm\">y</span> cannot cross into the <span class=\"mm\">b</span> block. Option (D) uses a string of the wrong shape (<span class=\"mm\">b^p a^p ∉ L</span>).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the complete DFA below over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0,1}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, final states <span class=\"mm\">{A, E}</span>. The minimal DFA equivalent to it has how many states?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |  0  |  1\n------+-----+-----\n  A*  |  B  |  C\n  B   |  A  |  D\n  C   |  D  |  E\n  D   |  C  |  B\n  E*  |  B  |  C</div>", "options": ["5", "4", "3", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimisation first deletes unreachable states, then merges states that are indistinguishable by every input string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All five states are reachable: <span class=\"mm\">A → B, C</span>; <span class=\"mm\">C → E</span>; <span class=\"mm\">B → D</span>.</li><li>Compare <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">E</span>. Both are final. <span class=\"mm\">δ(A,0) = B = δ(E,0)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">δ(A,1) = C = δ(E,1)</span>, so no input can separate them: <span class=\"mm\">A ≡ E</span>.</li><li>Every other pair is separated by a short string: <span class=\"mm\">B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span> are non-final but <span class=\"mm\">δ(B,0) = A</span> is final while <span class=\"mm\">δ(C,0) = D</span> is not; <span class=\"mm\">D</span> is separated from <span class=\"mm\">B</span> by <span class=\"mm\">0</span> (to <span class=\"mm\">C</span>, non-final) versus <span class=\"mm\">A</span> (final); and the final state <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is separated from every non-final state by <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>.</li><li>Merging <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">E</span> leaves <span class=\"mm\">{[A,E], B, C, D}</span> = 4 states.</li><li>As a check, the language is \"even number of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s <i>and</i> even number of <span class=\"mm\">1</span>'s\", whose product DFA has exactly 4 states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 4 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5 by assuming that two distinct final states must be distinguishable, or answering 3 by merging the two non-final states <span class=\"mm\">B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span> that differ on input <span class=\"mm\">0</span>. Merging is allowed only when <i>no</i> string separates the pair.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0,1}</span> has states <span class=\"mm\">{0,1,2,3}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">0</span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">{3}</span> and the transitions below (<span class=\"mm\">∅</span> means no move). The <b>minimal DFA</b> equivalent to this NFA has how many states?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |    0    |    1\n------+---------+--------\n  0   |  {0}    | {0, 1}\n  1   |  {2}    |  {2}\n  2   |  {3}    |  {3}\n  3   |    ∅    |    ∅</div>", "options": ["4", "5", "8", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two steps are needed: subset construction to get a DFA, then minimisation. Skipping either step gives a wrong count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The NFA accepts <span class=\"mm\">w</span> exactly when <span class=\"mm\">w</span> has a <span class=\"mm\">1</span> in the third position from the end — state <span class=\"mm\">0</span> waits, guesses with <span class=\"mm\">1</span> that the third-from-last symbol has been seen, and states <span class=\"mm\">1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">2</span> count the remaining two symbols.</li><li>Subset construction from <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span> --1--> <span class=\"mm\">{0,1}</span> --1--> <span class=\"mm\">{0,1,2}</span> --1--> <span class=\"mm\">{0,1,2,3}</span>, while <span class=\"mm\">0</span>-moves add <span class=\"mm\">2</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">3</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span> --0--> <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">{0,1}</span> --0--> <span class=\"mm\">{0,2}</span> --0--> <span class=\"mm\">{0,3}</span>. Closing the table gives exactly <span class=\"mm\">{0}, {0,1}, {0,2}, {0,3}, {0,1,2}, {0,1,3}, {0,2,3}, {0,1,2,3}</span> — 8 reachable subsets, so the <span class=\"mm\">2³</span> bound is met.</li><li>Minimisation cannot help: four of the eight subsets are accepting (those containing <span class=\"mm\">3</span>) and four are not, and the Myhill–Nerode argument for \"third symbol from the right is <span class=\"mm\">1</span>\" separates the <span class=\"mm\">2³</span> strings <span class=\"mm\">000, 001, …, 111</span> pairwise. So all 8 states survive.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 8 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 (the NFA's own state count) forgets that determinisation is mandatory. Answering 16 assumes that all <span class=\"mm\">2⁴</span> subsets are reachable, but this NFA reaches only 8. Answering 5 or 6 comes from dropping a subset state that is in fact reachable — check the table before deciding which subsets never appear.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which regular expression denotes exactly the strings over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span> that contain an <b>odd</b> number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s?</p>", "options": ["b*(ab*ab*)*ab*", "b*(ab*a)*b*", "(a+b)*a(a+b)*", "a(a+b)*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read a regular expression as a machine: the part <span class=\"mm\">ab*ab*</span> contains exactly two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, so repeating it adds an <i>even</i> number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s; a final <span class=\"mm\">a</span> outside the loop makes the total odd.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">b*</span> before the loop and <span class=\"mm\">b*</span> inside it absorb all <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s anywhere.</li><li>Each iteration of <span class=\"mm\">(ab*ab*)</span> contributes exactly two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li><li>The trailing <span class=\"mm\">ab*</span> contributes exactly one <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">= 2k + 1</span>, odd — and every odd-<span class=\"mm\">a</span> string can be parsed this way, so the expression is exact.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) <span class=\"mm\">b*(ab*a)*b*</span> generates an even number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s (including none), i.e. it is the parity-dual language. Options (C) and (D) only say \"at least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\".</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"mm\">L = {a^i b^j : i > j ≥ 0}</span>, a pumping-lemma proof takes <span class=\"mm\">w = a^{p+1} b^p</span>. With <span class=\"mm\">w = xyz</span>, <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span>, <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span>, the contradiction appears for which choice of the pump count <span class=\"mm\">i</span> in <span class=\"mm\">xy^i z</span>?</p>", "options": ["i = 0", "i = 2", "i = 3", "every i ≥ 0 gives a contradiction"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">L</span> only requires <i>more</i> <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s than <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, so pumping <i>up</i> preserves membership; only pumping <b>down</b> can break it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">w = a^{p+1} b^p ∈ L</span> because <span class=\"mm\">p+1 > p</span>; also <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ p</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> forces <span class=\"mm\">y = a^k</span> with <span class=\"mm\">1 ≤ k ≤ p</span>, so no <span class=\"mm\">b</span> is inside <span class=\"mm\">y</span>.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">i ≥ 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">xy^i z = a^{p+1+(i-1)k} b^p</span>, which still has more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s than <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s and lies in <span class=\"mm\">L</span> — no contradiction.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">i = 0</span>: <span class=\"mm\">xy⁰z = a^{p+1-k} b^p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p+1-k ≤ p</span>, so the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s no longer exceeds the number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s and <span class=\"mm\">xy⁰z ∉ L</span> — contradiction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — pump down, <span class=\"mm\">i = 0</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Pumping up by reflex (<span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span>) is the standard habit for <span class=\"mm\">{a^n b^n}</span> and fails here, because this language tolerates extra <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s. When a language is one-sided, always check whether pumping down is the move that breaks it.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about DFA minimisation are <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["A state that is not reachable from the start state can always be deleted without changing the accepted language.", "Two states p, q are equivalent if and only if for every string w, δ̂(p,w) ∈ F exactly when δ̂(q,w) ∈ F.", "Merging equivalent states never changes the language accepted by the automaton.", "A DFA in which every state is reachable and every pair of states is distinguishable can still be reduced further."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimisation = remove unreachable states, then merge equivalent states; the result is the unique minimal DFA up to renaming.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> An unreachable state is never entered on any input, so it can never affect acceptance.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> This is exactly the definition of state equivalence (<span class=\"mm\">≡</span> is the right congruence).</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> Merging equivalent states preserves the run of every string up to equivalence, hence preserves acceptance.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> Reachability plus pairwise distinguishability is precisely the definition of a minimal DFA; nothing is left to remove or merge.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a \"tidy-looking\" DFA can always be squeezed further, or that merging states always risks changing the language — merging <i>equivalent</i> states cannot.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For an NFA with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions and <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(q)</span> written for the set of states reachable from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> using only <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves, which statements are <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["q ∈ ε-closure(q) for every state q.", "ε-closure(q) is the set of states reachable from q by a path of ε-moves only, including q itself.", "In the subset construction, the start state of the DFA is ε-closure(q₀).", "ε-closure(q) can be empty when q has no ε-transitions."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure</span> is a reflexive, transitive closure: it always contains its argument and is closed under taking <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-steps again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> The empty path of length 0 reaches <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">q</span> is always included.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> That is the definition, made explicit about the empty path.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> Before reading any symbol the NFA may slide along <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves, so the DFA starts at the whole closure.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> Even with no <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions the closure is <span class=\"mm\">{q}</span>, never <span class=\"mm\">∅</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(q) = ∅</span> for a state with no <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves. The closure of a single state always contains at least that state; only a closure of the empty set is empty.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following regular-expression identities hold for all strings over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span>?</p>", "options": ["(a+b)* = (a*b*)*", "(ab)* = a*b*", "(a+b)* = (a*+b*)*", "a*b* = (a+b)*"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Identities are settled by proving both inclusions, or by producing one string that separates the two sides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> <span class=\"mm\">(a*b*)*</span> contains every string: write it as blocks of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s then blocks of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, e.g. <span class=\"mm\">ba = (ε)(b)·(a)(ε)</span> is two iterations. Conversely every generated string is over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span>.</li><li><b>(B) false.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ a*b*</span> but <span class=\"mm\">a ∉ (ab)*</span>; equally <span class=\"mm\">b ∈ a*b* ∖ (ab)*</span>.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a* ⊆ (a*+b*)*</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b* ⊆ (a*+b*)*</span>, and the star of a union of subsets of <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}*</span> stays inside <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span>; so both sides equal <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}*</span>.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> <span class=\"mm\">ba ∈ (a+b)*</span> but <span class=\"mm\">ba ∉ a*b*</span>, since <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> forces all <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s before all <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that <span class=\"mm\">(ab)*</span> and <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> are interchangeable because both \"use only <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>\". Order matters: <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> is closed under concatenation in a fixed shape, <span class=\"mm\">(ab)*</span> alternates.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Using the Myhill–Nerode theorem, which statements are <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["L is regular if and only if ≡_L has finitely many equivalence classes.", "If ≡_L has infinitely many equivalence classes, then L is not regular.", "The index of ≡_L equals the number of states of every DFA that accepts L.", "Every finite language is regular, so its ≡_L has finitely many classes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Myhill–Nerode turns a language into a <i>right-invariant</i> equivalence: <span class=\"mm\">x ≡_L y</span> iff for every <span class=\"mm\">z</span>, <span class=\"mm\">xz ∈ L ⇔ yz ∈ L</span>. The number of classes is the index.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> This is the theorem: a finite-index right congruence yields a DFA whose states are the classes, and a DFA with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> states yields at most <span class=\"mm\">n</span> classes.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> The contrapositive of the forward direction of (A).</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> The index equals the number of states of the <i>minimal</i> DFA only. A non-minimal DFA has more states and the same index.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> A finite language is accepted by a finite trie-like DFA, so it is regular, and by (A) its index is finite.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (C) as \"some DFA\" — the index is an invariant of the <i>language</i>, so it can only match the minimal machine.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span> are <b>NOT regular</b>?</p>", "options": ["{a^n b^n : n ≥ 0}", "{a^n b^m : n, m ≥ 0}", "{ww : w ∈ {a,b}*}", "{a^n : n is prime}"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language that needs an unbounded counter — equal counts, a duplicated block, or primality — is not regular; a language needing only a bounded amount of state is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) not regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p b^p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">y = a^k</span> pumps to <span class=\"mm\">a^{p+k}b^p ∉ L</span>.</li><li><b>(B) regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> describes it exactly — the two counts are independent, so no counter is needed.</li><li><b>(C) not regular.</b> Take <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p b a^p b</span>; a legal split keeps <span class=\"mm\">y</span> inside the first <span class=\"mm\">a^p</span> block, and <span class=\"mm\">xy²z</span> no longer has two identical halves.</li><li><b>(D) not regular.</b> Take <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p</span> prime; <span class=\"mm\">y = a^k</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">a^{p + (i-1)k}</span>, which for <span class=\"mm\">i = p+1</span> equals <span class=\"mm\">a^{p(1+k)}</span> — composite. (Myhill–Nerode gives the same answer: the strings <span class=\"mm\">a^n</span> with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> prime are pairwise inequivalent.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) — three non-regular languages.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating (C) as regular because it \"looks like a star\", or treating (B) as non-regular because it mentions two letters. Independence of the two counts is the deciding point.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Count the states of the minimal DFA over <span class=\"mm\">{0,1}</span> that accepts exactly the binary strings denoting a number divisible by 5. Take the usual convention: leading zeros are allowed and the empty string denotes 0, so <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> is accepted.</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Divisibility by <span class=\"mm\">k</span> is decided by the remainder of the value read so far, and reading a bit <span class=\"mm\">b</span> maps <span class=\"mm\">r ↦ (2r + b) mod 5</span>. The remainder is the state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States <span class=\"mm\">q₀ … q₄</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">q_r</span> means \"the number read so far leaves remainder <span class=\"mm\">r</span>\". Start <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>, final <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>.</li><li>Transitions: <span class=\"mm\">δ(q_r, 0) = q_{2r mod 5}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">δ(q_r, 1) = q_{(2r+1) mod 5}</span>.</li><li>All five remainders are reachable (<span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> by <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> by 1, <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> by 10, <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> by 11, <span class=\"mm\">q₄</span> by 100).</li><li>They are pairwise distinguishable: <span class=\"mm\">q_0</span> is the only final state, and from <span class=\"mm\">q_r</span>, <span class=\"mm\">q_s</span> one can always append digits to reach a multiple of 5 for one and not the other. So 5 is minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 by adding a separate \"no input yet\" start state; with the stated convention <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> denotes 0 and is accepted, so <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> doubles as the start state. Another trap is writing 4 or 10 by confusing divisor and radix.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many states does the minimal <b>complete</b> DFA have for <span class=\"mm\">L = {a^n b^m : n ≥ 1, m ≥ 2}</span> over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a,b}</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The machine must remember whether the initial <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block has started, and how many <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s have been seen (0, 1, ≥2) — plus a dead state for violations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> (start, non-final): no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> yet. On <span class=\"mm\">a</span> → <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span>; on <span class=\"mm\">b</span> → <span class=\"mm\">d</span> (violates <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 1</span>).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> (non-final): at least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span>. On <span class=\"mm\">a</span> → <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span>; on <span class=\"mm\">b</span> → <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> (non-final): exactly one <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. On <span class=\"mm\">b</span> → <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span>; on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> → <span class=\"mm\">d</span> (an <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after <span class=\"mm\">b</span> is illegal).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> (final): at least two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s. On <span class=\"mm\">b</span> → <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span>; on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> → <span class=\"mm\">d</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">d</span> (dead, non-final): self-loops on both symbols.</li><li>Five states, all reachable, pairwise distinguishable (<span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> is the only final state; <span class=\"mm\">d</span> is the only one from which nothing final is reachable; <span class=\"mm\">q₀,q₁,q₂</span> differ in how many <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s they still need) — so 5 is minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by dropping the dead state — but the DFA must be complete, and a <span class=\"mm\">b</span> before any <span class=\"mm\">a</span> has to go somewhere. Answering 6 by counting a separate start state for <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA has states <span class=\"mm\">{p, q, r}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">{r}</span> and the transitions below. Counting only the <b>reachable</b> subset states, how many states does the DFA produced by the subset construction have?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |    0    |   1\n------+---------+------\n  p   | {p, q}  | {p}\n  q   |    ∅    | {r}\n  r   |  {r}    | {r}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Subset construction: each DFA state is a set of NFA states, and the successor of a set is the union of the successors of its members.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <span class=\"mm\">{p}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{p}</span> on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{p,q}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{p}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{p,q}</span> on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{p,q}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{p} ∪ {r} = {p,r}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{p,r}</span> on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{p,q} ∪ {r} = {p,q,r}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{p} ∪ {r} = {p,r}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{p,q,r}</span> on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{p,q,r}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{p,r}</span>. No new sets appear.</li><li>Reachable subsets: <span class=\"mm\">{p}, {p,q}, {p,r}, {p,q,r}</span> — four. (The <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> state is never reached, since <span class=\"mm\">p</span> is in every subset.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 (the NFA's state count) or 8 (the full <span class=\"mm\">2³</span> bound). Only <i>reachable</i> subsets count, and here <span class=\"mm\">p</span> survives every transition, so exactly four of the eight subsets appear.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many strings of length exactly 4 over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span> belong to <span class=\"mm\">L((a+b)*ab(a+b)*)</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*ab(a+b)*</span> is the set of strings <i>containing</i> <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>. Counting the complement is faster than listing members.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>There are <span class=\"mm\">2⁴ = 16</span> strings of length 4 in total.</li><li>A string avoids <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> exactly when no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> is ever followed by a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, i.e. the string has the shape <span class=\"mm\">b*a*</span>: all <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s first, then all <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li><li>The <span class=\"mm\">b*a*</span> strings of length 4 are <span class=\"mm\">bbbb, bbba, bbaa, baaa, aaaa</span> — five of them.</li><li>So the count is <span class=\"mm\">16 − 5 = 11</span>. Check by listing: <span class=\"mm\">abab, abba, abbb, aabb, aaba, baab</span> — every length-4 string except the five listed above.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting strings that <i>end</i> in <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> (which gives 4), or forgetting that <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> may occur more than once and must not be double-counted — the complement count avoids both traps.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many of the following six languages over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span> are <b>NOT</b> regular?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">(i)   { a^n b^n : n >= 0 }\n(ii)  { w : #a(w) = #b(w) }\n(iii) { a^n b^m : n, m >= 0 }\n(iv)  { ww : w in {a,b}* }\n(v)   { w : w = w^R }   (palindromes)\n(vi)  { w : #a(w) is even }</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Regular languages cannot compare two unbounded counts, cannot remember an arbitrary block in order to repeat it, and cannot test arithmetic properties such as primality. Parity, however, is a bounded amount of state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(i) not regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a^n b^n</span> needs an unbounded counter; the pumping lemma with <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p b^p</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">a^{p+k}b^p ∉ L</span>.</li><li><b>(ii) not regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">#a = #b</span> again compares two unbounded counts; pump <span class=\"mm\">w = a^p b^p</span>.</li><li><b>(iii) regular.</b> The expression <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> describes it exactly.</li><li><b>(iv) not regular.</b> The copy language <span class=\"mm\">ww</span> needs unbounded memory of the first half.</li><li><b>(v) not regular.</b> Palindromes need unbounded memory to compare the two halves.</li><li><b>(vi) regular.</b> Two states remember the parity of the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 — the non-regular ones are (i), (ii), (iv) and (v).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Marking (iii) as non-regular because it \"looks like\" (i); the difference is that <span class=\"mm\">n</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m</span> are independent, so no comparison is required. Others mark (vi) non-regular, forgetting that parity needs only two states.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q26", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span>, how many states does the minimal <b>complete</b> DFA have that accepts exactly those strings which end in <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA reads left to right and cannot look ahead, so \"ends in <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>\" is decided by remembering only how much of the pattern has already been matched. The number of states equals the number of distinct residuals of the language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>State <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> — nothing of the pattern matched so far (also the start state, and the state reached after any symbol other than the one that advances the match).</li><li>State <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> — the last symbol read was <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</li><li>State <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> — the last two symbols read are <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>; this is the only final state.</li><li>Transitions: <span class=\"mm\">q₀ →a q₁, q₀ →b q₀, q₁ →a q₁, q₁ →b q₂, q₂ →a q₁, q₂ →b q₀</span>.</li><li>All three are reachable (<span class=\"mm\">ε, a, ab</span>) and pairwise distinguishable: <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> accepts <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> while the others reject it; <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> accepts <span class=\"mm\">b</span> while <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> rejects it. So no two states merge.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Two states cannot separate \"ends in <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\" from \"ends in <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>\"; the third residual is genuinely different.</li><li>(C) and (D) add a state for \"ends in <span class=\"mm\">b</span>\", which is the same residual as \"nothing matched\" — the table-filling method merges them.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Candidates insert an extra start state distinct from \"no partial match seen\". Those two are the same residual, and a DFA has one state per residual, not one per event.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A language that constrains the last <span class=\"mm\">k</span> symbols needs <span class=\"mm\">k + 1</span> states; the \"ends in\" pattern is the cheapest way GATE tests this.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q27", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the subset construction that converts an NFA with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions into an equivalent DFA, the start state of the DFA is:</p>", "options": ["{q₀}", "ε-closure({q₀})", "the set of all states reachable from q₀ by any string", "the set of final states"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A state of the constructed DFA is a <i>set</i> of NFA states — all the states the NFA could be in. Before a single input symbol is read, the NFA may follow any number of <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves for free, so every state reachable that way is already possible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The NFA begins in <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>.</li><li>It may take <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves without consuming input, so all of <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure({q₀})</span> is reachable at zero cost.</li><li>Hence the DFA start state must be the whole set <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure({q₀})</span>, not the singleton.</li><li>The same closure is applied to the successor: <span class=\"mm\">δ_DFA(S, a) = ε-closure( ∪_{q ∈ S} δ(q, a) )</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure({q₀})</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) ignores the free <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves and would reject strings the NFA accepts through them.</li><li>(C) is the set of states reachable by <i>arbitrary</i> strings — a global property, not a start state; using it would make the DFA accept far too much.</li><li>(D) is not a start state at all; finality is decided afterwards by \"does the set contain a final state?\".</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves as if they consumed a symbol. They consume nothing, which is exactly why they must be folded into the state <i>before</i> any input is read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two rules carry the whole construction: start with <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(q₀)</span>, and close every successor. A DFA state is final iff it contains an NFA final state.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q28", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The regular expression <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> denotes exactly:</p>", "options": ["all strings with equal numbers of a and b", "all strings in which no a occurs after a b", "all strings that begin with a and end with b", "all strings of even length"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Concatenation runs left to right and is not commutative: <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> first consumes a (possibly empty) block of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, then a block of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, and stops. Once the machine has moved into the <span class=\"mm\">b*</span> part it can never return.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take any string with no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. It has the shape <span class=\"mm\">a…a b…b</span>, possibly with one of the blocks empty.</li><li>That is precisely a block of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s followed by a block of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s.</li><li>So the expression matches it.</li><li>Conversely every string matched by <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> has this shape, so no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> follows a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — strings with no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">ab</code> has equal counts, but so does <code class=\"mm\">ba</code>, which is not in <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code>.</li><li>(C) the empty string <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> belongs to <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> but begins with nothing.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">a</code> has odd length and is accepted; length is unconstrained.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> as \"any mix of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s\". That would be <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code>. The absence of parentheses and a union matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> is the language with at most one \"boundary\" between the two letters. Read a concatenation as a sequence of phases, not as a set of allowed characters.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q29", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The pumping lemma for regular languages states that for every regular language <span class=\"mm\">L</span> there is a constant <span class=\"mm\">p ≥ 1</span> such that every <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ p</span> can be written <span class=\"mm\">w = xyz</span> satisfying:</p>", "options": ["|xy| ≤ p, |y| ≥ 1 and xyⁱz ∈ L for every i ≥ 0", "|xy| ≤ p, |y| ≥ 1 and xyz ∈ L only", "|y| ≤ p, |x| ≥ 1 and xyⁱz ∈ L for every i ≥ 0", "|xyz| ≤ p and xyⁱz ∈ L for every i ≥ 0"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA with <span class=\"mm\">p</span> states must repeat a state while reading the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols, and the loop between the two visits to that state is the pumpable block <span class=\"mm\">y</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take a DFA for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p</span> states and a word <span class=\"mm\">w</span> of length at least <span class=\"mm\">p</span>.</li><li>The first <span class=\"mm\">p + 1</span> configurations (before reading anything, then after each of the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols) must repeat a state.</li><li>The block between the repeats is <span class=\"mm\">y</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span>, and it lies entirely inside the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols, so <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span>.</li><li>Traversing that loop zero, one, two, … times leaves the DFA in the same state, so <span class=\"mm\">xyⁱz ∈ L</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">i ≥ 0</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span>, <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">xyⁱz ∈ L</span> for all <span class=\"mm\">i ≥ 0</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) states no pumping at all — it is just the hypothesis <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span>.</li><li>(C) swaps the bounds: it is <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> that must fit in the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols, not <span class=\"mm\">y</span> alone bounded by <span class=\"mm\">p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|x| ≥ 1</span>.</li><li>(D) bounds the whole word by <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, which would make the lemma useless — every long word is a counterexample.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|xyz| ≤ p</span>. The first confines the pumpable block to the prefix; the second is not part of the lemma.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the triple <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p, |y| ≥ 1, xyⁱz ∈ L ∀i ≥ 0</span>. The bound on <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> is the part that makes proofs work.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q30", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is TRUE of the minimal DFA of a regular language <span class=\"mm\">L</span>?</p>", "options": ["It is unique up to renaming of states.", "It always has strictly fewer states than every NFA that accepts L.", "Every one of its states must be a final state.", "It is never a complete DFA."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> By the Myhill–Nerode theorem the states of the minimal DFA are exactly the distinct residuals <span class=\"mm\">u⁻¹L = {v : uv ∈ L}</span> of the language. A language determines its residuals uniquely, so it determines its minimal DFA uniquely.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every DFA for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> assigns each state a residual: the set of suffixes accepted from it.</li><li>Two states with the same residual are indistinguishable and can be merged.</li><li>So every DFA for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> has at least as many states as <span class=\"mm\">L</span> has distinct residuals, and the minimal DFA has exactly that many.</li><li>Two minimal DFAs for the same language therefore have the same states up to the naming of states and the choice of the start state's name.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — unique up to renaming of states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) fails: for <span class=\"mm\">{w : the n-th symbol from the right is 1}</span> an NFA with <span class=\"mm\">n + 1</span> states suffices while every DFA needs <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> states, so the minimal DFA can be exponentially larger than an NFA.</li><li>(C) fails for any language that is not <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>; a DFA needs non-final states too.</li><li>(D) fails: the minimal DFA of a regular language is always complete — missing transitions would have to lead to an implicit rejecting sink, which is a state.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that different constructions produce genuinely different minimal machines. They can differ while being built, but the end result is the same machine up to names.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One regular language, one minimal DFA. This uniqueness is what lets GATE ask for \"the number of states\" without further qualification.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q31", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The class of languages accepted by nondeterministic finite automata with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions is:</p>", "options": ["strictly larger than the class accepted by DFAs", "exactly the same as the class accepted by DFAs", "exactly the class of context-free languages", "incomparable with the class accepted by DFAs"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nondeterminism lets a machine \"guess\", but a finite automaton has no memory to exploit the guess, so the extra freedom buys no extra power.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every DFA is trivially an NFA (a singleton transition set and no <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves), so DFA languages ⊆ NFA languages.</li><li>Conversely, given any NFA the subset construction produces a DFA that accepts exactly the same language, so NFA languages ⊆ DFA languages.</li><li>Adding <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-transitions is harmless: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-closures absorb them into the subset construction.</li><li>The two inclusions give equality; both classes are exactly the regular languages.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — exactly the same class.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the natural but wrong guess: determinisation is always possible, only potentially expensive.</li><li>(C) overshoots: <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> is context-free but no finite automaton accepts it.</li><li>(D) is impossible — the two classes are related by inclusion in one direction.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming nondeterminism adds power. For finite automata it does not; the leap in power happens only at the pushdown level, where <span class=\"mm\">NPDA ⊋ DFA</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NFA, <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-NFA, DFA and regular expressions all describe one class: the regular languages. They differ in succinctness, not in power.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q32", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which regular expression denotes exactly the strings over <span class=\"mm\">{a, b}</span> that contain an <b>even</b> number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s?</p>", "options": ["(a*ba*ba*)*a*", "(a*b)*", "(ba*ba*)*", "(a*ba*b)*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Counting modulo 2 is a regular condition: a DFA with two states, \"even so far\" and \"odd so far\", decides it. As a regular expression the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s must be consumed two at a time, with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s allowed anywhere.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pair the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s: a unit that adds two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s is <code class=\"mm\">a*ba*ba*</code>. Reading it, we take some <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, one <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, a second <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and some trailing <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li><li>Any number of such units gives <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*ba*)*</code>, which covers all even-<span class=\"mm\">b</span> strings except those with zero <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s that end in <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</li><li>A trailing <code class=\"mm\">a*</code> covers that gap: with zero units the word is a block of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, and with units the trailing <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s of the last unit are already allowed.</li><li>So the answer is <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*ba*)*a*</code>. Check: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> ✓, <code class=\"mm\">a</code> ✓, <code class=\"mm\">bb</code> ✓, <code class=\"mm\">bab</code> ✓, <code class=\"mm\">ab</code> ✗ (one <span class=\"mm\">b</span>).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*ba*)*a*</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">(a*b)*</code> accepts <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>, which has one <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">(ba*ba*)*</code> rejects <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">abb</code>, both of which have an even number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s. The missing leading and trailing <code class=\"mm\">a*</code> is the whole defect.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*b)*</code> pairs only one <span class=\"mm\">b</span> per unit, so it accepts <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*ba*)*</code> and stopping there. It matches every even-<span class=\"mm\">b</span> string that ends in <span class=\"mm\">b</span> but rejects <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">baa</code> and the empty string — the \"zero pairs\" case needs its own slack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a modulo-<span class=\"mm\">k</span> count, write a unit that adds exactly <span class=\"mm\">k</span> occurrences, star it, and add slack for the zero-unit case. Verify against <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> and a one-letter word before committing.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q33", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA has <span class=\"mm\">n</span> states, so the subset construction can produce up to <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> DFA states. Which of the following statements about this bound is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Some languages force exponentially many DFA states: for L_k = {w ∈ {0,1}* : the k-th symbol of w from the right is 1} every DFA needs 2ᵏ states, while an NFA with k + 1 states suffices.", "The bound can never be reached, because the empty set is never a state of the constructed DFA.", "The construction always produces exactly 2ⁿ states, whatever the NFA.", "A DFA obtained by the subset construction can never be minimised any further."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> DFA states are residuals: the DFA must remember exactly the information needed to decide every possible suffix. For <span class=\"mm\">L_k</span> that information is the last <span class=\"mm\">k</span> symbols, and there are <span class=\"mm\">2ᵏ</span> possibilities. An NFA can instead <i>guess</i> which symbol is the one <span class=\"mm\">k</span> places from the end and verify it with a short chain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two distinct strings <span class=\"mm\">u, v</span> of length <span class=\"mm\">k</span> differ in some position <span class=\"mm\">j</span>. Appending the right suffix of length <span class=\"mm\">j</span> exposes that position, so <span class=\"mm\">u</span> and <span class=\"mm\">v</span> are distinguishable.</li><li>Hence all <span class=\"mm\">2ᵏ</span> strings of length <span class=\"mm\">k</span> give distinct residuals, and every DFA for <span class=\"mm\">L_k</span> has at least <span class=\"mm\">2ᵏ</span> states.</li><li>An NFA does it with <span class=\"mm\">k + 1</span> states: loop on <span class=\"mm\">0,1</span> at the start state, and on a <span class=\"mm\">1</span> also branch into a chain of <span class=\"mm\">k</span> states that must be walked to the end.</li><li>So the gap between NFA and DFA size is genuinely exponential — the bound is not an artefact of the construction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) fails: the empty set does appear whenever a subset has no successor on some symbol, so the bound is not reduced by it.</li><li>(C) confuses a worst case with an identity: most NFAs give far fewer than <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> subsets.</li><li>(D) fails: determinisation guarantees a DFA, not a minimal one — this paper's Q23 is exactly such a case, where the construction yields 4 states for a 3-state NFA.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the subset construction gives the minimal DFA. It gives <i>a</i> DFA; minimisation is a separate step, and skipping it is a standard source of wrong state counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nondeterminism is exponentially more succinct, not more powerful. When a question says \"the DFA produced by the subset construction\", count reachable subsets; when it says \"the minimal DFA\", minimise afterwards.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q34", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A complete DFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0, 1}</span> has 6 states. Exactly 2 of them cannot be reached from the start state, and among the 4 reachable states exactly 2 are equivalent to each other. How many states does the minimal equivalent DFA have?</p>", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimisation is two operations in a fixed order: first delete unreachable states (they can never affect the accepted language), then merge equivalent reachable states (they are indistinguishable by any suffix).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start with 6 states.</li><li>Remove the 2 unreachable states: <span class=\"mm\">6 − 2 = 4</span> remain.</li><li>Among those 4, one pair is equivalent, so the pair collapses into a single state: <span class=\"mm\">4 − 1 = 3</span>.</li><li>The remaining 3 states are reachable and pairwise distinguishable, so no further merging is possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 3 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 4 forgets to merge the equivalent pair.</li><li>(C) 5 removes only one unreachable state, or merges before pruning.</li><li>(D) 6 assumes no reduction at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Merging first and pruning afterwards. It happens to give the same count here, but it can go wrong: an unreachable state can be equivalent to a reachable one, and merging them before pruning can hide a real distinction. Prune, then merge.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimal states = (reachable states) − (merges among them). Do the two steps in that order, and never count a state that cannot be entered.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q35", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">To show that <span class=\"mm\">L = {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span> is not regular, a proof takes the pumping length <span class=\"mm\">p</span> and the word <span class=\"mm\">w = 0ᵖ1ᵖ = xyz</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span>. Which of the following is then necessarily TRUE?</p>", "options": ["y consists only of 0's", "y consists only of 1's", "y = 0ᵖ", "y may contain both 0's and 1's"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The bound <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> says the whole prefix <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> lies within the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols of the word. In <span class=\"mm\">0ᵖ1ᵖ</span> those first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols are all <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The word is <span class=\"mm\">0ᵖ</span> followed by <span class=\"mm\">1ᵖ</span>; the first <span class=\"mm\">p</span> positions hold only <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s and position <span class=\"mm\">p + 1</span> is the first <span class=\"mm\">1</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> forces <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> to end at or before position <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, so both <span class=\"mm\">x</span> and <span class=\"mm\">y</span> consist entirely of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span> then makes <span class=\"mm\">y</span> a nonempty block of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s.</li><li>Pumping down with <span class=\"mm\">i = 0</span> removes <span class=\"mm\">y</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">0</span>^(p − |y|) <span class=\"mm\">1ᵖ</span>, which is not of the form <span class=\"mm\">0ⁿ1ⁿ</span> — the contradiction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <span class=\"mm\">y</span> is a nonempty block of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would place <span class=\"mm\">y</span> inside the <span class=\"mm\">1</span>-block, beyond position <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, which <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> forbids.</li><li>(C) would need <span class=\"mm\">x = ε</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|y| = p</span>; nothing forces <span class=\"mm\">y</span> to be the whole block.</li><li>(D) is impossible: a block spanning both letters would have to include position <span class=\"mm\">p + 1</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">|xy| > p</span>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that the bound is on <span class=\"mm\">xy</span>, not on <span class=\"mm\">y</span> alone, and then wondering whether <span class=\"mm\">y</span> could be <span class=\"mm\">1</span>'s. The bound is what pins it down, and it is the single most useful fact in these proofs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Choose <span class=\"mm\">w</span> so that <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> traps <span class=\"mm\">y</span> in one homogeneous block. Then pumping changes one letter count and not the other, and the contradiction writes itself.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q36", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The regular expression <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*a(a + b)*a(a + b)*</code> denotes exactly the strings over <span class=\"mm\">{a, b}</span> that contain:</p>", "options": ["exactly two a's", "at least two a's", "an even number of a's", "two consecutive a's"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a regular expression each concatenated factor must be matched, in order. Two explicit <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are concatenated, with <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code> allowed before, between and after them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code> consumes any prefix.</li><li>Then one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> must be read — the first witness.</li><li>Then any middle string, then a second <span class=\"mm\">a</span> — the second witness.</li><li>Then any suffix.</li><li>So a string is accepted exactly when it contains two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s in that order, i.e. at least two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s. A third or fourth <span class=\"mm\">a</span> is simply swallowed by the surrounding <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code> factors.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — at least two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">aaa</code> is accepted, so \"exactly two\" fails.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">aaa</code> has three <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and is accepted; parity is not tested.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> is accepted although the two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are separated.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the sandwiched <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code> as forcing a gap. It is allowed to be empty, so <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> is accepted — the two witnesses may be adjacent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*X(a + b)*Y(a + b)*</code> means \"contains X then Y somewhere\". Count the mandatory letters, not the star factors.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q37", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L = {w ∈ {a, b}* : #a(w) = 2·#b(w)}</span>. A pumping-lemma proof takes the pumping length <span class=\"mm\">p</span> and the word <span class=\"mm\">w = a²ᵖbᵖ = xyz</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|y| ≥ 1</span>, so that <span class=\"mm\">y = aᵏ</span> for some <span class=\"mm\">1 ≤ k ≤ p</span>. Which of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Both xy⁰z and xy²z lie outside L.", "xy⁰z lies in L but xy²z does not.", "xy²z lies in L but xy⁰z does not.", "xyⁱz lies in L for every i ≥ 0."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">L</span> fixes a ratio between two letter counts, so it is not regular: a finite automaton cannot hold an unbounded count. In the proof, <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> traps <span class=\"mm\">y</span> inside the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block, so pumping changes only the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and leaves the number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s fixed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write <span class=\"mm\">y = aᵏ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">1 ≤ k ≤ p</span>.</li><li>After pumping, <span class=\"mm\">xyⁱz = a</span>^(2p + (i − 1)k) <span class=\"mm\">bᵖ</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">#a = 2p + (i − 1)k</span> and <span class=\"mm\">#b = p</span>.</li><li>Membership needs <span class=\"mm\">#a = 2·#b</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">2p + (i − 1)k = 2p</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">(i − 1)k = 0</span>.</li><li>Since <span class=\"mm\">k ≥ 1</span>, this holds exactly when <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span>. Every other value of <span class=\"mm\">i</span> leaves the word outside <span class=\"mm\">L</span> — in particular <span class=\"mm\">i = 0</span> and <span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — both <span class=\"mm\">xy⁰z</span> and <span class=\"mm\">xy²z</span> are outside <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) claims <span class=\"mm\">xy⁰z</span> ∈ <span class=\"mm\">L</span>; with <span class=\"mm\">i = 0</span> the word is <span class=\"mm\">a</span>^(2p − k)<span class=\"mm\">bᵖ</span> and <span class=\"mm\">2p − k ≠ 2p</span> for <span class=\"mm\">k ≥ 1</span>.</li><li>(C) claims <span class=\"mm\">xy²z</span> ∈ <span class=\"mm\">L</span>; there <span class=\"mm\">#a = 2p + k > 2p = 2·#b</span>.</li><li>(D) is the pumping conclusion, which would mean <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is pumpable as stated — it is exactly what the contradiction refutes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Picking <span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span> on the instinct that \"more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s must still be fine\". Here the ratio is exact, so both directions break it; the algebra <span class=\"mm\">(i − 1)k = 0</span> is the reliable way to see that only <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span> survives.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With a counting-ratio language, write the pumped word symbolically and solve for <span class=\"mm\">i</span>. Do not guess: one value of <span class=\"mm\">i</span> often keeps the ratio, and that is the whole point.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q38", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the complete DFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0, 1}</span> with start state <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, final states <span class=\"mm\">{C, D}</span> and the transition table below. The minimal DFA equivalent to it has how many states?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |  0  |  1\n------+-----+-----\n  A   |  B  |  E\n  B   |  D  |  C\n  C*  |  F  |  A\n  D*  |  A  |  C\n  E   |  D  |  C\n  F   |  B  |  F</div>", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimality is decided by two tests: every state must be reachable from the start state, and no two states may be equivalent. States with identical rows <i>and</i> the same finality are equivalent and merge.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Reachability.</b> From <span class=\"mm\">A</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → {B, E}</span>; from <span class=\"mm\">B</span>: <span class=\"mm\">D, C</span>; from <span class=\"mm\">C</span>: <span class=\"mm\">F, A</span>; from <span class=\"mm\">D</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A, C</span>; from <span class=\"mm\">E</span>: <span class=\"mm\">D, C</span>; from <span class=\"mm\">F</span>: <span class=\"mm\">B, F</span>. All six states are reachable, so nothing is deleted.</li><li><b>First refinement.</b> Split into final <span class=\"mm\">{C, D}</span> and non-final <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, E, F}</span>.</li><li><b>Compare rows inside the non-final block.</b> <span class=\"mm\">B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">E</span> both go to <span class=\"mm\">D</span> on 0 and to <span class=\"mm\">C</span> on 1, and both are non-final — the rows are identical, so <span class=\"mm\">B ≡ E</span>. <span class=\"mm\">A</span> (→<span class=\"mm\">B, E</span>) and <span class=\"mm\">F</span> (→<span class=\"mm\">B, F</span>) differ from that and from each other, and they sit in different classes from <span class=\"mm\">{C, D}</span>, so they stay separate.</li><li><b>Compare inside the final block.</b> <span class=\"mm\">C</span> goes to <span class=\"mm\">F</span> on 0 while <span class=\"mm\">D</span> goes to <span class=\"mm\">A</span>; <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">F</span> are already separated (they reach different classes), so <span class=\"mm\">C</span> and <span class=\"mm\">D</span> are distinguishable.</li><li><b>Count.</b> Classes are <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {B, E}, {C}, {D}, {F}</span> — five states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 5 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 4 would need a second merge, but no other pair shares both row and finality.</li><li>(C) 6 is the unminimised count: it misses <span class=\"mm\">B ≡ E</span>.</li><li>(D) 7 exceeds the number of states in the given table, which is impossible for a minimal DFA.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Merging states with equal rows but different finality. Here <span class=\"mm\">B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">E</span> are both non-final, which is what licenses the merge; a final and a non-final state can never be equivalent because <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Run table filling in order: split final from non-final, then refine by destination classes until nothing changes. Identical rows in the same block are an immediate merge.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q39", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0, 1}</span> has states <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">{D}</span> and the transitions below (<span class=\"mm\">∅</span> means no move). The minimal DFA equivalent to this NFA has how many states?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |    0    |    1\n------+---------+--------\n  A   |  {A}    | {A, B}\n  B   |  {C}    |  {C}\n  C   |  {D}    |  {D}\n  D   |    ∅    |    ∅</div>", "options": ["4", "6", "8", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The NFA guesses <i>when</i> it has reached the third symbol from the end: at any <span class=\"mm\">1</span> it may start a three-step chain, and it accepts only if exactly three symbols remain. So <span class=\"mm\">L = {w ∈ {0,1}* : the third symbol of w from the right is 1}</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Trace the chain: from <span class=\"mm\">A</span> on a <span class=\"mm\">1</span> the NFA may move to <span class=\"mm\">B</span> (one symbol consumed), then <span class=\"mm\">B → C</span> (two), then <span class=\"mm\">C → D</span> (three). <span class=\"mm\">D</span> has no outgoing moves, so the run dies unless the input ends there.</li><li>Hence acceptance needs the guessed <span class=\"mm\">1</span> to be exactly the third symbol from the right.</li><li>A DFA must remember the last three symbols, since each of them can matter. There are <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> such windows, and any two different windows are distinguished by a suitable suffix of length at most 3 — so all eight residuals are distinct.</li><li>The subset construction confirms it: the reachable subsets are <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {A,B}, {A,B,C}, {A,B,C,D}, {A,B,D}, {A,C}, {A,C,D}, {A,D}</span> — exactly 8, and no two are equivalent.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 8 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 4 is the number of NFA states, not DFA states — determinisation can be exponentially larger.</li><li>(B) 6 would be the count if two of the eight windows collapsed; they do not, since each position of the window can independently be <span class=\"mm\">0</span> or <span class=\"mm\">1</span> and be exposed by a different suffix.</li><li>(D) 16 is <span class=\"mm\">2⁴</span>, the bound for a 4-state NFA; only 8 of the 16 subsets are reachable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering with the NFA's state count, or with the worst-case <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span>. The right number is the number of <i>reachable</i> subsets, then minimised — here 8, which happens to equal the worst case only because the language really needs every window.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> \"The k-th symbol from the right is 1\" always costs <span class=\"mm\">2ᵏ</span> DFA states. Recognise the pattern and the count follows without drawing the table.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q40", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements about the pumping lemma for regular languages is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every non-regular language fails the pumping lemma.", "A language that satisfies the pumping lemma is necessarily regular.", "The pumping lemma gives a necessary condition for regularity, so satisfying it does not prove that a language is regular.", "The pumping lemma applies only to infinite languages."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The lemma says: <span class=\"mm\">L</span> regular <span class=\"mm\">⟹ L</span> is pumpable. Read as an implication, it is a one-way tool. Its contrapositive — not pumpable <span class=\"mm\">⟹</span> not regular — is what makes it useful.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>\"Regular <span class=\"mm\">⟹</span> pumpable\" means pumpability is necessary for regularity.</li><li>A necessary condition cannot certify regularity: satisfying it is consistent with being non-regular.</li><li>The contrapositive certifies non-regularity, and that is the only direction the lemma gives.</li><li>There are indeed non-regular languages that are pumpable — for example <span class=\"mm\">{aⁱbʲcᵏ : i = 1 ⟹ j = k}</span> — which is exactly why the converse fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the converse-style error: some non-regular languages do satisfy the lemma, so \"every\" is false.</li><li>(B) is the converse itself, and it fails for the same reason.</li><li>(D) is false: a finite language is regular and satisfies the lemma trivially by taking <span class=\"mm\">p</span> larger than its longest word, which makes the hypothesis <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ p</span> vacuous.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the pumping lemma as a characterisation of regular languages. It is one of the standard \"necessary but not sufficient\" conditions GATE likes to test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pumping lemma: disproves regularity only. Myhill–Nerode: decides regularity in both directions. Use the right tool for the direction you need.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q41", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> have a minimal <b>complete</b> DFA with exactly 3 states? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["{w : w ends in ab}", "{w : w has an even number of a's}", "{w : w contains aa as a substring}", "{w : |w| ≡ 1 (mod 3)}"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of states of the minimal DFA equals the number of distinct residuals. Each option is checked by counting the residuals it forces.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) ends in <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>.</b> Three residuals: \"no partial match\", \"last symbol was <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\", \"last two symbols are <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>\". All reachable, all distinguishable. <b>3 states</b> ✓</li><li><b>(B) even number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</b> Only the parity of the count matters, and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s change nothing. Two residuals. <b>2 states</b> ✗</li><li><b>(C) contains <code class=\"mm\">aa</code>.</b> Three residuals: no <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> yet and last symbol not <span class=\"mm\">a</span>; no <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> yet and last symbol is <span class=\"mm\">a</span>; <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> already seen (absorbing, accepting). <b>3 states</b> ✓</li><li><b>(D) <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≡ 1 (mod 3)</span>.</b> Three residuals, indexed by <span class=\"mm\">|w| mod 3</span>, the one with residue 1 accepting. <b>3 states</b> ✓</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding a separate dead state to (C) on the grounds that a wrong symbol must be rejected. After <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> every further symbol is accepted, so the third state absorbs; and before <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> there is no rejecting sink because every symbol keeps the run alive. Counting states \"intuitively\" instead of by residual is the usual slip.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask \"what must be remembered?\" For suffix patterns it is the matched prefix length; for counts it is the residue; for absorbing conditions it is a single flag state.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q42", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-closures and the subset construction are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["ε-closure(q) always contains q itself.", "ε-closure is not transitive: ε-closure(ε-closure(S)) can be strictly larger than ε-closure(S).", "The subset construction always yields a DFA with exactly 2ⁿ states for an n-state NFA.", "Every state of the DFA produced by the subset construction is a subset of the NFA's state set."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(S)</span> is the set of states reachable from <span class=\"mm\">S</span> using <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves only. It is a closure operator: extensive, monotone and idempotent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> Zero <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves reach <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">q ∈ ε-closure(q)</span>. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li><li><b>(B)</b> Closure is idempotent: everything reachable from <span class=\"mm\">ε-closure(S)</span> by <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves was already reachable from <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, because <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves compose. So the second application adds nothing. <b>FALSE</b> ✗</li><li><b>(C)</b> <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> is a worst case. Only <i>reachable</i> subsets become states, and many subsets are unreachable — this paper's Q23 builds 4 states from a 3-state NFA. <b>FALSE</b> ✗</li><li><b>(D)</b> Each DFA state is by definition a set of NFA states, so it is a subset of the NFA state set. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing (B) — that closing a set twice can grow it. It cannot: closure is transitive by construction, which is why one application suffices at every step of the construction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Closure is idempotent, and <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> is a bound, not a count. Both facts are needed to reason about determinisation without drawing tables.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q43", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following regular-expression identities hold for all strings over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["(a*b)* = a*b*", "(ab)*a = a(ba)*", "(a + b)* = (a*b*)*", "(ab + ba)* denotes exactly the strings with equal numbers of a's and b's"], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two expressions are equal exactly when they accept the same set of strings. To refute an identity one separating string is enough; to establish one, argue over all strings.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> <code class=\"mm\">(a*b)*</code> allows several <code class=\"mm\">a*b</code> units, so <code class=\"mm\">ba</code> is in it (take the units <code class=\"mm\">b</code> then <code class=\"mm\">a</code>). But <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> has no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. <b>FALSE</b> ✗ — separated by <code class=\"mm\">ba</code>.</li><li><b>(B)</b> Both sides describe an odd-length alternation that starts and ends with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: <code class=\"mm\">(ab)*a</code> reads pairs <code class=\"mm\">ab</code> then one <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">a(ba)*</code> reads one <code class=\"mm\">a</code> then pairs <code class=\"mm\">ba</code>. The same strings, regrouped. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li><li><b>(C)</b> <code class=\"mm\">(a*b*)*</code> concatenates blocks of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, which can produce any string over <span class=\"mm\">{a,b}</span>; conversely any string splits into such blocks. So it equals <code class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</code>. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li><li><b>(D)</b> <code class=\"mm\">aabb</code> has two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, but every factor of <code class=\"mm\">(ab + ba)*</code> alternates, so no accepted string contains <code class=\"mm\">aa</code>. <b>FALSE</b> ✗</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">(a*b)*</code> as \"a's then b's, repeated\" and therefore equal to <code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code>. Repetition breaks the phase structure: the second unit may restart with <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s after <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, which is how <code class=\"mm\">ba</code> gets in.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Test identities on short strings first — <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, then all words of length 1 and 2. Most false identities die at <code class=\"mm\">ba</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ab</code> or <code class=\"mm\">aabb</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q44", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["If L is regular then some pumping length p exists for L.", "To prove that L is not regular it suffices to show that for every p there is a word w ∈ L with |w| ≥ p that cannot be pumped.", "If L satisfies the pumping lemma then L is regular.", "The pumping lemma is used to prove that a language is regular."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The pumping lemma is an implication: regular <span class=\"mm\">⟹</span> pumpable. It can be used in the direction of its contrapositive and in no other.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> This is the statement of the lemma itself. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li><li><b>(B)</b> \"For every <span class=\"mm\">p</span> some long word fails to pump\" is the negation of \"there exists <span class=\"mm\">p</span> such that every long word pumps\", which is the lemma's conclusion. Negating a necessary condition refutes regularity. <b>TRUE</b> ✓</li><li><b>(C)</b> The converse fails: pumpable non-regular languages exist. <b>FALSE</b> ✗</li><li><b>(D)</b> The lemma can only rule regularity out, never establish it. <b>FALSE</b> ✗</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the lemma as a sufficient condition. Candidates who prove \"this language pumps\" and conclude \"so it is regular\" lose the mark every time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pumping lemma: one direction only, and it points away from regularity. Note also that the quantifier order matters — <span class=\"mm\">∀p ∃w</span> is what refutes, not <span class=\"mm\">∃w ∀p</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q45", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> are NOT regular? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["{a²ⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}", "{w : #a(w) > #b(w)}", "{aⁿbᵐ : n, m ≥ 0 and n + m is odd}", "{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0} ∪ {a}"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language that requires an unbounded count, or a comparison between two unbounded counts, is not regular. Regular languages are closed under intersection with a regular set and under union with a finite set, so both operations are useful in proofs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> <span class=\"mm\">{a²ⁿbⁿ}</span> compares two unbounded counts in a fixed ratio 2 : 1. Pumping with <span class=\"mm\">|xy| ≤ p</span> traps <span class=\"mm\">y</span> in the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block, so pumping changes the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-count but not the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-count and the ratio breaks. <b>NOT regular</b> ✓</li><li><b>(B)</b> <span class=\"mm\">{w : #a(w) > #b(w)}</span> compares two unbounded counts. If it were regular, then <span class=\"mm\">L ∩ a*b* = {aⁿbᵐ : n > m}</span> would also be regular (closure under intersection), but pumping shows it is not. <b>NOT regular</b> ✓</li><li><b>(C)</b> The condition <span class=\"mm\">n + m</span> odd depends only on <span class=\"mm\">|w|</span>, so the language is <span class=\"mm\">a*b* ∩ {w : |w| is odd}</span> — the intersection of two regular sets. <b>Regular</b> ✗</li><li><b>(D)</b> Adding the single word <span class=\"mm\">a</span> to the non-regular <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> leaves it non-regular: if the union were regular, intersecting it with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> (and removing <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, a finite set) would make <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> regular. <b>NOT regular</b> ✓</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a finite union can \"repair\" a non-regular language, or that a parity condition makes a language irregular. Parity of length is about as regular as a condition gets; it is the comparison of counts that breaks regularity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two questions decide most cases: does the language force an unbounded count, and does it compare two of them? Then use closure under intersection with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> to strip away everything else.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q46", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many states does the minimal <b>complete</b> DFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> have that accepts exactly those strings which contain <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> as a substring?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> \"Contains <code class=\"mm\">aba</code>\" is decided by remembering the longest prefix of <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> that is a suffix of what has been read. That gives one state per prefix length plus one absorbing accepting state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> — no prefix of <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> matched.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> — the last symbol is <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, a prefix of length 1.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> — the last two symbols are <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>, a prefix of length 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> — <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> has been seen; absorbing and accepting.</li><li>Transitions: <span class=\"mm\">q₀ →a q₁, q₀ →b q₀</span>; <span class=\"mm\">q₁ →a q₁, q₁ →b q₂</span>; <span class=\"mm\">q₂ →a q₃, q₂ →b q₀</span>; <span class=\"mm\">q₃ →a q₃, q₃ →b q₃</span>.</li><li>The four residuals are distinct (<span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> from the rest; <code class=\"mm\">ba</code> separates <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span>; <code class=\"mm\">ba</code> against <code class=\"mm\">b</code> separates <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span>), so no merging is possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 by forgetting the accepting state, or 5 by adding a dead state for \"wrong symbol\". After <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> every input is accepted, so the accepting state absorbs; and before <code class=\"mm\">aba</code> no input kills the run, so no dead state is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A pattern of length <span class=\"mm\">k</span> needs <span class=\"mm\">k + 1</span> states when the matched state absorbs. The subtle part is the fallback transitions: <span class=\"mm\">q₂ →b q₀</span> because <code class=\"mm\">abb</code> ends in <code class=\"mm\">bb</code>, not in a prefix of <code class=\"mm\">aba</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q47", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many states does the minimal <b>complete</b> DFA over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0, 1}</span> have that accepts exactly those binary strings whose value is congruent to 2 modulo 3? Leading zeros are allowed and the empty string denotes 0.</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reading a binary string left to right, appending a bit <span class=\"mm\">b</span> maps the value <span class=\"mm\">v</span> to <span class=\"mm\">2v + b</span>. Modulo 3 only the residue of <span class=\"mm\">v</span> matters, so three residues suffice — and are necessary.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States <span class=\"mm\">r₀, r₁, r₂</span> meaning \"value so far ≡ <span class=\"mm\">i</span> (mod 3)\". Start state <span class=\"mm\">r₀</span>, because the empty string has value 0.</li><li>Transitions follow <span class=\"mm\">r → (2r + b) mod 3</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r₀ →0 r₀, r₀ →1 r₁</span>; <span class=\"mm\">r₁ →0 r₂, r₁ →1 r₀</span>; <span class=\"mm\">r₂ →0 r₁, r₂ →1 r₂</span>.</li><li>The single final state is <span class=\"mm\">r₂</span>.</li><li>Check: <code class=\"mm\">10</code> = 2 → <span class=\"mm\">r₀ →1 r₁ →0 r₂</span> ✓ accepted; <code class=\"mm\">11</code> = 3 ≡ 0 → <span class=\"mm\">r₁ →1 r₀</span> ✓ rejected.</li><li>The three residues are pairwise distinguishable — from <span class=\"mm\">r₀</span> the string <code class=\"mm\">10</code> is accepted, from <span class=\"mm\">r₁</span> the string <code class=\"mm\">0</code> is accepted, from <span class=\"mm\">r₂</span> the empty string is accepted — so all three are needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the number of bits as the state count, or forgetting that leading zeros are allowed so <code class=\"mm\">010</code> and <code class=\"mm\">10</code> must land in the same state. The state is the residue, not the length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For \"value mod <span class=\"mm\">k</span>\" languages the minimal DFA has exactly <span class=\"mm\">k</span> states, with <span class=\"mm\">r → (2r + b) mod k</span>. Moduli other than 2 need the general rule, not the parity shortcut.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q48", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An NFA has states <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">{C}</span> and the transitions below. Counting only <i>reachable</i> subset states, how many states does the DFA produced by the subset construction have?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.2px;line-height:1.62;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">      |    0    |   1\n------+---------+------\n  A   | {A, B}  | {A}\n  B   |    ∅    | {C}\n  C   |  {C}    | {C}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under the subset construction a DFA state is a set of NFA states. Start from the singleton start set, then repeatedly close each successor under the transition function; count only the sets that actually appear.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>. On 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span>: on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{A, B} ∪ ∅ = {A, B}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{A} ∪ {C} = {A, C}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, C}</span>: on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{A, B} ∪ {C} = {A, B, C}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{A} ∪ {C} = {A, C}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span>: on 0 → <span class=\"mm\">{A, B} ∪ ∅ ∪ {C} = {A, B, C}</span>; on 1 → <span class=\"mm\">{A} ∪ {C} ∪ {C} = {A, C}</span>.</li><li>No new set appears, so the reachable subsets are <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {A, B}, {A, C}, {A, B, C}</span> — four states. The final subsets are those containing <span class=\"mm\">C</span>, namely <span class=\"mm\">{A, C}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> by counting every subset. Three of the eight (<span class=\"mm\">{B}, {C}, {B, C}</span>) are unreachable, so the construction never creates them. Equally common: dropping <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span> because it \"looks redundant\" — it is reachable via <span class=\"mm\">{A, C} →0</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The subset construction is a reachability search over subsets. Enumerate successors until nothing new appears, and never assume the <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> bound is attained.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q49", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many strings of length exactly 5 over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> contain no two consecutive <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "13", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> \"No <code class=\"mm\">aa</code>\" is a regular condition decided by the last symbol only, so the strings are counted by a two-case recurrence — equivalently they are exactly the strings matched by <code class=\"mm\">(b + ab)*(a + ε)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">f(n)</span> be the number of valid strings of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span>.</li><li>A valid string of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span> either ends in <span class=\"mm\">b</span> (any valid prefix of length <span class=\"mm\">n − 1</span>) or ends in <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> (any valid prefix of length <span class=\"mm\">n − 2</span>).</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">f(n) = f(n − 1) + f(n − 2)</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">f(0) = 1</span> (<span class=\"mm\">ε</span>) and <span class=\"mm\">f(1) = 2</span> (<code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>).</li><li>Values: <span class=\"mm\">f(2) = 3, f(3) = 5, f(4) = 8, f(5) = 13</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting by subtracting \"contains <code class=\"mm\">aa</code>\" carelessly and double-counting strings with several <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> blocks. The recurrence avoids inclusion–exclusion altogether because the forbidden pattern is adjacent-only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <span class=\"mm\">f(n) = f(n − 1) + f(n − 2)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">f(0) = 1, f(1) = 2</span> gives 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, … — the Fibonacci numbers shifted by one. Memorise the pair <span class=\"mm\">(f(0), f(1))</span> so you never start the sequence one place off.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q50", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many strings of length exactly 6 over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> contain an <b>even</b> number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "32", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set of strings of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span> with an even number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s is the language of <code class=\"mm\">(a*ba*ba*)*a*</code> restricted to length <span class=\"mm\">n</span>. Counting it means choosing which positions hold the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A string of length 6 is fixed once the positions of its <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are chosen; the rest are <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s.</li><li>The number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s must be even: 0, 2, 4 or 6.</li><li>Count each case: <span class=\"mm\">C(6,0) + C(6,2) + C(6,4) + C(6,6)</span> = <span class=\"mm\">1 + 15 + 15 + 1</span>.</li><li>Total = 32, which is exactly half of <span class=\"mm\">2⁶ = 64</span> — as it must be, since flipping the first symbol pairs every even-<span class=\"mm\">a</span> string with an odd-<span class=\"mm\">a</span> one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 32.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only <span class=\"mm\">C(6,2) + C(6,4)</span> = 30 and forgetting the two extreme cases 0 and 6. Zero is even, and the all-<span class=\"mm\">b</span> string is a perfectly good member.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a parity condition on one letter, exactly half of the <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> strings qualify. Use that as a sanity check on the binomial sum, and remember that 0 is even.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar with productions <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | b</code>. How many internal (non-leaf) nodes does the derivation tree of the string <code class=\"mm\">aaab</code> have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a derivation tree each internal node is one application of a production, and its children are exactly the right-hand side of that production. A left-recursive production <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS</code> therefore adds one <span class=\"mm\">S</span> node and one terminal leaf per step, so the number of internal nodes is the number of derivation steps.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Derive the string: <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; aS &rArr; aaS &rArr; aaaS &rArr; aaab</span>.</li><li>The last step uses <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; b</span>; the three earlier steps each use <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS</span>.</li><li>Each of the four steps creates one internal node: <span class=\"mm\">S&#8320;, S&#8321;, S&#8322;, S&#8323;</span>. The children are <span class=\"mm\">a, a, a, b</span> — four leaves.</li><li>Check with the edge count: four internal nodes with child counts 2, 2, 2, 1 give <span class=\"mm\">2 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 7</span> edges, and a tree with 8 nodes has 7 edges.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and answering 3, or counting the leaves and answering 4 by a different route. The reliable rule is: one internal node per production applied, and the derivation above applies four productions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Internal nodes = derivation steps; leaves = length of the derived string (plus one leaf if a production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is used).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following is necessarily true of a complete derivation (parse) tree for a context-free grammar <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span>?</p>", "options": ["Every internal node is labelled by a terminal of T .", "The tree has one leaf for every production used in the derivation.", "Reading the leaves from left to right gives a string over T , and that string belongs to L(G) .", "Every leaf is labelled by a nonterminal of V ."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A derivation tree has the root labelled <span class=\"mm\">S</span>; an internal node labelled <span class=\"mm\">A</span> has children spelling some right-hand side of a production for <span class=\"mm\">A</span>; and in a <i>complete</i> tree every leaf is labelled by a terminal (or by <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>). The frontier read left to right is called the <i>yield</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every leaf of a complete tree is a terminal, so the yield is a string over <span class=\"mm\">T</span>.</li><li>Reading the internal nodes top-down in breadth-first order and recording the productions used gives a valid derivation <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr;* w</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">w</span> is the yield.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">w &isin; L(G)</span>, which is exactly the statement in (C).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Internal nodes carry nonterminals; terminals appear only at the leaves.</li><li>(B) A production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> creates two children, not one leaf, so the leaf count tracks the length of the yield, not the number of productions.</li><li>(D) Exactly the reverse: leaves carry terminals in a complete tree.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that leaves can be nonterminals in a finished parse. A leaf that still holds a nonterminal means the derivation is only partially expanded.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For a context-free grammar <span class=\"mm\">G</span> and a string <span class=\"mm\">w &isin; L(G)</span>, which of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The number of distinct derivations of w equals the number of distinct derivation trees for w .", "The number of distinct leftmost derivations of w equals the number of distinct derivation trees for w .", "If w has two distinct derivation trees then G is unambiguous.", "Every derivation tree for w has exactly |w| internal nodes."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A derivation tree records only <i>which</i> production was applied where, not the <i>order</i> in which independent nonterminals were expanded. Fixing the order — always expand the leftmost nonterminal — removes the freedom without losing any tree, so leftmost derivations and derivation trees are in one-to-one correspondence.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>From any derivation tree, walk the internal nodes in preorder and expand the leftmost nonterminal each time: this yields one leftmost derivation, and it is unique.</li><li>Conversely, a leftmost derivation records the exact production used at each node, so it rebuilds a unique tree.</li><li>The two maps are inverse, hence the counts in (B) agree.</li><li>All derivations of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> are many more than the trees: once a sentential form has several nonterminals, any of them may be expanded next, so (A) fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Overcounts: the same tree is reachable by many expansion orders.</li><li>(C) Two distinct trees is precisely the definition of ambiguity, so the grammar is ambiguous, not unambiguous.</li><li>(D) Internal nodes equal the number of production applications, which is generally larger than <span class=\"mm\">|w|</span>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"two derivations\" as ambiguity. Every nontrivial string has many derivations; ambiguity needs two derivations that differ in structure, i.e. two distinct trees.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be a context-free grammar with no <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-productions and no unit productions, and let <span class=\"mm\">w &isin; L(G)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| = n &ge; 1</span>. What is the largest possible number of nodes — internal nodes plus leaves — in a derivation tree for <span class=\"mm\">w</span>?</p>", "options": ["2n − 1", "n²", "3n − 1", "n + 1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two kinds of internal node can appear. A <i>branching</i> node comes from a production whose right-hand side has two or more symbols; a <i>terminal-production</i> node comes from <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> and has exactly one child. Banning <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>- and unit productions removes neither kind, so both must be counted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The yield has <span class=\"mm\">n</span> terminal leaves.</li><li>Each leaf is the only child of the production that emitted it, so there are at most <span class=\"mm\">n</span> terminal-production nodes — at most, because one production such as <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; ab</span> can emit two leaves at once.</li><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">b</span> be the number of branching nodes and <span class=\"mm\">u</span> the number of terminal-production nodes. Counting edges: <span class=\"mm\">2b + u = b + u + n &minus; 1</span> when every branching node is binary, so <span class=\"mm\">b = n &minus; 1</span>. A ternary production would only reduce <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</span> is the maximum.</li><li>Total internal nodes <span class=\"mm\">&le; (n &minus; 1) + n = 2n &minus; 1</span>, and total nodes <span class=\"mm\">&le; (2n &minus; 1) + n = 3n &minus; 1</span>.</li><li>The bound is attained: for <span class=\"mm\">n = 3</span> take <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; CD</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; c</code>. The tree has 2 branching nodes, 3 terminal-production nodes and 3 leaves — 8 nodes <span class=\"mm\">= 3(3) &minus; 1</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">3n &minus; 1</span></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <span class=\"mm\">2n &minus; 1</span> is the count of <i>internal</i> nodes in a binary-branching tree, not the count of all nodes, and it also forgets the terminal-production nodes.</li><li>(B) Quadratic growth needs unary chains, which the ban on unit productions removes.</li><li>(D) <span class=\"mm\">n + 1</span> is far too small for any tree with more than one branching node.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting <span class=\"mm\">2n &minus; 1</span> from the CNF derivation-length theorem and applying it to the total node count. There <span class=\"mm\">2n &minus; 1</span> counts the <i>steps</i> of the derivation, which are the internal nodes; the leaves are additional.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about derivation trees that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["If some string has two distinct derivation trees, the grammar is ambiguous.", "The children of any internal node, read from left to right, form the right-hand side of some production of G .", "Two different derivations of the same string can share one derivation tree.", "A grammar in which some nonterminal appears twice on a root-to-leaf path of a derivation tree is ambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The definition of ambiguity is stated in terms of derivation trees (equivalently leftmost derivations), while the tree itself is built strictly from production right-hand sides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of an ambiguous grammar. <b>True.</b></li><li>(B) is the construction rule for a derivation tree: a node labelled <span class=\"mm\">A</span> with children <span class=\"mm\">X&#8321;&hellip;X&#8342;</span> requires <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; X&#8321;&hellip;X&#8342;</span> in <span class=\"mm\">P</span>. <b>True.</b></li><li>(C) is true whenever the sentential form has two or more nonterminals: expanding them in either order gives two different derivations but the same tree. <b>True.</b></li><li>(D) is false. A repeated nonterminal on a path is just recursion, and recursion is what every grammar for an infinite language needs. For example <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | b</code> repeats <span class=\"mm\">S</span> on every path yet is unambiguous. <b>False.</b></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (D) as \"recursion causes ambiguity\". Ambiguity needs two <i>structurally different</i> trees for one string, not merely a nonterminal occurring more than once along a path.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA | a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; bB | b</code>. How many internal nodes does the derivation tree of <span class=\"mm\">a&sup3;b&sup2;</span> have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each internal node corresponds to one production application. Count the applications used inside the <span class=\"mm\">A</span> part, inside the <span class=\"mm\">B</span> part, and the root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span> contributes 1 internal node.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> must produce three <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s: <span class=\"mm\">A &rArr; aA &rArr; aaA &rArr; aaa</span>, which uses <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA</span> twice and <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> once — 3 internal nodes.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B</span> must produce two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s: <span class=\"mm\">B &rArr; bB &rArr; bb</span>, which uses <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; bB</span> once and <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span> once — 2 internal nodes.</li><li>Total: <span class=\"mm\">1 + 3 + 2 = 6</span> internal nodes, with 5 leaves.</li><li>Cross-check with edges: child counts are 2, 2, 2, 1, 2, 1, summing to 10, and a tree with 11 nodes has 10 edges. Consistent.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5, the number of terminals, by treating the tree as if it had one internal node per symbol. Here the derivation needs 6 production applications for 5 terminals, because the root production <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span> produces no terminal at all.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSc | T</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T &rarr; bTc | &epsilon;</code>. Which of the following strings is the yield of a derivation tree of <span class=\"mm\">G</span>?</p>", "options": ["a²b²c³", "ab²c²", "a³b²c⁴", "a²bc³"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read each recursive pair as a matched pair: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSc</span> wraps one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and one <span class=\"mm\">c</span> around the inside, and <span class=\"mm\">T &rarr; bTc</span> does the same with <span class=\"mm\">b</span> and <span class=\"mm\">c</span>. Counting the wrappers gives the shape of every string in the language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Using <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSc</span> exactly <span class=\"mm\">i</span> times and then <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; T</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">a&#7511; T c&#7511;</span>.</li><li>Using <span class=\"mm\">T &rarr; bTc</span> exactly <span class=\"mm\">j</span> times and then <span class=\"mm\">T &rarr; &epsilon;</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">b&#7505; c&#7505;</span>.</li><li>Substituting: the string is <span class=\"mm\">a&#7511; b&#7505; c&#7505;&#8314;&#7511;</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">a&#7511; b&#7505; c&#7511;&#8314;&#7505;</span>.</li><li>Test (D) <span class=\"mm\">a&sup2;bc&sup3;</span>: take <span class=\"mm\">i = 2, j = 1</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">a&sup2; b c&sup3;</span>. It matches.</li><li>Test the others: <span class=\"mm\">a&sup2;b&sup2;c&sup3;</span> would need <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-count <span class=\"mm\">2 + 2 = 4</span>; <span class=\"mm\">ab&sup2;c&sup2;</span> would need <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 = 3</span>; <span class=\"mm\">a&sup3;b&sup2;c&#8308;</span> would need <span class=\"mm\">3 + 2 = 5</span>. None matches.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">a&sup2;bc&sup3;</span></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) each add the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-wrappers and the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-wrappers to the wrong total, so the <span class=\"mm\">c</span> count is short or long by one.</li><li>(B) has one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> but needs three <span class=\"mm\">c</span>'s for <span class=\"mm\">i + j = 3</span>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two counts are independent. Both families of wrappers deposit <span class=\"mm\">c</span>'s in the same tail, so the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-block length is <span class=\"mm\">i + j</span>, never <span class=\"mm\">j</span> alone.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A context-free grammar <span class=\"mm\">G</span> is ambiguous if and only if:</p>", "options": ["some string in L(G) has two distinct derivation trees.", "some string in L(G) has two distinct derivations.", "some production of G has two nonterminals on its right-hand side.", "G generates an infinite language."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ambiguity is a property of the grammar, and it is defined structurally: a string with two genuinely different parse trees has two different meanings.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every string of length &ge; 2 in any nontrivial grammar has several derivations, differing only in the order of expanding independent nonterminals. That is why (B) is too weak.</li><li>Two derivations that correspond to the same tree carry the same meaning, so they do not make the grammar ambiguous.</li><li>Two distinct trees — equivalently two distinct leftmost derivations — do. That is (A).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Every string has multiple derivations in most grammars; the correct notion counts trees, or leftmost derivations.</li><li>(C) Binary productions are the hallmark of Chomsky Normal Form, which is not ambiguous by construction; <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</code> alone is harmless.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | b</code> generates an infinite language and is unambiguous.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating \"many derivations\" with \"ambiguous\". Only distinct <i>structures</i> count.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a</code>. How many distinct derivation trees does <span class=\"mm\">G</span> have for the string <span class=\"mm\">aaa</span>?</p>", "options": ["1", "3", "2", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</code> the only branching production, a tree for <span class=\"mm\">a&#8319;</span> is a full binary tree with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> leaves, and the count is the Catalan number <span class=\"mm\">C&#8342;&#8331;&#8321;</span>. For <span class=\"mm\">n = 3</span> that is <span class=\"mm\">C&#8322; = 2</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root must be <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span> (it cannot be <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; a</span>, since the string has length 3).</li><li>The split point decides the tree: the first subtree derives a prefix of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, the second the rest.</li><li>Split 1 + 2: <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; aS &rArr; aSS &rArr; aaa</span> — one tree.</li><li>Split 2 + 1: <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; SSS &rArr; aSS &rArr; aaS &rArr; aaa</span>, giving the other tree.</li><li>So there are exactly 2 trees, and <span class=\"mm\">aaa</span> has two distinct leftmost derivations — the grammar is ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 1 is the count for an unambiguous grammar; here the two splits are genuinely different trees.</li><li>(B) and (D) are not Catalan numbers for 3 leaves; <span class=\"mm\">C&#8322; = 2</span> is the only correct value.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the number of derivations (many) with the number of trees (2). Also, some candidates forget the <span class=\"mm\">SS</span> split 2 + 1 because the string is symmetric.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a</code>. How many distinct derivation trees does <span class=\"mm\">G</span> have for the string <span class=\"mm\">a&#8309;</span> (five <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s)?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "14", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A tree for <span class=\"mm\">a&#8319;</span> is a full binary tree with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> leaves in which every internal node is an <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span> application. The number of such trees is the Catalan number <span class=\"mm\">C&#8342;&#8331;&#8321; = (1/n)&#183;C(2n&minus;2, n&minus;1)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <span class=\"mm\">n = 5</span>, so the answer is <span class=\"mm\">C&#8324;</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">C&#8324; = C(8, 4)/5 = 70/5 = 14</span>.</li><li>Equivalently, with <span class=\"mm\">T(1) = 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">T(n) = &Sigma;&#8342;&#8330;&#8321; T(k)&#183;T(n&minus;k)</span>: <span class=\"mm\">T(2) = 1, T(3) = 2, T(4) = 5, T(5) = 14</span>.</li><li>The recursion is exactly the split of the root <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span> into a left part of <span class=\"mm\">k</span> and a right part of <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; k</span> leaves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 14</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">2&#8308; = 16</span> by counting splits independently at each level, or 5 by confusing <span class=\"mm\">n</span> with <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</span> and quoting <span class=\"mm\">C&#8323; = 5</span>. The recurrence is the Catalans, not powers of two.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages is inherently ambiguous?</p>", "options": ["{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 1}", "{aᵡbᵑcᵏ : i = j or j = k}", "{wwᵀ : w ∈ {a,b}*}", "{aⁿbⁿcᵐ : n, m ≥ 1}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language is <i>inherently ambiguous</i> when every grammar that generates it is ambiguous — no clever rewriting can remove the ambiguity. A language is inherently ambiguous exactly when it is a union of two languages whose intersection needs unbounded agreement between two independent counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) is <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; &cup; L&#8322;</span> with <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; = {a&#7521;b&#7521;c&#7503;}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L&#8322; = {a&#7521;b&#7503;c&#7503;}</span>. Their intersection is <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span>, which is not context-free.</li><li>Strings of the form <span class=\"mm\">a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;</span> lie in both halves, and a single derivation cannot decide which count to bind first. The standard pumping argument shows every grammar for <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; &cup; L&#8322;</span> must be ambiguous. Hence (B) is inherently ambiguous.</li><li>(A) has the unambiguous grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</code>.</li><li>(C) is deterministic, hence unambiguous: <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSa | bSb | &epsilon;</code>.</li><li>(D) is the concatenation of <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;}</span> with <span class=\"mm\">c&#8318;</span>, again unambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) are deterministic context-free languages, and every DCFL has an unambiguous grammar.</li><li>(D) is a concatenation of two unambiguous pieces with a separating symbol, so no ambiguity arises.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that every context-free language can be made unambiguous with enough care. Inherent ambiguity is a property of the language, not of a poorly written grammar, and <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span> sitting in the intersection of the two halves is what makes (B) unavoidable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["Every regular language has an unambiguous context-free grammar.", "If a language has an unambiguous grammar, then every grammar for it is unambiguous.", "If G is ambiguous, then some string in L(G) has two distinct leftmost derivations.", "Every language accepted by a deterministic pushdown automaton is inherently ambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ambiguity lives on grammars; inherent ambiguity lives on languages. Determinism is the cleanest route to unambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Take a DFA for the regular language and turn each transition <span class=\"mm\">&delta;(q, a) = q&prime;</span> into <span class=\"mm\">Q&#8339; &rarr; aQ&#8339;&prime;</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">Q&#8339; &rarr; &epsilon;</span> for final states. Every string has exactly one leftmost derivation, since the DFA run is unique. <b>True.</b></li><li>(B) is false: <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;}</span> has the unambiguous <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</code> and also the ambiguous <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | aSb | &epsilon;</code>. <b>False.</b></li><li>(C) restates the definition: two distinct trees is equivalent to two distinct leftmost derivations. <b>True.</b></li><li>(D) inverts the truth. Every DFA is a special DPDA, and DCFLs are exactly the unambiguous languages that have a deterministic parser; they are the opposite of inherently ambiguous. <b>False.</b></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking unambiguity is inherited by the language rather than chosen by the grammar. A language can be unambiguous while some of its grammars are ambiguous — which is exactly why \"inherently ambiguous\" is a separate and stronger notion.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The grammar <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; E + E | E * E | (E) | id</code> is ambiguous. Which replacement makes it unambiguous, keeps the same language, and gives <code class=\"mm\">*</code> higher precedence than <code class=\"mm\">+</code>?</p>", "options": ["E → E + T | T , T → T * F | F , F → (E) | id", "E → E + E | T , T → T * T | (E) | id", "E → E * T | T , T → T + F | F , F → (E) | id", "E → E + E | E * E | id"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Precedence is encoded by <i>layering</i>: the lowest-precedence operator sits at the top nonterminal, the next one below it, and atoms at the bottom. Left recursion at a layer gives left-associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In (A), <span class=\"mm\">E</span> generates sums of <span class=\"mm\">T</span>'s, and each <span class=\"mm\">T</span> generates products of <span class=\"mm\">F</span>'s.</li><li>So a <span class=\"mm\">+</span> can never appear inside a <span class=\"mm\">T</span>: <span class=\"mm\">id + id * id</span> can only be parsed as <span class=\"mm\">id + (id * id)</span>, which is the required precedence.</li><li>Each layer is left-recursive at exactly one production, so every string has a unique leftmost derivation: the grammar is unambiguous.</li><li>The three layers <span class=\"mm\">E, T, F</span> together generate the same language as the original, since parentheses are still available at the atom level.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) keeps <span class=\"mm\">E &rarr; E + E</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">id + id + id</span> still has two trees — still ambiguous — and it also collapses the <span class=\"mm\">*</span> layer into one self-recursive nonterminal.</li><li>(C) reverses precedence: now <span class=\"mm\">+</span> binds tighter than <span class=\"mm\">*</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">id + id * id</span> parses as <span class=\"mm\">(id + id) * id</span>.</li><li>(D) drops the parentheses production, so it no longer generates the same language.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that removing left recursion is the same as removing ambiguity. (B) shows the two are different problems: precedence needs layering, and associativity needs a single recursive production per layer.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which condition must every production of a grammar in Chomsky Normal Form satisfy?</p>", "options": ["A → BC with both B and C nonterminals, or A → a with a a terminal.", "A → aα , where a is a terminal and α a string of nonterminals.", "A → α with |α| ≤ 2 .", "A → aB , where a is a terminal and B a nonterminal."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Chomsky Normal Form allows exactly two shapes: a binary split into two nonterminals, or a single terminal. The optional exception is <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, allowed only when <span class=\"mm\">S</span> never appears on a right-hand side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A binary production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> doubles the tree height but keeps every derivation binary, which is what makes CYK run in <span class=\"mm\">O(n&sup3;)</span>.</li><li>A terminal production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> supplies the leaves.</li><li>Any other production is rewritten: long right-hand sides are split with fresh nonterminals, terminals mixed with nonterminals are isolated, <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>- and unit productions are removed.</li><li>Hence (A) is the definition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the definition of Greibach Normal Form, not CNF.</li><li>(C) would permit <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aB</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> alike; the mixed case <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aB</span> is not allowed in CNF.</li><li>(D) is precisely the mixed shape CNF forbids; it must be rewritten as <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A&#8336;B</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A&#8336; &rarr; a</span>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Allowing <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aB</span> because it \"looks binary\". CNF counts nonterminals on the right, not symbols.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following grammars is in Chomsky Normal Form?</p>", "options": ["S → aB , A → a , B → b", "S → ABC , A → a , B → b , C → c", "S → Sb , A → a , B → b", "S → AB , A → a , B → b"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Check every production against the two permitted shapes <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>. One violating production disqualifies the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aB</span> mixes a terminal with a nonterminal — not CNF.</li><li>(B) <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; ABC</span> has three symbols on the right — not CNF.</li><li>(C) <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; Sb</span> mixes a nonterminal with a terminal, and is also left-recursive — not CNF.</li><li>(D) <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span> is binary, <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span> are terminal productions. Every production fits, so (D) is in CNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Overlooking that in (A) and (C) a terminal and a nonterminal appear together. In CNF a terminal may appear on a right-hand side only as the whole right-hand side.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A grammar is in Chomsky Normal Form, and the derivation tree of a string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| = 9</span> is drawn in the usual way, with the terminals as leaves and every internal node having exactly two children. How many internal nodes does that tree have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In CNF the only internal nodes come from productions <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span>, and each such node has exactly two children. A tree in which every internal node has exactly two children is a full binary tree, so leaves <span class=\"mm\">=</span> internal nodes <span class=\"mm\">+ 1</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The yield has length 9, so the tree has 9 terminal leaves.</li><li>Full binary tree identity: <span class=\"mm\">L = I + 1</span>, hence <span class=\"mm\">I = L &minus; 1 = 9 &minus; 1 = 8</span>.</li><li>Equivalently, the derivation uses <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1 = 8</span> binary productions and <span class=\"mm\">n = 9</span> terminal productions, so <span class=\"mm\">2n &minus; 1 = 17</span> steps in total, of which 8 create internal nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 9 by assuming one internal node per leaf, or 17 by counting derivation steps and calling them internal nodes. The 9 terminal productions create leaves, not internal nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following is TRUE of Greibach Normal Form?</p>", "options": ["Every production is A → BC or A → a .", "Every production is A → aα with a a terminal and α a string of nonterminals, and every context-free language without ε has such a grammar.", "It is the class of grammars accepted by LL(1) parsers, and it permits ε -productions anywhere.", "It guarantees that the grammar is unambiguous and that every string has exactly one derivation tree."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In Greibach Normal Form every right-hand side starts with exactly one terminal, followed by any number of nonterminals. Every production therefore consumes exactly one input symbol, which makes the number of steps to derive a string of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span> exactly <span class=\"mm\">n</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition is the first half of (B).</li><li>Existence: every CFL without <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> can be converted to GNF by first reaching CNF and then repeatedly substituting so that each right-hand side begins with a terminal. So the second half of (B) is also true.</li><li>(A) describes CNF, not GNF.</li><li>(C) and (D) overreach: GNF grammars can still be ambiguous, and the class of LL(1) grammars is strictly smaller and defined by the FIRST/FOLLOW condition, not by GNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is Chomsky Normal Form.</li><li>(C) confuses a normal form with a parsing restriction; LL(1) is about disjoint FIRST sets, and GNF permits <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> only for the start symbol, not \"anywhere\".</li><li>(D) GNF constrains the shape of productions, not the number of parse trees: <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | aS | a</code> is in GNF and ambiguous.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a normal form buys determinism. Normal forms standardise production shapes for algorithms; they say nothing about ambiguity.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Apply the standard <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-elimination procedure to the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA | &epsilon;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; bB | &epsilon;</code>. How many productions does the resulting grammar have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A nonterminal is <i>nullable</i> if it derives <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>. The procedure adds, for every production, all variants obtained by deleting any nonempty subset of nullable symbols from its right-hand side; it then drops all <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-productions except possibly <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> when <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is nullable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Nullable nonterminals: <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, and then <span class=\"mm\">S</span> because <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span> and both are nullable.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA</span> gives the extra variant <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>: 2 productions.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; bB</span> gives the extra variant <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span>: 2 productions.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span> with both nullable gives <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span>, <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A</span>, <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; B</span>; and since <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is nullable, <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is retained: 4 productions.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">2 + 2 + 4 = 8</span> productions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> along with the others. Because <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is the start symbol and is nullable, that production must stay — otherwise the empty string leaves the language. Also, deleting both nullable symbols at once is only one of the four variants; <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; B</span> are needed too.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["Every context-free grammar whose language does not contain ε has an equivalent grammar in Chomsky Normal Form.", "In a CNF grammar, every derivation of a string of length n ≥ 1 from S uses exactly 2n − 1 productions.", "In a GNF grammar the right-hand side of every production begins with a terminal, so the next input symbol restricts the applicable productions to those starting with that terminal.", "Every grammar in Chomsky Normal Form is unambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Normal forms are about production <i>shape</i>; they are used to make algorithms uniform, and they deliberately do not constrain ambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the standard CNF conversion theorem: remove <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>- and unit productions, isolate terminals, then split long right-hand sides with fresh nonterminals. <b>True.</b></li><li>(B) In CNF every internal node is binary and every leaf is one terminal production. A tree for <span class=\"mm\">n</span> terminals has <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</span> internal nodes and <span class=\"mm\">n</span> leaves, and each node is one production: <span class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 1) + n = 2n &minus; 1</span>. <b>True.</b></li><li>(C) is the defining structural property of GNF and the reason GNF grammars admit a left-to-right predictive parse. <b>True.</b></li><li>(D) is false, and a one-line counterexample settles it: <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB | BA</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; a</code> is in CNF, and <span class=\"mm\">aa</span> has two distinct derivation trees. Normalising production shapes does not remove ambiguity. <b>False.</b></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that because CNF is used for parsing algorithms it must produce a unique parse. CYK on a CNF grammar counts or enumerates all parses precisely because several may exist.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The class of context-free languages is closed under:</p>", "options": ["union, concatenation and Kleene star.", "union, intersection and complement.", "intersection and complement.", "complement and Kleene star."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The standard closure trio for CFLs is union, concatenation and Kleene star — the three operations that build regular expressions, and the three that can be implemented by gluing grammars together.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Union: take disjoint copies of the two grammars and add <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S&#8321; | S&#8322;</span>.</li><li>Concatenation: add <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S&#8321;S&#8322;</span>.</li><li>Kleene star: add <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | &epsilon;</span>.</li><li>Intersection and complement are <i>not</i> preserved: <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#7504;}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a&#7504;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span> are both context-free, yet their intersection <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span> is not. So every option containing intersection or complement fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) all include intersection or complement, both of which break closure.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the CFL class is a Boolean algebra like the regular languages. It is not: it is closed under union but not intersection, and not under complement.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be context-free and <span class=\"mm\">R</span> regular. Which of the following is always context-free?</p>", "options": ["L̄ , the complement of L", "L ∩ L′ , where L′ is an arbitrary context-free language", "L ∩ R", "L · L̄"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A CFL intersected with a <i>regular</i> language stays context-free. The proof takes a PDA for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and a DFA for <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and runs them in parallel, keeping the DFA state in the PDA control state. Only one stack is needed, so the product is still a PDA.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States of the product: pairs <span class=\"mm\">(p, q)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p</span> a PDA state and <span class=\"mm\">q</span> a DFA state.</li><li>On input <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, the PDA moves by its own rule and the DFA moves by <span class=\"mm\">&delta;</span>; the stack is untouched by the DFA part.</li><li>Accept when the PDA accepts and the DFA state is final. Hence <span class=\"mm\">L &cap; R</span> is context-free.</li><li>The same construction fails for a second CFL, because two stacks cannot be simulated by one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) CFLs are not closed under complement.</li><li>(B) Two arbitrary CFLs can intersect to a non-CFL, e.g. <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#7504;} &cap; {a&#7504;b&#8319;c&#8319;} = {a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span>.</li><li>(D) involves a complement, so it is not guaranteed to be context-free.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that \"regular is small, so intersecting with it must be fine for the other operand too\" — the asymmetry is real: one side must be regular.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every operation under which the class of context-free languages is closed.</p>", "options": ["union", "intersection with a regular language", "homomorphism", "complement"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Closure holds when the operation can be simulated without a second stack. Union and homomorphism act on productions; intersection with a regular language acts on control states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Union: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S&#8321; | S&#8322;</span> with disjoint nonterminal sets. <b>Closed.</b></li><li>Intersection with a regular language: product of a PDA and a DFA, one stack only. <b>Closed.</b></li><li>Homomorphism: replace each terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span> in every production by the grammar of <span class=\"mm\">h(a)</span>; the result is again context-free. <b>Closed.</b></li><li>Complement: not closed. <span class=\"mm\">L = {a&#7521;b&#7505;c&#7503; : i &ne; j or j &ne; k}</span> is context-free but its complement <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span> is not. <b>Not closed.</b></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming closure under complement because CFLs are closed under union. If CFLs were closed under both union and complement they would be closed under intersection, which they are not — a one-line contradiction worth remembering.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L&#8322;</span> be context-free languages and <span class=\"mm\">R</span> a regular language. Select every statement that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["L₁ − R is context-free.", "L₁ ∩ L₂ is context-free.", "L₁* is context-free.", "L₁ ∪ L₂ is context-free."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Use the known closure properties and reduce each statement to one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; &minus; R = L&#8321; &cap; R&#772;</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">R&#772;</span> is regular, so this is a CFL intersected with a regular language. <b>True.</b></li><li>(B) The intersection of two CFLs need not be context-free: <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#7504;} &cap; {a&#7504;b&#8319;c&#8319;} = {a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span>. <b>False.</b></li><li>(C) Kleene star is a basic closure property: add <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | &epsilon;</span>. <b>True.</b></li><li>(D) Union is a basic closure property. <b>True.</b></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating set difference as \"removing something, so it must be safe\" without checking. It is safe here only because the removed set is regular; <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; &minus; L&#8322;</span> for two CFLs is not guaranteed context-free.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321; = {a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#7504; : n, m &ge; 0}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L&#8322; = {a&#7504;b&#8319;c&#8319; : m, n &ge; 0}</span>. Which of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Both are context-free and L₁ ∩ L₂ is context-free.", "Both are context-free, but L₁ ∩ L₂ = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0} is not context-free.", "L₁ is not context-free, though L₂ is.", "Both are context-free, but L₁ ∪ L₂ is not context-free."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each language has <i>one</i> matched pair and one free block, so a single stack suffices. The intersection forces <i>two</i> matched pairs at once, which needs two stacks.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">L&#8321;</span> is generated by <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aAb | &epsilon;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; cB | &epsilon;</code>. So it is context-free.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L&#8322;</span> is generated by <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA | &epsilon;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; bCc | &epsilon;</code>. Also context-free.</li><li>In the intersection, a string must satisfy <span class=\"mm\">#a = #b</span> (from <span class=\"mm\">L&#8321;</span>) and <span class=\"mm\">#b = #c</span> (from <span class=\"mm\">L&#8322;</span>), so it is exactly <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span>.</li><li>By the pumping lemma for CFLs, <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span> is not context-free: pumping any <span class=\"mm\">v, x</span> pair disturbs at least one of the three equal counts.</li><li>Union, on the other hand, is always context-free — just add <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S&#8321; | S&#8322;</span> — so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) contradicts the non-context-freeness of <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;}</span>.</li><li>(C) misreads the grammars: both have a single matched pair.</li><li>(D) ignores closure under union, which holds unconditionally for CFLs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a language with three symbols in a fixed order must be non-context-free. Ordering is irrelevant; the question is how many independent counts must be held at the same time.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages is NOT context-free?</p>", "options": ["{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}", "{aⁿbᵐ : n ≠ m}", "{w ∈ {a,b}* : #a(w) = #b(w)}", "{ww : w ∈ {a,b}*}"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A CFL can compare one pair of counts using its stack. Copying an <i>arbitrary</i> string requires remembering it in full, which a single stack cannot do while the second copy is read in the same left-to-right order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the model CFL: <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | &epsilon;</code>.</li><li>(B) <span class=\"mm\">{a&#8319;b&#7504; : n &lt; m} &cup; {a&#8319;b&#7504; : n &gt; m}</span>, a union of two CFLs, hence context-free.</li><li>(C) <span class=\"mm\">#a = #b</span> is generated by <code class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSbS | bSaS | &epsilon;</code> — context-free.</li><li>(D) Suppose a PDA accepts <span class=\"mm\">{ww}</span>. On <span class=\"mm\">a&#8319;b&#8319;a&#8319;b&#8319;</span> the stack must hold the first half and then match it, but the stack pops in reverse order, so the second half would have to be read backwards. The pumping lemma confirms the failure, so (D) is not context-free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (B) and (C) each compare a fixed number of counts, which one stack handles.</li><li>Note that <span class=\"mm\">{ww&#7488;}</span> <i>is</i> context-free — the reversal is exactly what the stack provides. Only the non-reversed copy fails.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing <span class=\"mm\">{ww}</span> with <span class=\"mm\">{ww&#7488;}</span>. The single superscript <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is the whole difference between a CFL and a non-CFL.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q26", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a derivation tree an internal node is labelled A, and its children, read from left to right, are labelled X&#8321;, &#8230;, X&#8342;. Which statement is necessarily true?", "options": ["A &#8594; X&#8321;&#8230;X&#8342; is a production of the grammar.", "X&#8321;&#8230;X&#8342; is a string belonging to L(G).", "k = 2 for every production used in the tree.", "A is a terminal symbol of the grammar."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A derivation tree is a picture of the productions that were applied: each internal node and its children spell out exactly one production, and nothing else is recorded in the tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An internal node is created by expanding a nonterminal, so its label A is a nonterminal.</li><li>The children are, by construction, the right-hand side of the production that was applied to A.</li><li>So the node and its ordered children read X&#8321;&#8230;X&#8342; together encode A &#8594; X&#8321;&#8230;X&#8342; &#8712; P.</li><li>The string X&#8321;&#8230;X&#8342; need not be terminal, so it need not belong to L(G); k is not fixed at 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the children as a word of the language. Only the leaves of a complete tree give a string of L(G); the children of one node give the right-hand side of one production.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Tree node + ordered children = one production. Leaves left to right = the derived string.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q27", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be the grammar S &#8594; aSb | &#949;. The derivation tree of the string a&#8308;b&#8308; is drawn completely, with the &#949;-production shown as a single child labelled &#949;. How many internal (non-leaf) nodes does that tree have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A derivation tree for a&#8319;b&#8319; under S &#8594; aSb | &#949; has one S-node per matched pair of a and b, plus one final S-node that closes the derivation with &#949;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each application of S &#8594; aSb adds one S-node and one a and one b to the frontier.</li><li>For a&#8308;b&#8308; the production S &#8594; aSb is applied four times, creating four S-nodes.</li><li>The innermost S is then expanded by S &#8594; &#949;, which is a fifth S-node; it has a single child labelled &#949;, so it is internal.</li><li>Internal nodes = 4 + 1 = 5. (Leaves are a,a,a,a,&#949;,b,b,b,b.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the four S-nodes that produced a and b and forgetting the innermost S that applies the &#949;-production. That node still has a child, so it is internal, not a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Under S &#8594; aSb | &#949;, the tree for a&#8319;b&#8319; has n+1 internal nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q28", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be the grammar S &#8594; aSb | SS | &#949;. Which of the following strings is not in L(G)?", "options": ["aabb", "abab", "abba", "aaabbb"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> S &#8594; aSb | SS | &#949; generates exactly the balanced strings over {a, b}, in which a and b behave like an opening and a closing bracket. A string is in L(G) only if every prefix has at least as many a's as b's and the totals are equal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read a as +1 and b as &#8722;1 and track the running total.</li><li>aabb: 1, 2, 1, 0 &#8212; never negative, ends at 0, so it is balanced.</li><li>abab: 1, 0, 1, 0 &#8212; balanced.</li><li>aaabbb: 1, 2, 3, 2, 1, 0 &#8212; balanced.</li><li>abba: 1, 0, &#8722;1 &#8212; the running total goes negative at the third symbol, so no balanced decomposition exists and abba &#8713; L(G).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only that the counts of a and b are equal. abba has two of each, yet it cannot be built from S &#8594; aSb | SS | &#949;, because the second S of SS must itself generate a balanced block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For S &#8594; aSb | SS | &#949;, membership is exactly the Dyck (balanced-prefix) condition, not just equal counts.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q29", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about derivation trees and sentential forms that is TRUE.", "options": ["The yield of a complete derivation tree is always a string of terminals.", "Every internal node of a derivation tree is labelled by a nonterminal.", "A sentential form may contain both terminals and nonterminals.", "The yield of a subtree rooted at an arbitrary nonterminal A is always a string of L(G)."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A complete derivation tree has only terminals at its leaves, while an intermediate sentential form is the frontier of a partially grown tree and can mix terminals with nonterminals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A: true &#8212; a tree is complete exactly when every leaf carries a terminal, and the leaves read left to right give the derived word.</li><li>B: true &#8212; leaves are the only nodes that carry terminals; every internal node is a nonterminal being expanded.</li><li>C: true &#8212; after S &#8594; AB and A &#8594; aA, the frontier is aAB, which contains a terminal and two nonterminals.</li><li>D: false &#8212; A &#8658;* &#945; only says &#945; is derivable from A. It belongs to L(G) only when A = S, the start symbol.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A, B and C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating every nonterminal as a start symbol. L(G) is defined by derivations from S alone; a subtree rooted at A yields a string derivable from A.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Leaves of a complete tree &#8594; terminals; frontier of a partial tree &#8594; sentential form; subtree at A &#8594; a string in the language of A, not of G.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q30", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be a context-free grammar with no &#949;-productions and no unit productions, and let the derivation tree of w &#8712; L(G) contain a path on which the same nonterminal A labels two different nodes. Which conclusion is valid?", "options": ["G is ambiguous.", "|w| &#8804; |V|, where V is the set of nonterminals of G.", "The tree for w has strictly more than |V| leaves.", "w can be written as uvwxy with vx &#8800; &#949; such that uv&#7522;wx&#7522;y &#8712; L(G) for every i &#8805; 0."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two nodes labelled A on one root-to-leaf path enclose a sub-derivation that starts and ends at A. That sub-derivation can be repeated or deleted, which is exactly the pumping step in the CFL pumping lemma.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let the upper A be A<sub>1</sub> and the lower one A<sub>2</sub>, both on the same path, with A<sub>1</sub> &#8658;* A<sub>2</sub> inside the tree.</li><li>The leaves under A<sub>2</sub> are some vwx and the leaves under A<sub>1</sub> are uvwxy, so A &#8658;* uAy and A &#8658;* vwx.</li><li>Because there are no &#949;- or unit productions, vx &#8800; &#949; &#8212; the sub-derivation A &#8658;* vwx genuinely produces symbols.</li><li>Substituting the second derivation into the first i times gives A &#8658;* uv&#7522;wx&#7522;y, so every uv&#7522;wx&#7522;y lies in L(G).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding ambiguity. A repeated nonterminal on a path proves the language can be pumped; it says nothing about whether the grammar has two trees for the same string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A repeated nonterminal on one path is the tree-level statement of the pumping lemma; ambiguity is a different property entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q31", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A derivation tree for w &#8712; L(G) contains a node labelled A, and the leaves below that node, read left to right, form the string &#945;. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["A &#8658;* &#945; in G.", "&#945; &#8712; L(G).", "&#945; is always a single terminal.", "&#945; must contain the symbol A."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The subtree rooted at a node labelled A is a complete derivation of &#945; from A, which is precisely the meaning of A &#8658;* &#945;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Replace the node A and everything below it by a tree whose root is A &#8212; that is the definition of a subtree of a derivation tree.</li><li>Reading its leaves gives a string &#945; obtained from A by applying productions, so A &#8658;* &#945;.</li><li>&#945; need not be terminal (the subtree may be partial), need not be a single symbol, and never contains A itself unless a production produced A.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing &#945; &#8712; L(G). Only the whole tree from S yields a word of the language; a subtree yields a word of the language generated from A.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Subtree at A = derivation from A; only the root S gives a string of L(G).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q32", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following grammars is ambiguous?", "options": ["S &#8594; aSb | ab", "S &#8594; SS | a", "S &#8594; aA, A &#8594; b", "S &#8594; aSb | &#949;"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grammar is ambiguous when some single string has two different derivation trees. The surest way to build one is to let a nonterminal derive a concatenation of itself, because the split point can then be chosen in more than one way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>S &#8594; aSb | ab generates {a&#8319;b&#8319; : n &#8805; 1}. Each string forces exactly n applications of aSb, so the tree is unique.</li><li>S &#8594; SS | a generates a&#8318;. For aaa the split S &#8658; SS can put one a on the left and two on the right, or two on the left and one on the right &#8212; two distinct trees, so it is ambiguous.</li><li>S &#8594; aA, A &#8594; b generates the single string ab, with one tree.</li><li>S &#8594; aSb | &#949; generates {a&#8319;b&#8319; : n &#8805; 0}, again with a unique tree per string.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any grammar with a recursive production is ambiguous. S &#8594; aSb is recursive but unambiguous, because the recursion is tied to matching a with b.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recursion is not ambiguity; a nonterminal deriving itself twice in a concatenation (S &#8594; SS) is what creates the choice.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q33", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be the grammar S &#8594; S + S | a. How many distinct derivation trees does G have for the string a + a + a?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With S &#8594; S + S | a, a derivation tree is a full binary tree whose leaves are the three a's; the number of trees is the number of ways to bracket the expression, which is the Catalan number C&#8322;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every tree for a + a + a has exactly three leaves, one per a, and each internal node is one application of S &#8594; S + S.</li><li>The root splits the three a's into a left group of i a's and a right group of 3 &#8722; i a's, with i = 1 or i = 2 only (both groups must be non-empty).</li><li>i = 1 gives (a) + (a + a); i = 2 gives (a + a) + (a).</li><li>That is 2 trees, matching the Catalan number C&#8322; = 2 for three leaves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 by assuming the grammar is unambiguous. The grammar is precisely the ambiguous left/right-association grammar, and the two bracketings are genuinely different trees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For S &#8594; S + S | a the count for n a's is the Catalan number C&#8345;&#8331;&#8321;.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q34", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The grammar S &#8594; aS | Sa | a generates a&#8318;. Which is the shortest string in L(G) that has two distinct derivation trees?", "options": ["a", "aaa", "aa", "aaaa"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two recursive productions S &#8594; aS and S &#8594; Sa attach the next a to the left end or to the right end, and both describe the same string, so ambiguity appears as soon as there are two a's to attach.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For a: only S &#8594; a applies, so there is exactly one tree.</li><li>For aa: S &#8658; aS &#8658; a(a) and S &#8658; Sa &#8658; (a)a are two distinct trees for the same string.</li><li>For aaa and aaaa the count grows further (4 and 8 trees), so the shortest ambiguous string is aa.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing aaa because it 'looks' more ambiguous. The question asks for the shortest witness, and aa already has two trees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Left-attachment and right-attachment productions create ambiguity as soon as two symbols must be attached.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q35", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about ambiguity that is TRUE.", "options": ["If a language has an unambiguous grammar, then every grammar for that language is unambiguous.", "A grammar is unambiguous if and only if every string in its language has exactly one derivation tree.", "Whether a given context-free grammar is ambiguous is a decidable problem.", "There exist context-free languages for which every grammar is ambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ambiguity is a property of a grammar, not of a language. A language is called inherently ambiguous only when no grammar for it is unambiguous, and the existence of such languages is the main reason the property is worth separating.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A: false &#8212; an unambiguous grammar can always be padded with useless duplicate productions to make a new grammar for the same language that is ambiguous.</li><li>B: true &#8212; that is the definition, since a derivation tree corresponds to a leftmost derivation.</li><li>C: false &#8212; ambiguity of a context-free grammar is undecidable; there is no algorithm that decides it for every grammar.</li><li>D: true &#8212; languages such as {a&#7504;b&#7505;c&#7506; : i = j or j = k} are inherently ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B and D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating ambiguity as a property of the language. Most ambiguous grammars can be rewritten into an equivalent unambiguous one; only inherently ambiguous languages cannot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grammar-level ambiguity is undecidable; language-level inherent ambiguity exists but is a stronger, rarer condition.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q36", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be the grammar S &#8594; AB | &#949;, A &#8594; aA | &#949;, B &#8594; bB | &#949;. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["G is unambiguous, because every non-empty string has exactly one derivation tree.", "G is ambiguous, because ab has two derivation trees.", "L(G) is empty.", "G is ambiguous, because &#949; has two derivation trees: S &#8594; &#949; and S &#8658; AB &#8658; &#949;&#949;."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ambiguity is a property of the grammar, and the empty string is a string like any other. Two different trees that both yield &#949; are enough to make the grammar ambiguous, even if every non-empty string is unambiguous.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>L(G) = a*b*, since A generates a* and B generates b*.</li><li>For &#949;: one tree is the single node S expanded by S &#8594; &#949;.</li><li>A second tree is S &#8658; AB, then A &#8658; &#949; and B &#8658; &#949;, giving two children both yielding &#949;.</li><li>Two distinct trees for the same string &#949; &#8712; L(G) make G ambiguous. Every non-empty string, by contrast, has a unique tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only non-empty strings. The empty string is the usual hiding place for ambiguity in a grammar that also has an explicit S &#8594; &#949;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always test &#949; when a grammar has both an &#949;-production on S and a nullable concatenation.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q37", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be the grammar S &#8594; SS | a. How many distinct derivation trees does G have for the string aaaa?", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each tree for a&#8319; is a full binary tree with n leaves, and the number of full binary trees with n leaves is the Catalan number C&#8345;&#8331;&#8321;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every internal node is one application of S &#8594; SS and every leaf is one application of S &#8594; a, so a tree with n leaves has n &#8722; 1 internal nodes.</li><li>The count therefore depends only on the shape, i.e. the number of full binary trees with 4 leaves.</li><li>C&#8320; = 1, C&#8321; = 1, C&#8322; = 2, C&#8323; = 5.</li><li>So there are 5 derivation trees for aaaa.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by doubling the count for three a's, or 8 by counting bracketings of a chain. The count for a&#8319; grows as a Catalan number, not as a power of 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> S &#8594; SS | a: number of trees for a&#8319; = C&#8345;&#8331;&#8321;, i.e. 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42, &#8230;</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q38", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be a grammar in Greibach Normal Form and let w &#8712; L(G) with |w| = n. How many production steps does every derivation of w from S take?", "options": ["Exactly n + 1 steps", "Exactly n steps", "Exactly 2n &#8722; 1 steps", "It varies with the choice of derivation"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In Greibach Normal Form every production A &#8594; a&#945; consumes one input symbol and appends only nonterminals, so the derivation has exactly one step per symbol of the derived word.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every GNF production emits exactly one terminal, namely the first symbol of its right-hand side.</li><li>A derivation of w must emit the n symbols of w, so it applies exactly n productions.</li><li>No step can emit two terminals, and no step can emit none, so the count cannot vary between derivations.</li><li>This is the reason GNF gives a simple membership test: simulate all derivations of length n.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2n &#8722; 1, which is the node count for Chomsky Normal Form. CNF trees have binary internal nodes; GNF derivations are counted in steps, one per terminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> GNF: n steps for |w| = n. CNF: 2n &#8722; 1 nodes. Two different normal forms, two different counts.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q39", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the standard construction that converts a grammar to Greibach Normal Form, the nonterminals are ordered A&#8321;, &#8230;, A&#8345; and every production A&#7522; &#8594; A&#11388;&#947; with j &#8804; i is eliminated. What does the condition j &gt; i guarantee?", "options": ["That every right-hand side begins with a terminal.", "That the resulting grammar is unambiguous.", "That the substitution process terminates, because each substitution replaces a nonterminal by one that comes later in the order.", "That the language of the grammar is unchanged."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> GNF conversion works by repeatedly substituting a leading nonterminal. Ordering the nonterminals and insisting that leading nonterminals only move forward in that order turns the process into a well-founded one, so it cannot loop forever.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The conversion replaces A&#7522; &#8594; A&#11388;&#947; by substituting the productions of A&#11388; for the leading A&#11388;.</li><li>If only j &gt; i substitutions were allowed, every substitution strictly increases the index of the leading nonterminal, so the index is bounded by n and the process must stop.</li><li>After the leading nonterminals are gone, left recursion has been removed and every right-hand side begins with a terminal &#8212; that final shape is the GNF guarantee itself, not what the ordering condition provides.</li><li>The condition says nothing about ambiguity, and language preservation is guaranteed by the correctness of each individual rewriting step.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the ordering condition with the definition of GNF. The definition is about right-hand sides starting with a terminal; the ordering is the bookkeeping that makes the algorithm terminate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ordering by index + 'only move forward' = a termination measure for the GNF conversion.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q40", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G have the productions S &#8594; A | B | c, A &#8594; aA | a, B &#8594; bB | b. The two unit productions S &#8594; A and S &#8594; B are eliminated in the standard way and nothing else is changed. How many productions does the resulting grammar have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Eliminating a unit production A &#8594; B means copying B's non-unit right-hand sides onto A and deleting the unit production. No new nonterminal is needed, but the number of productions grows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The unit productions are S &#8594; A and S &#8594; B; S &#8594; c, A &#8594; aA, A &#8594; a, B &#8594; bB, B &#8594; b are already non-unit.</li><li>Deleting S &#8594; A adds A's non-unit right-hand sides to S: S &#8594; aA and S &#8594; a.</li><li>Deleting S &#8594; B adds B's non-unit right-hand sides to S: S &#8594; bB and S &#8594; b.</li><li>S now has aA, a, bB, b, c (5 productions); A keeps 2 and B keeps 2, giving 5 + 2 + 2 = 9 productions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 9.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 7 by deleting the two unit productions without copying the right-hand sides, which would shrink the language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Unit elimination deletes one production but copies a whole family of others &#8212; the total usually goes up.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q41", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about simplifying context-free grammars that is TRUE.", "options": ["Removing a useless symbol can change the language of the grammar.", "A nonterminal is useless if it is unreachable from S, or if no string of terminals can be derived from it.", "Eliminating unit productions can require adding new productions.", "A unit production is one whose right-hand side is a single nonterminal."], "answer_display": "Options (B, C and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The standard simplification pipeline removes &#949;-productions, unit productions and useless symbols, and each step is designed to leave the generated language exactly as it was.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A: false &#8212; a useless symbol contributes nothing to any derivation from S, so deleting it cannot remove any string of L(G).</li><li>B: true &#8212; that is the definition: a symbol must be both reachable from S and productive (able to yield terminals).</li><li>C: true &#8212; deleting A &#8594; B requires copying B's other right-hand sides onto A, which adds productions.</li><li>D: true &#8212; a production of the form A &#8594; B with B a single nonterminal is called a unit production.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B, C and D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that simplification shrinks the grammar. Removing useless symbols shrinks it, but &#949;-elimination and unit-elimination typically add productions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every simplification step preserves L(G); the size of the grammar may go up or down.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q42", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let G be a grammar in Chomsky Normal Form with k nonterminals and no &#949;-productions, and let w &#8712; L(G) with |w| &#8805; 2k. The pumping lemma for context-free languages then gives a decomposition w = uvwxy with vx &#8800; &#949;. Which is the sharpest bound on |vwx| that this CNF-based proof yields?", "options": ["|vwx| &#8804; 2<sup>k</sup>", "|vwx| &#8804; k", "|vwx| &#8804; 2k", "|vwx| &#8804; &#8968;log&#8322;|w|&#8969;"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a CNF tree a path of length k+1 must repeat a nonterminal, because there are only k nonterminals. The subtree below the repeated pair has height at most k+1, and a binary tree of that height has at most 2<sup>k</sup> leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A CNF derivation tree is binary, so a subtree of height h has at most 2<sup>h</sup> leaves.</li><li>Along a path of k + 1 internal nodes only k nonterminals exist, so some nonterminal repeats; take the highest such pair on a longest path.</li><li>The upper node of that pair has height at most k + 1, hence at most 2<sup>k</sup> leaves.</li><li>Those leaves are exactly vwx, so |vwx| &#8804; 2<sup>k</sup>. The bounds k and 2k are too small to follow from the argument.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Replacing 2<sup>k</sup> by k because there are k nonterminals. The number of nonterminals bounds the height of the repeated segment; the number of leaves below it is exponential in that height.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CNF pumping: |w| &#8805; 2<sup>k</sup> forces a repeat, and the repeated block satisfies |vwx| &#8804; 2<sup>k</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q43", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A grammar G is in Chomsky Normal Form, with the single relaxation that S &#8594; &#949; is allowed when &#949; &#8712; L(G). Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["G generates &#949; because every CNF grammar is nullable.", "G generates &#949; only if S &#8594; &#949; is one of its productions.", "&#949; can be generated by repeated application of productions of the form A &#8594; BC.", "A CNF grammar can never generate &#949;."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every CNF production either emits a terminal (A &#8594; a) or splits a nonterminal into two (A &#8594; BC). Neither can shorten the frontier to nothing, so the empty string needs its own explicit production on the start symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A &#8594; a increases the number of terminals by one; A &#8594; BC replaces one nonterminal by two.</li><li>Starting from S, no sequence of such steps can ever remove all symbols, so the frontier never becomes empty.</li><li>Hence &#949; &#8712; L(G) only if S &#8594; &#949; is present, which is why the definition of CNF makes this one exception.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that CNF cannot handle &#949; at all, and therefore claiming CNF is not a normal form for all context-free languages. The single allowed exception S &#8594; &#949; covers it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CNF + the optional S &#8594; &#949; is a normal form for every context-free language.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q44", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every grammar that is in Greibach Normal Form. (Every production must have the form A &#8594; a&#945;, where a is a terminal and &#945; is a possibly empty string of nonterminals.)", "options": ["S &#8594; aSB | b", "S &#8594; aS | Sb", "S &#8594; a | aAB, A &#8594; aA | b, B &#8594; b", "S &#8594; AB | a, A &#8594; a"], "answer_display": "Options (A and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> GNF is a purely syntactic condition on each production: exactly one terminal, in first position, followed by any number of nonterminals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A: S &#8594; aSB has terminal a first and then the nonterminals S, B; S &#8594; b has terminal b and nothing after it. In GNF.</li><li>B: S &#8594; aS is fine, but S &#8594; Sb ends in a terminal with a nonterminal first. Not in GNF.</li><li>C: S &#8594; a and S &#8594; aAB both start with a; A &#8594; aA and A &#8594; b start with a terminal; B &#8594; b starts with a terminal. In GNF.</li><li>D: S &#8594; AB begins with the nonterminal A, so it is not a GNF production.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A and C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging only the first production of each grammar. Every production of every nonterminal must satisfy the shape, and a single non-GNF production disqualifies the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check each production separately: terminal first, nonterminals only afterwards.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q45", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The class of context-free languages is closed under which one of the following operations?", "options": ["Reversal of strings", "Intersection with an arbitrary context-free language", "Complement", "None of these"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reversing every string of a context-free language is easy at the grammar level: reverse the right-hand side of every production and the same grammar generates the reversed language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Given G, build G&#7488; by replacing every production A &#8594; &#945; with A &#8594; &#945;&#7488; (the reverse of &#945;).</li><li>Every derivation in G mirrors into a derivation in G&#7488; producing the reversed string, so L(G&#7488;) = L(G)&#7488;.</li><li>Intersection of two context-free languages need not be context-free ({a&#8319;b&#8319;c&#8319;} is the standard counterexample), and complement likewise fails, since closure under both would force closure under intersection through De Morgan.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing intersection because the intersection of two context-free languages often happens to be context-free. It is not guaranteed, which is exactly what 'closed under' requires.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFL is closed under union, concatenation, star, reversal, homomorphism and intersection with a regular language &#8212; but not intersection or complement in general.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q46", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The CYK algorithm decides whether a string of length n belongs to a context-free language by filling a table entry t[i, j] for every pair of positions with 1 &#8804; i &#8804; j &#8804; n. For n = 10, how many table entries does the algorithm fill?", "options": [], "answer_display": "55", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The CYK table is triangular: the entries for substrings of length L number n &#8722; L + 1, and summing over all lengths gives n(n + 1)/2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entries with j &#8722; i + 1 = L are the substrings of length L, of which there are n &#8722; L + 1.</li><li>Total = &#931;<sub>L=1..n</sub> (n &#8722; L + 1) = n + (n &#8722; 1) + &#8230; + 1 = n(n + 1)/2.</li><li>For n = 10: 10 &#215; 11 / 2 = 55.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 55.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 100 by treating the table as a full n &#215; n matrix. CYK only fills the upper triangle, because t[i, j] is defined for i &#8804; j.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CYK fills n(n+1)/2 entries in O(n&#179;) total time, since each entry combines O(n) splits.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q47", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For languages L and R the right quotient is L / R = { x : xy &#8712; L for some y &#8712; R }. If L is context-free and R is regular, which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["L / R is always regular.", "L / R need not be context-free.", "L / R is context-free.", "L / R is context-free only when R is finite."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Quotienting a context-free language by a regular language is a regular-language operation on the states of an automaton for R, and it can be carried out on a pushdown automaton for L without losing context-freeness.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take a pushdown automaton P for L and a DFA M for R.</li><li>Mark a configuration of P as accepting-in-the-quotient if, from that configuration, P can consume some string y that M accepts from its current state and end in an accepting configuration of P.</li><li>Because M has finitely many states, the marking is a finite refinement of P's control states, and the result is still a pushdown automaton.</li><li>Hence L / R is context-free. It need not be regular: with L = {a&#8319;b&#8319;} and R = {b&#8319;} the quotient is {a&#8319;}, which is context-free but not regular.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the quotient with intersection. Closure under intersection with a regular language is a different theorem; the quotient result is proved by re-marking the automaton's states, not by intersecting languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFL is closed under right quotient by a regular language; the regularity of R is essential.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q48", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement that is TRUE.", "options": ["The class of context-free languages is closed under inverse homomorphism.", "The class of context-free languages is closed under substitution.", "Every context-free language is decidable.", "If both L and its complement are context-free, then L is regular."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Context-free languages sit between the regular and the decidable languages: they are closed under most 'constructive' operations, and membership in them can always be decided, but not every closure property of regular languages carries over.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A: true &#8212; given a PDA for L and a homomorphism h, feeding h(a) into the PDA symbol by symbol gives a PDA for h&#8315;&#185;(L).</li><li>B: true &#8212; substituting a context-free language for each terminal of a context-free grammar again yields a context-free grammar.</li><li>C: true &#8212; CYK decides membership in O(n&#179;), so every context-free language is decidable.</li><li>D: false &#8212; take L = {a&#8319;b&#8319;}. It is context-free, its complement is context-free as well (the class of deterministic CFLs is closed under complement), yet L is the standard example of a non-regular language. The implication fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A, B and C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Accepting D by analogy with regular languages, where L and its complement both regular trivially holds. For context-free languages the hypothesis is much weaker than regularity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFL is closed under inverse homomorphism and substitution, and is a proper subclass of the decidable languages.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q49", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following languages is context-free?", "options": ["{ a&#7504; b&#7505; c&#7506; : i = j = k }", "{ a&#8319; b&#8319; c&#8319; : n &#8805; 0 }", "{ a&#7504; b&#7505; c&#7506; : i &lt; j &lt; k }", "{ a&#7504; b&#7505; c&#7506; : i &#8800; j, k &#8805; 0 }"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language stays context-free when the constraint can be checked with one stack while reading left to right. Comparing three quantities that must all agree at once needs more than one stack and is not context-free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For {a&#7504;b&#7505;c&#7506; : i &#8800; j}: split it into i &lt; j and i &gt; j.</li><li>i &lt; j is recognised by pushing a's, then matching some b's and leaving at least one b unmatched, then accepting any number of c's &#8212; a pushdown automaton handles this.</li><li>i &gt; j is the mirror case, and the union of two context-free languages is context-free, so the first language is context-free.</li><li>The other three require simultaneous agreement of two or three independent counts, which the pumping lemma rules out for each of them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rejecting the first option because it 'looks' like the classic non-context-free {a&#7504;b&#7505;c&#7506; : i = j = k}. Inequality constraints are much weaker than equality constraints, and the union trick applies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One comparison &#8594; context-free; two or three simultaneous equalities &#8594; usually not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U2-Q50", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let L = { a&#8319;b&#8319; : n &#8805; 0 }. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["L is not context-free.", "Both L and its complement are context-free, and L is not regular.", "L is context-free but its complement is not.", "Both L and its complement are regular."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deterministic context-free languages are closed under complement, and {a&#8319;b&#8319;} is recognised by a deterministic pushdown automaton, so both it and its complement are context-free &#8212; even though the language is not regular.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>{a&#8319;b&#8319;} is accepted by a deterministic PDA: push a's, then pop one per b, and accept exactly when both the input and the stack are empty.</li><li>The class of deterministic context-free languages is closed under complement.</li><li>So the complement is also context-free, while {a&#8319;b&#8319;} is the standard example of a language that is not regular.</li><li>So the correct statement is the one saying both L and its complement are context-free while L is not regular.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that the complement cannot be context-free because context-free languages are not closed under complement in general. That non-closure is about arbitrary context-free languages; deterministic ones are closed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DCFL is closed under complement, so L and its complement can both be context-free without either being regular.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A pushdown automaton is <b>deterministic</b> exactly when which condition holds?</p>", "options": ["For every state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, stack symbol <span class=\"mm\">X</span> and input symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> has at most one element, and <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, X)</span> is non-empty only if <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> is empty for every input symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.", "The automaton has exactly one final state and a stack alphabet of exactly two symbols.", "Every move consumes exactly one input symbol.", "Its stack height never exceeds a fixed constant."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A configuration of a PDA is the triple (state, remaining input, whole stack). Determinism means that from every configuration there is at most one move available.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For a fixed state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, stack top <span class=\"mm\">X</span> and input symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, the move table <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> must contain at most one element — otherwise the machine could choose between two successors.</li><li>An ε-move is a second source of choice: if <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, X)</span> is non-empty while <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> is also non-empty, the machine may either consume <span class=\"mm\">a</span> or move without consuming input, and two moves exist from one configuration.</li><li>The two restrictions together are exactly the definition: one successor per <span class=\"mm\">(q, a, X)</span>, and ε permitted on <span class=\"mm\">(q, X)</span> only where no input-consuming move exists.</li><li>Option (B) constrains the shape of the machine, not its behaviour. Option (C) forbids ε-moves, which a DPDA is allowed to have. Option (D) bounds the stack, which has nothing to do with determinism.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a deterministic PDA may never use ε-moves. It may — the prohibition is on having an ε-move <i>and</i> an input move from the same configuration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Determinism is a statement about the number of successors of a configuration, not about the number of states, the size of the stack alphabet or the use of ε.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following languages is accepted by a <b>deterministic</b> pushdown automaton?</p>", "options": ["{ <span class=\"mm\">w w<sup>R</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">w c w<sup>R</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> c<sup>n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">w w</span> : <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> }"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DPDA reads its input strictly left to right and cannot look ahead, so it can only handle a language if the point where it must start popping the stack is marked by something it can actually see.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <span class=\"mm\">{w c w<sup>R</sup>}</span> the marker <span class=\"mm\">c</span> tells the machine exactly where the first half ends: push every symbol before <span class=\"mm\">c</span>, then pop one symbol per input symbol after it. No guess is needed, so a DPDA accepts it.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">{w w<sup>R</sup>}</span> nothing marks the midpoint. A DPDA would have to decide nondeterministically when to switch from pushing to popping, and the pumping argument for DPDAs rules the language out.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">{w w}</span> the second half is not the reverse of the first, so a single stack cannot compare them at all — this language is not even context-free.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> c<sup>n</sup>}</span> two independent counts must be matched simultaneously; one stack cannot do it, and the language is not context-free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a DPDA can 'guess' the middle of a string. Guessing is exactly what determinism forbids; the visible marker <span class=\"mm\">c</span> is what makes option (B) deterministic and option (A) not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A deterministic PDA needs a visible cue before it may change direction.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many of the following six languages are <b>deterministic</b> context-free?<br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>m</sup> : n ≠ m, n, m ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>3</sub> = { w w<sup>R</sup> : w ∈ {a, b}* }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub> = { w c w<sup>R</sup> : w ∈ {a, b}* }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>5</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> c<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>6</sub> = { a<sup>i</sup> b<sup>j</sup> c<sup>k</sup> : i = j or j = k }</span></p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language is a DCFL when some deterministic PDA accepts it. Two facts do most of the work here: the class of DCFLs is closed under complement, and it is closed under intersection with a regular language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub></span> — deterministic.</b> Push one symbol per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, pop one per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, accept when the input ends exactly as the stack empties. No choice is ever made.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span> — deterministic.</b> Inside <span class=\"mm\">a* b*</span>, <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span> is the complement of <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub></span>. DCFLs are closed under complement, and intersecting with the regular set <span class=\"mm\">a* b*</span> preserves determinism, so <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span> is a DCFL.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>3</sub></span> — not deterministic.</b> Nothing marks the midpoint of <span class=\"mm\">w w<sup>R</sup></span>, so the machine must guess when to start popping.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub></span> — deterministic.</b> The visible <span class=\"mm\">c</span> marks the midpoint.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>5</sub></span> — not even context-free</b>, so certainly not deterministic: one stack cannot match two independent counts.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>6</sub></span> — not deterministic.</b> It is a context-free language (a union of two context-free languages), but a DPDA would have to commit to 'match <span class=\"mm\">i</span> with <span class=\"mm\">j</span>' or to 'match <span class=\"mm\">j</span> with <span class=\"mm\">k</span>' before it has seen the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-block. This is the standard example of a CFL that is not a DCFL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Count.</b> Deterministic: <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub>, L<sub>2</sub>, L<sub>4</sub></span> = 3.</p><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two opposite errors. Counting <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>3</sub></span> as deterministic by confusing it with <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub></span>; and assuming that any '≠' condition destroys determinism, when <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span> shows it can survive through complement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Inequality between two counts is often still deterministic; equality of two counts is too; simultaneous equality of three counts, or an unmarked midpoint, is not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a deterministic PDA, suppose <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, X)</span> is non-empty for some state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> and stack symbol <span class=\"mm\">X</span>. Which statement must be TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The automaton must reach a final state by that ε-move.", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> may be non-empty provided it leads to the same state as the ε-move.", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> must be empty for every input symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.", "The ε-move may be taken only after the whole input has been consumed."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The determinism condition is imposed per <i>configuration</i>. Two moves are available from the configuration <span class=\"mm\">(q, w, Xγ)</span> if one is an ε-move on <span class=\"mm\">X</span> and the other consumes the next input symbol on the same <span class=\"mm\">X</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fix <span class=\"mm\">(q, X)</span> and suppose <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, X)</span> is non-empty.</li><li>If some <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> were also non-empty, then from the configuration <span class=\"mm\">(q, aw, Xγ)</span> the machine could either consume <span class=\"mm\">a</span> or move on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> — two successors.</li><li>Determinism therefore forces <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X) = ∅</span> for every input symbol <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</li><li>Where the destination is irrelevant: the restriction is on the source configuration, not on where each move leads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) looks like a harmless relaxation — 'both moves lead to the same place, so it is still deterministic'. It is not: the choice itself is the nondeterminism, and it may consume different amounts of input, so the two runs diverge later.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a given <span class=\"mm\">(q, X)</span>, an ε-move and an input move may never coexist.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about the class of deterministic context-free languages (DCFLs) that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["The class of DCFLs is closed under complement.", "The class of DCFLs is closed under union.", "Every DCFL is generated by some unambiguous context-free grammar.", "The class of DCFLs is closed under intersection with a regular language."], "answer_display": "Options (A, C and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The DCFLs form a proper subclass of the CFLs, and their closure properties are much weaker than those of the CFLs. The only Boolean operation that survives is complement; intersection and union with a regular language are also safe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> Given a DPDA, add a rejecting sink and swap the roles of final and non-final states; the result is still deterministic because every computation of a DPDA is a single path. Hence the complement of a DCFL is a DCFL.</li><li><b>(B) false.</b> Both <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 1}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>2n</sup> : n ≥ 1}</span> are DCFLs, yet their union is a CFL that is not a DCFL. A DPDA must decide, while still reading the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block, whether to match one <span class=\"mm\">b</span> per <span class=\"mm\">a</span> or two, and it has no way to know.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> The computation of a DPDA on a fixed string is unique, so a DPDA yields at most one parse per string; converting the DPDA to a grammar therefore produces an unambiguous grammar.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> Run the DPDA in parallel with the DFA of the regular language (product construction). The stack is still driven by the DPDA alone, so the product is deterministic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying over the CFL closure properties wholesale. The CFLs are closed under union, but that proof uses nondeterministic guessing between the two machines, which determinism removes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DCFL: closed under complement, intersection and union with regular languages; not closed under union or intersection in general.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following languages is <b>not</b> a deterministic context-free language?</p>", "options": ["{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>m</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≠ m, n, m ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> } ∪ { <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>2n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> c</span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">w c w<sup>R</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> }"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The union of two DCFLs need not be a DCFL. The standard witness is the union of the 'one b per a' language with the 'two b's per a' language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>2n</sup>}</span> are DCFLs: push one symbol per <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and then pop one (respectively two) per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>Their union is context-free — join the two machines with a new start state and ε-moves — so it is a CFL.</li><li>It is not a DCFL. A DPDA must commit, at the end of the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block, to a matching rule: one <span class=\"mm\">b</span> per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, or two. Both families of strings begin with the same <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup></span> prefix, and the stack alone cannot encode 'either n or 2n', so whichever rule is chosen, the other family is rejected.</li><li>By contrast, <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>m</sup> : n ≠ m}</span> is a DCFL (it is the complement of <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span> inside the regular set <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span>), and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{w c w<sup>R</sup>}</span> are deterministic because the trailing <span class=\"mm\">c</span> marks the switch point.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that because each component is deterministic, the union must be too. Union is exactly the operation that reintroduces a choice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Determinism is fragile under union; a language can be context-free, and even a union of two DCFLs, and still fail to be a DCFL.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">M</span> be the PDA with states <span class=\"mm\">{q<sub>0</sub>, q<sub>1</sub>, q<sub>2</sub>}</span>, stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>2</sub></span>, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>, and moves<br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A A)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>2</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br>Select every statement that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L(M) = { a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 1 }</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">M</span> is deterministic.", "<span class=\"mm\">M</span> accepts its language by empty stack.", "On the input <span class=\"mm\">aab</span>, the computation of <span class=\"mm\">M</span> ends in a non-final configuration."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read the move table as a program: <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> pushes one <span class=\"mm\">A</span> per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> pops one <span class=\"mm\">A</span> per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and the only ε-move hands control from <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> to the final state once the stack is down to <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> On <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span> the stack after the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block is <span class=\"mm\">A<sup>n</sup>Z<sub>0</sub></span>; the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> symbols <span class=\"mm\">b</span> pop all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> copies of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, and the ε-move then reaches <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>2</sub></span>. Trace <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>2</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, accepted.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> Every entry of the table is either empty or a singleton, and the only ε-move is on <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> is empty. So no configuration has two successors.</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> Acceptance happens in state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>2</sub></span>, and the ε-move writes <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> back, so the stack still holds <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> when the machine accepts. It accepts by final state.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> Trace <span class=\"mm\">aab</span>: after two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s the stack is <span class=\"mm\">AAZ<sub>0</sub></span> in <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>; the single <span class=\"mm\">b</span> pops one <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and moves to <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>)</span>. The input is exhausted, <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> is not final, and the ε-move does not apply because the top symbol is <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, not <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>. The run stops in a non-final configuration, so <span class=\"mm\">aab</span> is rejected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the final state as 'stack emptied'. Acceptance by final state and acceptance by empty stack are different conditions; here the bottom marker is deliberately restored, which is exactly what makes the machine deterministic and lets it stop at the right moment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To check determinism, look for any <span class=\"mm\">(q, X)</span> that has both an ε-move and an input move — not at the number of moves in the whole table.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statement about the class of languages accepted by <b>nondeterministic</b> pushdown automata is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["It is exactly the class of context-free languages.", "It is exactly the class of regular languages.", "It is exactly the class of deterministic context-free languages.", "It is strictly larger than the class of languages generated by context-free grammars."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The NPDA and the context-free grammar are two notations for the same class of languages. Each direction of the equivalence has a standard construction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>CFG → NPDA.</b> A single-state automaton with initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span> replaces the top nonterminal by the right-hand side of a production, and pops a terminal when it matches the next input symbol. It accepts by empty stack.</li><li><b>NPDA → CFG.</b> Introduce a variable <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span> for every triple of states and stack symbols; the variable generates exactly the strings that take the automaton from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> on top to <span class=\"mm\">p</span> having popped <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</li><li>Both constructions preserve the language exactly, so the two classes coincide — they are the context-free languages.</li><li>The regular languages and the deterministic CFLs are proper subclasses of this class: <span class=\"mm\">{w w<sup>R</sup>}</span> is context-free but not regular, and not a DCFL either.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) — believing that a machine with a stack must beat a grammar. They are exactly matched, so neither is stronger.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NPDA ≡ CFG. Any question phrased in one notation can be answered in the other.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the PDA <span class=\"mm\">M</span> with states <span class=\"mm\">{q<sub>0</sub>, q<sub>1</sub>}</span>, stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span>, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>, and moves<br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A A)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, ε), (q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br>On the input <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>, how many distinct sequences of moves take <span class=\"mm\">M</span> from its start configuration to a configuration that has read the whole input and is in the final state?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An NPDA is a tree of computations, not a single path. To count accepting computations, follow every branch and discard only the ones that die.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read the two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s. Only one branch survives: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>0</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>0</sub>, AAZ<sub>0</sub>)</span>. The input left is <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>.</li><li>The first <span class=\"mm\">b</span> has two choices, so the tree splits:<br><b>branch 1</b> — <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>)</span> with input <span class=\"mm\">b</span> remaining;<br><b>branch 2</b> — <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>)</span> with input <span class=\"mm\">b</span> remaining.</li><li><b>Branch 2 dies.</b> <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A)</span> is not defined, and the ε-move of <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> needs <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> on top, which it is not.</li><li><b>Branch 1 splits again</b> on the second <span class=\"mm\">b</span>: to <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> or to <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>. Both have consumed all four symbols.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> is final, so this is an accepting computation. <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> is not final, but the ε-move carries it to <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> with no input consumed — a second accepting computation.</li><li>Total: <span class=\"mm\">1 + 0 + 1 = 2</span> accepting computations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the number of accepting <i>states</i> reached rather than the number of distinct move sequences. Two different sequences may end in the same configuration, and each is counted separately.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count in an NPDA by drawing the computation tree and pruning dead branches — never by following one path and assuming the rest behave the same.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The PDA <span class=\"mm\">M</span> has states <span class=\"mm\">{q<sub>0</sub>, q<sub>1</sub>}</span>, stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>, no final states, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>, and accepts by empty stack, with moves<br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A A)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br>Which language does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> accept?</p>", "options": ["{ <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> : <span class=\"mm\">#<sub>a</sub>(w) = #<sub>b</sub>(w)</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>m</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n, m ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 1</span> }"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read the move table as a program: <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> is the phase in which <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are pushed, <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> is the phase in which <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s are popped, and acceptance by empty stack means the input is finished and nothing but the marker is left, which the ε-move then removes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each <span class=\"mm\">a</span> pushes exactly one <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, and only <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> can read an <span class=\"mm\">a</span>. So all <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s must come first.</li><li>The first <span class=\"mm\">b</span> switches to <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span> and pops one <span class=\"mm\">A</span>; every later <span class=\"mm\">b</span> also pops one. So the number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s must equal the number of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</li><li>After the last <span class=\"mm\">b</span> the stack is <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> alone, and the ε-move empties it: accepted. Trace <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, AZ<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>) → (q<sub>1</sub>, ε)</span>.</li><li>Order matters: on <span class=\"mm\">abab</span> the third symbol is an <span class=\"mm\">a</span> while the machine is in <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span>, where no <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-move exists, so the branch dies and the string is rejected.</li><li>The empty string is rejected: the ε-move is defined only on <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, and with no input read the machine is still in <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">L(M) = {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 1}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 1 }.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) ignores the order requirement: the phase states force all <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s before all <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, so equal counts are not enough. Option (C) forgets that <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> never reaches <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span>, so <span class=\"mm\">n = 0</span> is not covered.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With acceptance by empty stack, always check separately whether the empty string can empty the stack — the ε-move is keyed on a state and a stack symbol, and both must be reachable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about nondeterministic pushdown automata (NPDAs) that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["Every context-free language is accepted by some NPDA.", "An NPDA may have several moves available from the same configuration.", "For every NPDA there is a context-free grammar generating the same language.", "An NPDA cannot accept the empty string."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> NPDAs are the machine model of the context-free languages. Nondeterminism is what gives them their power, and it is also what makes the two acceptance modes interchangeable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> Take a grammar in Greibach Normal Form and build the single-state NPDA that replaces the top nonterminal by a right-hand side and pops matching terminals; it accepts by empty stack.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> That is the definition of nondeterminism: <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, X)</span> may contain two or more elements, and an ε-move may coexist with an input move.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> The triple construction <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span> produces a grammar for the language of any PDA.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> The empty string is accepted whenever the start configuration is already accepting — for instance a grammar with <span class=\"mm\">S → ε</span> gives an NPDA that accepts <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> by empty stack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that 'the stack must be emptied by reading input' rules out <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>. ε-moves can empty the stack without consuming anything.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NPDA = CFG, nondeterministic by design, and perfectly able to accept <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A grammar <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span> is converted into an NPDA by the standard top-down construction: one state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>, acceptance by empty stack. Which move is added for the production <span class=\"mm\">A → X<sub>1</sub> X<sub>2</sub> … X<sub>k</sub></span>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, X<sub>1</sub>, A)</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">(q, X<sub>2</sub> … X<sub>k</sub>)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, A, ε)</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">(q, X<sub>1</sub> … X<sub>k</sub>)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, X<sub>1</sub>)</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">(q, A)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, A)</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">(q, X<sub>1</sub> X<sub>2</sub> … X<sub>k</sub>)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The construction simulates a leftmost derivation on the stack. The stack holds the unexpanded sentential form with the leftmost symbol on top, so expanding a nonterminal means replacing the top symbol by the right-hand side, in order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Expansion consumes no input, so the move must be on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>.</li><li>The symbol being expanded is the stack top, so the move is keyed on <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, A)</span>.</li><li>The replacement is the whole right-hand side <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>1</sub>…X<sub>k</sub></span>, written so that <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>1</sub></span> becomes the new top.</li><li>The other half of the construction is a move <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, a) = {(q, ε)}</span> for each terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: when the top is a terminal that matches the input, both are consumed.</li><li>Option (A) would consume <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>1</sub></span> as input even when it is a nonterminal; option (B) has the arguments of <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> in the wrong order; option (C) expands backwards.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the arguments: <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> takes (state, input symbol or <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, stack top). The stack top is the last argument, and it is the nonterminal being expanded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nonterminal on top → ε-move that expands it; terminal on top → consume the matching input symbol.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following languages is accepted by some nondeterministic PDA but by <b>no</b> deterministic PDA?</p>", "options": ["{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>i</sup> b<sup>j</sup> c<sup>k</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">i = j</span> or <span class=\"mm\">j = k</span>, <span class=\"mm\">i, j, k ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>n</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">w c w<sup>R</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ {a, b}*</span> }", "{ <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup> b<sup>m</sup></span> : <span class=\"mm\">n ≠ m, n, m ≥ 0</span> }"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An NPDA can guess; a DPDA cannot. The languages that separate the two classes are exactly the ones where the machine must commit to one of several matching rules before it has enough information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) is context-free: it is the union of <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>i</sup>c<sup>k</sup>}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>j</sup>}</span>, each of which is context-free, and the CFLs are closed under union. An NPDA guesses at the start which of the two branches to follow.</li><li>Option (A) is not a DCFL. A DPDA reading the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block must decide whether it will compare the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-count with the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-count or the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-count with the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-count, and that decision cannot be made until the whole string has been seen. Whichever rule it commits to, the strings that satisfy only the other rule are rejected. This is the standard example of a CFL that is not deterministic.</li><li>Options (B) and (C) are DCFLs — push the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block and pop on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s; or push up to the visible <span class=\"mm\">c</span> and pop after it.</li><li>Option (D) is a DCFL too: it is the complement of <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span> inside the regular set <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span>, and DCFLs are closed under complement and under intersection with a regular language.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'or' as automatically nondeterministic and '≠' as automatically nondeterministic. It is the other way round here: the union in (A) is the nondeterministic one, while the inequality in (D) is deterministic.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two independent matching rules, chosen before the information arrives, is the signature of a CFL that is not deterministic.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A pushdown automaton is said to accept a string <b>by empty stack</b> when:</p>", "options": ["it reaches a final state at some point while reading the string.", "the stack is empty at some point during the computation.", "after reading the whole string the stack is empty, whatever the state.", "it reaches a final state after reading the whole string and the stack is also empty."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two acceptance modes look at different things. Acceptance by final state looks at the state after the input is consumed; acceptance by empty stack looks at the stack after the input is consumed and ignores the state entirely.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A computation must consume the whole input before it can accept — that rules out option (A).</li><li>In empty-stack acceptance the set of final states is irrelevant, so option (D) imposes an extra condition that is not required.</li><li>Option (B) drops the requirement that the input be exhausted: a machine may empty its stack halfway and then keep reading.</li><li>The correct condition is: the whole input is consumed and the stack is empty, with no condition on the state.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating 'empty stack' as an event that can happen mid-string. Acceptance always requires the input to be finished first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Final-state acceptance ignores the stack; empty-stack acceptance ignores the state. Both require the input to be exhausted.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A pushdown automaton has 2 states and stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>. A <i>configuration</i> is the current state together with the entire contents of the stack. How many distinct configurations does the automaton have in which the stack holds exactly one or two symbols?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A configuration of a PDA is (state, remaining input, stack contents). Counting configurations of bounded stack height is a counting problem on the stack alphabet, multiplied by the number of states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stacks of length 1: any single symbol of the stack alphabet, so <span class=\"mm\">2</span> possibilities (<span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> and <span class=\"mm\">A</span>).</li><li>Stacks of length 2: two independent choices, so <span class=\"mm\">2<sup>2</sup> = 4</span> possibilities (<span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>Z<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>A, AZ<sub>0</sub>, AA</span>).</li><li>Stack contents altogether: <span class=\"mm\">2 + 4 = 6</span>.</li><li>Multiply by the 2 states: <span class=\"mm\">2 × 6 = 12</span> configurations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only stacks that respect the bottom marker, i.e. requiring <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> to be at the bottom. That is a restriction on legal computations, not on configurations, and it would give 4 stacks instead of 6.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Configurations are state × stack, and the number of stacks of length <span class=\"mm\">L</span> over a <span class=\"mm\">k</span>-symbol alphabet is <span class=\"mm\">k<sup>L</sup></span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <b>nondeterministic</b> pushdown automata, which statement about the two acceptance modes is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Empty-stack acceptance is strictly weaker than final-state acceptance.", "A language is accepted by some NPDA by final state if and only if it is accepted by some NPDA by empty stack.", "Final-state acceptance is strictly weaker than empty-stack acceptance.", "Neither mode can accept the empty string."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For NPDAs the two acceptance modes are interchangeable: each can simulate the other, so they define the same class of languages, the context-free languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Final state → empty stack.</b> Add a new bottom marker below the old one and a new single final state; from every old final state add ε-moves that empty the stack. The new machine accepts by empty stack exactly when the old one reached a final state.</li><li><b>Empty stack → final state.</b> Add a new bottom marker and a new single final state; whenever the old stack would empty, move on ε to the new final state instead. Acceptance by empty stack becomes acceptance by final state.</li><li>Both simulations are language-preserving, so neither mode is weaker.</li><li>Option (D) is false: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> is accepted whenever the start configuration already satisfies the acceptance condition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Importing the deterministic picture, where the two modes really are not interchangeable. The equivalence is a property of <i>nondeterministic</i> machines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NPDA: the two modes are equivalent. DPDA: they are not, and that asymmetry is a favourite exam point.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about the two acceptance modes of pushdown automata that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["Every NPDA that accepts by final state can be converted into an NPDA that accepts the same language by empty stack.", "Every DPDA that accepts by final state can be converted into a DPDA that accepts the same language by empty stack.", "In a PDA that accepts by empty stack, acceptance depends only on the stack and not on the state.", "If a DPDA accepts a language by empty stack, then no proper prefix of a word of the language belongs to the language."], "answer_display": "Options (A, C and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The conversion between acceptance modes is free for NPDAs and is <i>not</i> free for DPDAs, because the conversion has to empty the stack at the right instant and a deterministic machine cannot postpone or duplicate that decision.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> Add a fresh bottom marker and a fresh single final state, and from each old final state add ε-moves that pop the stack away. Nothing here needs a choice, so the construction works for NPDAs.</li><li><b>(B) false.</b> A DPDA may reach a final state while input still remains. Emptying the stack at that moment would also accept every longer string with that prefix, which changes the language. Since the machine is deterministic it cannot keep the stack and empty it 'later instead'. The classic witness is <span class=\"mm\">L = {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>} ∪ {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c}</span>.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> That is the definition: the final-state set plays no part.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> Suppose <span class=\"mm\">x ∈ L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">x</span> is a proper prefix of <span class=\"mm\">y ∈ L</span>. After reading <span class=\"mm\">x</span> the stack is empty, and with an empty stack no further move is possible, so <span class=\"mm\">y</span> could never be accepted. This is the prefix property.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the NPDA equivalence carries over to DPDAs. It does not: only DCFLs with the prefix property can be accepted by a DPDA by empty stack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Empty-stack acceptance forces the prefix property; final-state acceptance does not. That is the whole difference.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Why can a DPDA that accepts by final state <b>not</b> always be turned into a DPDA that accepts the same language by empty stack?</p>", "options": ["Because a DPDA has no final states to remove.", "Because determinism forbids ε-moves, which such a conversion needs.", "Because the empty stack cannot be represented in a DPDA.", "Because the machine may reach a final state while input remains, so emptying the stack there would also accept every longer string having that prefix."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Empty-stack acceptance is a strictly stronger requirement: the stack must empty at the exact instant the last input symbol is read. A deterministic machine cannot arrange that if it was designed to stop at an earlier moment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take a DPDA <span class=\"mm\">M</span> accepting by final state and a word <span class=\"mm\">x ∈ L(M)</span>. In many DPDAs the machine enters a final state with input still unread — nothing forbids that.</li><li>The natural conversion adds ε-moves that empty the stack on entering a final state. Applied at that moment, the stack empties before the input is finished.</li><li>With an empty stack no move is possible, so the machine stops and the rest of the input is never read. Under the convention that acceptance requires the whole input to be consumed, it rejects; but under the naive reading it would accept every extension <span class=\"mm\">xy</span> as well.</li><li>Because the machine is deterministic it cannot both keep the stack and empty it 'later', so there is no way to delay the emptying until the input ends. Hence the conversion can fail.</li><li>Options (A), (B) and (C) are all false: DPDAs do have final states (which the conversion simply stops using), DPDAs may use ε-moves, and the empty stack is of course representable — it is the acceptance condition itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the conversion is always safe because it is safe for NPDAs. Nondeterminism lets the NPDA keep both options open; a DPDA must commit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A DPDA accepting by empty stack accepts a language with the prefix property. Languages without it can be accepted by final state only.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be a language accepted by some DPDA <b>by empty stack</b>. Which property must <span class=\"mm\">L</span> necessarily have?</p>", "options": ["No word of <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is a proper prefix of another word of <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is regular.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is closed under reversal.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is finite."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Once a stack is empty there is no symbol left to consult, so no further move is possible. That single observation forces the prefix property.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Suppose <span class=\"mm\">x, y ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">x</span> a proper prefix of <span class=\"mm\">y</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">y = xz</span> for some non-empty <span class=\"mm\">z</span>.</li><li>The accepting computation on <span class=\"mm\">y</span> must, after reading <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, still have consumed only the prefix <span class=\"mm\">x</span>. Since the machine is deterministic it is in the same configuration as it would be after reading <span class=\"mm\">x</span> alone — and that configuration accepts <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, so the stack is empty.</li><li>With an empty stack, <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> has no stack top to read, so no move exists and the remaining input <span class=\"mm\">z</span> can never be consumed. Contradiction.</li><li>So no word of <span class=\"mm\">L</span> can be a proper prefix of another — the prefix property.</li><li>The other options are far too strong: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 1}</span> is accepted by a DPDA by empty stack, is not regular, is not closed under reversal (<span class=\"mm\">{b<sup>n</sup>a<sup>n</sup>}</span> is not even deterministic in the required sense) and is infinite.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the prefix property with 'prefix-closed'. The prefix property says the language contains <i>no</i> word together with a proper prefix of it — the exact opposite of prefix-closed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Empty-stack acceptance ⇒ prefix property. Conversely, a language with the prefix property that is accepted by a DPDA by final state can be accepted by empty stack as well.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statement about context-free grammars and pushdown automata is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every context-free grammar has an equivalent deterministic pushdown automaton.", "Every context-free grammar has an equivalent nondeterministic pushdown automaton, and every NPDA has an equivalent context-free grammar.", "Every NPDA has an equivalent regular expression.", "A context-free grammar can generate only regular languages."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CFGs and NPDAs are two presentations of one class: the context-free languages. The equivalence fails as soon as 'nondeterministic' is replaced by 'deterministic'.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>CFG → NPDA.</b> The top-down construction: one state, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>, an ε-move per production and a pop per matching terminal.</li><li><b>NPDA → CFG.</b> The triple construction with variables <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span>.</li><li>Option (A) is false: <span class=\"mm\">{w w<sup>R</sup>}</span> is context-free but no DPDA accepts it.</li><li>Options (C) and (D) confuse the context-free class with the regular class; the regular languages are a proper subclass.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 'PDA' when 'NPDA' is meant. The default reading of 'PDA' in most textbooks is nondeterministic; determinism must be stated explicitly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFG ≡ NPDA, strictly weaker than nothing and strictly stronger than regular expressions.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The grammar <span class=\"mm\">S → AB</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A → aA | a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">B → bB | b</span> is converted to an NPDA by the standard top-down construction: one state, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>, a production replaces the top nonterminal by its right-hand side, and a terminal on top is popped when it matches the next input symbol. While the automaton accepts the string <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>, what is the largest number of symbols the stack ever holds, counting the bottom marker <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The stack of the top-down NPDA holds the unexpanded part of the sentential form, with the leftmost symbol on top. Its height therefore tracks how much of the derivation is still outstanding.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">S → AB</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>AB</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">A → aA</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>aAB</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">4</span>. This is the maximum.</li><li>Pop the matched <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>AB</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">A → a</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>aB</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>; pop: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>B</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">B → bB</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>bB</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>; pop: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>B</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">B → b</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>b</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>; pop: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> — height <span class=\"mm\">1</span>, stack empty of everything but the marker, input exhausted, accepted.</li><li>The largest height reached on this (the only accepting) computation is <span class=\"mm\">4</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only nonterminals, or forgetting that expanding <span class=\"mm\">A → aA</span> momentarily puts the terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span> on the stack as well. That terminal is a real stack symbol until it is matched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Height = <span class=\"mm\">1</span> (marker) + number of symbols in the current sentential form.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the standard construction of a context-free grammar from a pushdown automaton, the variables are triples <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span>. What does the variable <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span> generate?</p>", "options": ["All strings accepted by the automaton when it starts in state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>.", "All strings whose length is at most the stack height reached in state <span class=\"mm\">p</span>.", "All strings <span class=\"mm\">w</span> such that the automaton, starting in state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> on top of the stack, can read <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and end in state <span class=\"mm\">p</span> having popped <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, never inspecting the symbols below <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.", "All strings that take the automaton from its start state to a final state."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The triple construction records a whole sub-computation: from a state with a particular symbol on top, to another state with that symbol gone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The automaton's behaviour depends on the state and on the top stack symbol only — the symbols below it are invisible until the top is popped. That is why a variable needs exactly three pieces of information.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span> describes a computation that starts in <span class=\"mm\">q</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> on top, ends in <span class=\"mm\">p</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> removed, and never looks below <span class=\"mm\">A</span> in between.</li><li>Productions encode single moves: for a move that replaces <span class=\"mm\">A</span> by <span class=\"mm\">B<sub>1</sub>…B<sub>k</sub></span> and steps from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> to <span class=\"mm\">r</span>, add <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p] → a [r, B<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>1</sub>] … [r<sub>k−1</sub>, B<sub>k</sub>, p]</span>.</li><li>The start variable is <span class=\"mm\">[q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>, p]</span> for each final state <span class=\"mm\">p</span> — the whole computation is one such triple with the bottom marker.</li><li>Options (A) and (D) drop the stack symbol and the end state, which the construction needs; option (B) is not a language-theoretic notion at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that <span class=\"mm\">[q, A, p]</span> generates a string of the language. Only the start triples do; the others generate fragments of the computation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three pieces of information — start state, stack top, end state — because the rest of the stack is hidden.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A grammar has 5 productions and terminal alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{a, b}</span>. In the standard single-state NPDA construction, one move is added for each production and one for each terminal symbol. How many moves does the resulting NPDA have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The construction mirrors the grammar piece by piece: one ε-move per production (expand a nonterminal) and one input-consuming move per terminal (match and pop).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Production moves: <span class=\"mm\">5</span>, each of the form <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, A) ∋ (q, α)</span>.</li><li>Terminal moves: <span class=\"mm\">2</span>, namely <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, a) = {(q, ε)}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, b, b) = {(q, ε)}</span>.</li><li>Total: <span class=\"mm\">5 + 2 = 7</span> moves.</li><li>No move is needed for the start-up step: the initial stack is simply defined to be <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding an extra move for the start symbol or for the bottom marker. Neither is a move — both are part of the initial configuration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Moves = <span class=\"mm\">|P| + |T|</span> for the single-state construction.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The grammar <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | ε</span> is converted to an NPDA by the standard top-down construction with initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>. During the computation that accepts <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>, what is the largest number of symbols the stack ever holds, counting <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>?</p>", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each application of <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb</span> adds two symbols to the sentential form; each matched terminal removes one. The peak comes just after an expansion, before the terminal is matched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>aSb</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">4</span>.</li><li>Match the first <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>Sb</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb</span> again: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>aSbb</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">5</span>. This is the maximum.</li><li>Match the second <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>Sbb</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">4</span>.</li><li>Expand <span class=\"mm\">S → ε</span>: <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>bb</span> — height <span class=\"mm\">3</span>.</li><li>Match both <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s: height <span class=\"mm\">2</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">1</span>. Input exhausted, only <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> left, accepted by empty stack.</li><li>Maximum height: <span class=\"mm\">5</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) 6 is reached by expanding <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb</span> a third time, but that branch dies: only two <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are available in <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>. The question asks about the accepting computation, not about every branch the nondeterministic machine can explore.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | ε</span> on <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span> the peak height is <span class=\"mm\">2n + 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">n</span> unmatched <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-expansions plus the marker.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about the equivalence of context-free grammars and pushdown automata that is TRUE.</p>", "options": ["A grammar in Greibach Normal Form is simulated by an NPDA that has a single state and accepts by empty stack.", "The class of languages accepted by nondeterministic pushdown automata is exactly the class of context-free languages.", "Every context-free grammar has an equivalent deterministic pushdown automaton.", "If an NPDA accepts a language by empty stack, then some context-free grammar generates that language."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The equivalence CFG ≡ NPDA is exact, and it has a concrete constructive form on each side. The single-state simulation works especially cleanly for Greibach Normal Form, where every production begins with a terminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> In GNF every production is <span class=\"mm\">A → aα</span>. The single-state automaton reads <span class=\"mm\">a</span> from the input and replaces <span class=\"mm\">A</span> by <span class=\"mm\">α</span> in one move; after <span class=\"mm\">|w|</span> moves the stack is empty exactly when <span class=\"mm\">w</span> has been generated.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> This is the standard equivalence, proved by the top-down and triple constructions.</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> <span class=\"mm\">{w w<sup>R</sup>}</span> has a context-free grammar but no DPDA, because the midpoint is unmarked.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> That is the direction of the equivalence that converts a machine into a grammar.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'equivalent' as 'equivalent deterministically'. The equivalence is with the nondeterministic machine; the deterministic subclass is strictly smaller.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> GNF gives the cleanest CFG → NPDA simulation: one state, one move per production application.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the formal definition of a <b>deterministic</b> pushdown automaton, the transition function <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> has which of the following types?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × Σ × Γ → Q × Γ*</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) × Γ → 2<sup>Q × Γ*</sup></span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) × Γ → (Q × Γ*) ∪ {∅}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × Σ* × Γ* → Q</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A deterministic pushdown automaton is defined by a <i>partial</i> function: from each argument <span class=\"mm\">(q, a, X)</span> there is at most one successor, and possibly none. The set-valued transition function is what makes a machine nondeterministic.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The three arguments of <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> are the current state, the next input symbol (or <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> for an input-free move) and the symbol currently on top of the stack, so the domain must be <span class=\"mm\">Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) × Γ</span>.</li><li>The value must be at most one pair (next state, string pushed in place of the stack top). A value of <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> means \"no move is possible\", which is exactly what a partial function expresses.</li><li>Option (A) drops <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> from the domain, so it forbids input-free moves; a deterministic PDA is allowed to have them.</li><li>Option (B) is the transition function of a <i>nondeterministic</i> PDA: a set of successors per argument.</li><li>Option (D) has the wrong domain and returns a state with no stack string, so it cannot describe how the stack changes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates think a deterministic machine must be <i>total</i>, so they reject the <span class=\"mm\">∪ {∅}</span> in the codomain. Determinism limits the number of successors; it never requires one to exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Deterministic = at most one successor per <span class=\"mm\">(q, a, X)</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> allowed as an input symbol. Set-valued <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> is the hallmark of nondeterminism.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["Every deterministic context-free language is regular.", "Every context-free language is accepted by some deterministic pushdown automaton.", "The class of deterministic context-free languages is closed under union.", "Every regular language is accepted by some deterministic pushdown automaton."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The four classes nest as regular ⊂ deterministic context-free ⊂ context-free. A DFA is a degenerate DPDA that never writes to its stack, which gives the first inclusion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take any DFA and add a stack that is never read or written. Every move of the DFA becomes a move of the PDA that leaves the stack untouched, and at most one move is available from each configuration. The result is a DPDA accepting the same language, so (D) is true.</li><li>(A) is false: <span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span> is accepted by a DPDA but is not regular.</li><li>(B) is false: <span class=\"mm\">{ w w<sup>R</sup> : w ∈ {a, b}* }</span> is context-free but has no deterministic PDA, because nothing marks the midpoint.</li><li>(C) is false: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>2n</sup>}</span> are both deterministic context-free, yet their union is not, since a DPDA cannot decide how many <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s to match per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"deterministic context-free\" as \"almost regular\". The stack still gives full counting power; determinism restricts the number of choices, not the memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL, and both inclusions are strict.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A deterministic pushdown automaton has 3 states, input alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b}</span> and stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Γ = {Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>. Its transition function is a partial function of <span class=\"mm\">(state, input symbol or ε, stack top)</span>. At most how many distinct arguments can that transition function specify?", "options": [], "answer_display": "18", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The arguments of <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> range over states × (input symbols together with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>) × stack symbols, so the number of possible arguments is the product of the three sizes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States: 3.</li><li>Input symbols together with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>: <span class=\"mm\">2 + 1 = 3</span>.</li><li>Stack symbols: 2.</li><li>Product: <span class=\"mm\">3 × 3 × 2 = 18</span>.</li><li>Determinism says each of these 18 arguments carries at most one successor; it does not require any of them to be defined, so 18 is an upper bound that a machine can attain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 18</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> and answering <span class=\"mm\">3 × 2 × 2 = 12</span>. An input-free move is a legal argument of <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> in a deterministic PDA, so it must be counted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Number of possible <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> arguments = <span class=\"mm\">|Q| × (|Σ| + 1) × |Γ|</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c : n ≥ 0 }</span>, and let <span class=\"mm\">L = L<sub>1</sub> ∪ L<sub>2</sub></span>. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["The DCFLs are not closed under union, so <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not deterministic context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free but not deterministic context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is regular.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is deterministic context-free."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The failure of closure under union is caused by a machine that would have to <i>guess</i> which of two shapes it is reading. Here no guess is needed, because the two shapes differ at a point the automaton can see.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A DPDA reads <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup></span>, pushing one <span class=\"mm\">A</span> per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, then reads <span class=\"mm\">b<sup>n</sup></span> popping one <span class=\"mm\">A</span> per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>When the stack returns to <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> the machine has consumed exactly <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span>. It now looks at the next symbol, if any.</li><li>If the input has ended, the word is in <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub></span> and the machine accepts. If the next symbol is <span class=\"mm\">c</span>, it consumes that single <span class=\"mm\">c</span> and accepts, which covers <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span>.</li><li>Every step above is forced, so the machine is deterministic and <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is a DCFL.</li><li>Contrast the classical counterexample: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>} ∪ {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>2n</sup>}</span>. There the choice \"one <span class=\"mm\">b</span> per <span class=\"mm\">a</span> or two\" must be made while the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s are still being read, with no visible difference between the two cases.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the closure table mechanically: \"DCFLs are not closed under union, therefore any union of two DCFLs is not a DCFL\". Closure fails for <i>some</i> unions, not for all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A union is deterministic when the two languages can be told apart by information the machine can see deterministically — here, end of input versus a literal <span class=\"mm\">c</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about deterministic pushdown automata that is TRUE.", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is a deterministic context-free language, then so is its complement.", "Every deterministic context-free language is accepted by some DPDA by final state.", "The class of deterministic context-free languages is closed under intersection.", "Every deterministic context-free language is accepted by some DPDA by empty stack."], "answer_display": "Options (A and B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DPDA has exactly one computation on each input, so its accepting behaviour can be complemented by swapping final and non-final states. The same uniqueness is what breaks down when the acceptance mode is changed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true. Every computation of a DPDA is a single path. Add a non-accepting sink state for the case where the machine halts early, then exchange final and non-final states. The result is still deterministic and accepts exactly the complement.</li><li>(B) true by definition: the class of DCFLs <i>is</i> the class of languages accepted by deterministic PDAs by final state.</li><li>(C) false. <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>m</sup> }</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub> = { a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup> }</span> are both DCFLs, but their intersection is <span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup> }</span>, which is not even context-free.</li><li>(D) false. Empty-stack acceptance forces the prefix property, and <span class=\"mm\">L = {a} ∪ {ab}</span> is a DCFL that violates it: it is accepted by a DPDA by final state, but a DPDA that empties its stack after reading <span class=\"mm\">a</span> can never read the <span class=\"mm\">b</span> of <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the DCFLs inherit the closure properties of the CFLs. The CFL proofs use nondeterministic guessing, which determinism removes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DCFL: complement yes; union and intersection in general no; intersection and union with a regular language yes.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["Every deterministic context-free language has an unambiguous context-free grammar, but not every unambiguous context-free language is deterministic context-free.", "Every unambiguous context-free language is deterministic context-free.", "Every context-free language has an unambiguous context-free grammar.", "A language is deterministic context-free if and only if it has an unambiguous context-free grammar."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DPDA produces at most one parse per string, so determinism implies unambiguity. The converse fails: unambiguity is a property of one grammar, determinism a property of the language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>From a DPDA build a grammar by the triple construction. Each accepting computation of the DPDA gives one derivation, and there is at most one computation per string, so the grammar is unambiguous. The first half of (A) holds.</li><li>Now take <span class=\"mm\">L = { w w<sup>R</sup> : w ∈ {a, b}* }</span>. It has the unambiguous grammar <span class=\"mm\">S → aSa | bSb | ε</span>: the outermost pair of symbols determines the outer production uniquely, and the argument repeats inside.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L</span> is nevertheless not a DCFL: nothing marks the midpoint, so a DPDA cannot know when to stop pushing and start matching.</li><li>So unambiguity does not imply determinism, which kills (B) and (D).</li><li>(C) is false: languages such as <span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>k</sup> : i = j or j = k }</span> are inherently ambiguous — every grammar for them is ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"unambiguous\" and \"deterministic\" as synonyms. Unambiguity says a string has one parse; determinism says the machine never chooses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DCFL ⊊ unambiguous CFL ⊊ CFL, and all three inclusions are strict.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "deterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "How many of the following five languages are <b>deterministic</b> context-free?<br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup> : n ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>3</sub> = { w c w<sup>R</sup> : w ∈ {a, b}* }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub> = { a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>k</sup> : i = j or j = k, i, j, k ≥ 0 }</span><br><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>5</sub> = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>m</sup> : n ≠ m, n, m ≥ 0 }</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three separate tests decide the count: can a single stack supply the memory; is a midpoint visible; and is a \"not equal\" condition expressible as a complement inside a regular set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub></span>: push one symbol per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, pop one per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. No choice is ever made, so it is deterministic — count 1.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>2</sub></span>: needs two independent counts to be matched at once. One stack cannot do it, so the language is not context-free, hence not deterministic.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>3</sub></span>: the literal <span class=\"mm\">c</span> marks the midpoint, so pushing up to <span class=\"mm\">c</span> and matching afterwards is forced. Deterministic — count 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub></span>: context-free, but a DPDA would have to commit to \"match the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block with the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-block\" or to \"match the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-block with the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-block\" before it has seen the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>-block. Not deterministic.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L<sub>5</sub></span>: inside the regular set <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> it is the complement of <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span>. DCFLs are closed under complement and under intersection with a regular language, so it is deterministic — count 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two opposite errors: calling <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>5</sub></span> nondeterministic because of the \"≠\", and calling <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>4</sub></span> deterministic because it \"looks like a union of two simple languages\".</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A \"≠\" between two counts usually survives determinism through complement; an unmarked midpoint or two competing matching rules do not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A <b>nondeterministic</b> pushdown automaton accepts a string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> when:", "options": ["every computation on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> ends in an accepting configuration.", "at least one computation on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> reads all of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and ends in an accepting configuration.", "at least one computation on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> enters an accepting configuration, whether or not the input has been fully read.", "its computation tree on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> contains no infinite branch."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Acceptance by a nondeterministic machine is existential: one successful branch is enough. But \"successful\" still means the whole input has been consumed and the accepting condition is met at that moment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The computation of an NPDA on a fixed input is a tree, whose branches are the different choices of moves.</li><li>Acceptance is defined by the existence of <i>one</i> branch that reads the entire input and halts in a final state — option (B).</li><li>(A) describes the universal condition, which no nondeterministic machine uses.</li><li>(C) drops the requirement that the input be finished, so it would accept every string having an accepted prefix.</li><li>(D) is irrelevant: a branch may loop forever without affecting acceptance, since only one finite accepting branch is needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that an NPDA must explore every branch successfully, or that an infinite branch spoils acceptance. Neither is true: acceptance is one good branch, and infinite branches are simply ignored.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NPDA acceptance = ∃ a computation that consumes the whole input and accepts at the end.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A pushdown automaton has 2 states and stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Γ = {Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>. A configuration is the current state together with the whole stack content, and the stack always holds at least one symbol. How many distinct configurations does the automaton have in which the stack is exactly three symbols long?", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A configuration is a pair (state, stack content). The number of configurations of a fixed stack height is the number of states times the number of stack contents of that height.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A stack content of length 3 is a string of three symbols drawn from <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>, so there are <span class=\"mm\">2<sup>3</sup> = 8</span> contents.</li><li>Each content can occur in either of the 2 states.</li><li>Total: <span class=\"mm\">2 × 8 = 16</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the input as well, or forgetting the state. A configuration counts only the state and the stack; the remaining input is a third component that this question does not ask about.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Configurations at stack height <span class=\"mm\">h</span> = <span class=\"mm\">|Q| × |Γ|<sup>h</sup></span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A grammar <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span> is turned into an NPDA by the standard top-down construction: one state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub>S</span>, acceptance by empty stack. The accepting computations of that NPDA on a string <span class=\"mm\">w</span> correspond exactly to:", "options": ["the leftmost derivations of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> in <span class=\"mm\">G</span>.", "the rightmost derivations of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> in <span class=\"mm\">G</span>.", "the derivations of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> in <span class=\"mm\">G</span>, counted once for every possible order in which the nonterminals are expanded.", "the strings of length <span class=\"mm\">|w|</span> that <span class=\"mm\">G</span> can generate."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The stack always shows the leftmost remaining nonterminal on top, so the machine can only ever expand that one. A run of the machine is therefore a leftmost derivation, and conversely.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Initially the stack holds <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, which is the leftmost remaining nonterminal.</li><li>A production move replaces the top nonterminal by its right-hand side, written so that the leftmost symbol ends on top. The next nonterminal to be expanded is again the leftmost one.</li><li>A terminal on top is popped only when it matches the next input symbol, which forces the derivation to proceed in the order the string is read.</li><li>So each accepting computation is exactly one leftmost derivation, and each leftmost derivation gives exactly one accepting computation.</li><li>(B) and (C) are wrong because other expansion orders are never simulated; (D) confuses the set of generated strings with the derivations of one fixed string.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the NPDA enumerates all derivations. It simulates only leftmost ones, which is why the number of accepting computations equals the number of parse trees — each tree has exactly one leftmost derivation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Accepting computations of the single-state NPDA ↔ leftmost derivations of <span class=\"mm\">w</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about the class of languages accepted by nondeterministic pushdown automata that is TRUE.", "options": ["It is closed under union.", "It is closed under intersection.", "It is closed under complement.", "It is closed under concatenation."], "answer_display": "Options (A and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is exactly the class of context-free languages, and the CFL closure table is highly asymmetric: all the \"combine two machines\" operations work, the \"restrict\" operations do not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: run the two NPDAs from a new start state, guessing which one to follow.</li><li>(B) false: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>m</sup>} ∩ {a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>} = {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>}</span>, which is not context-free.</li><li>(C) false: if the CFLs were closed under complement, then by closure under union and the identity <span class=\"mm\">L<sub>1</sub> ∩ L<sub>2</sub> = ¬(¬L<sub>1</sub> ∪ ¬L<sub>2</sub>)</span> they would also be closed under intersection, contradicting (B).</li><li>(D) true: connect the final states of the first machine to the start of the second with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the Boolean algebra of regular languages carries over. It does not: the CFLs are closed under union but under neither intersection nor complement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFL: closed under union, concatenation, Kleene star, reversal, homomorphism; not under intersection or complement.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">L = { w ∈ {a, b}* : w = w<sup>R</sup> }</span>, the set of palindromes over <span class=\"mm\">{a, b}</span>. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is regular.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is deterministic context-free but not regular.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free but not deterministic context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not context-free."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Palindromes are the textbook example of a language that a single stack can handle only nondeterministically: the machine must guess where the middle of the word is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">L</span> is generated by <span class=\"mm\">S → aSa | bSb | ε | a | b</span>, so it is context-free.</li><li>A deterministic PDA has to decide, at each position, whether it is still in the first half or already in the second half. With no marker in the string, both readings are consistent with the input it has seen so far.</li><li>Any deterministic strategy therefore fails on some word: choose a palindrome whose middle is not where the machine decided to switch, and it either pops too early or too late.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not a DCFL, which rules out (B); it is not regular because a DFA cannot store the first half, which rules out (A); (D) is false by the grammar above.</li><li>Compare <span class=\"mm\">{ w c w<sup>R</sup> }</span>: the literal <span class=\"mm\">c</span> is a visible marker, and that language <i>is</i> a DCFL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing palindromes with marked palindromes. <span class=\"mm\">{w c w<sup>R</sup>}</span> is deterministic; the unmarked version is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No marker at the midpoint ⟹ the machine must guess ⟹ nondeterminism is required.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "nondeterministic-pda", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Consider the NPDA <span class=\"mm\">M</span> with states <span class=\"mm\">{q<sub>0</sub>, q<sub>1</sub>}</span>, stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{Z<sub>0</sub>, A}</span>, start state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span>, final state <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>1</sub></span>, initial stack <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span>, and moves<br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, A A)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>0</sub>, ε), (q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)}</span><br><span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q<sub>1</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span><br>How many distinct sequences of moves take <span class=\"mm\">M</span> from its start configuration to a configuration that has read the whole of <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> and is in the final state?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Counting accepting computations means walking the computation tree and keeping only the branches that consume the entire input and finish in the final state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, aabb, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>. The first <span class=\"mm\">a</span> must use <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, abb, A Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>.</li><li>The second <span class=\"mm\">a</span> must use <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, a, A)</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, bb, A A Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>. No choice yet.</li><li>The first <span class=\"mm\">b</span> has two choices. <b>Branch 1</b>: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, ε)</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, b, A Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>. <b>Branch 2</b>: <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, ε)</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>.</li><li>Branch 1 again has two choices on the last <span class=\"mm\">b</span>: staying in <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> gives <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, and switching gives <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, which is accepting at once.</li><li>Branch 2 has only one choice, <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>1</sub>, b, A)</span>, giving <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, which is accepting.</li><li>The leftover branch <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> can still take <span class=\"mm\">δ(q<sub>0</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> to <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>1</sub>, ε, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span>, which is accepting too.</li><li>The three accepting computations are therefore: switch on the first <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; switch on the second <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; and stay in <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> throughout, using the <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-move at the end.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at two branches. The <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-move out of <span class=\"mm\">(q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>)</span> creates a third accepting route, and it is easy to overlook because no input symbol is consumed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count along the whole computation tree, and remember that <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves branch too.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A PDA that accepts by final state reaches a final state after reading a proper prefix of <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, and then goes on reading the rest of <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Does it accept <span class=\"mm\">w</span>?", "options": ["Yes, because a final state was reached during the computation.", "No, because acceptance requires that the whole input has been read at the moment the final state is entered.", "Yes, provided the stack is also empty at that moment.", "No, unless the automaton has no <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Acceptance by final state is a statement about the <i>end</i> of the computation: the input must be exhausted and the state final. Passing through a final state earlier counts for nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition reads: <span class=\"mm\">w</span> is accepted when some computation starting from the start configuration consumes all of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and halts in a final state.</li><li>A final state visited in the middle of the input is only an intermediate configuration, so it is not acceptance — option (B).</li><li>(A) and (C) both treat the intermediate visit as acceptance, and (C) additionally mixes in the other acceptance mode.</li><li>(D) invents a restriction on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves that plays no part here.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the \"all input consumed\" clause, which is exactly the clause that makes the empty-stack and final-state modes differ in power.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Final-state acceptance = input exhausted ∧ state final, at the same instant.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements about acceptance by <b>empty stack</b> is TRUE?", "options": ["The stack must become empty at some point during the computation.", "The automaton must have no final states.", "The state in which the computation ends plays no part in the decision.", "The stack must be empty before the whole input has been read."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Acceptance by empty stack is decided purely by the stack, evaluated after the whole input has been read. The final state is irrelevant to it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition reads: <span class=\"mm\">w</span> is accepted when some computation consumes all of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and ends with an empty stack, whatever state it is in.</li><li>So the ending state does not matter — option (C).</li><li>(A) and (D) both move the emptiness to the wrong time: the stack must be empty at the end, not \"at some point\" and certainly not before the input is finished.</li><li>(B) is false. A machine may perfectly well have final states; they are simply ignored when acceptance is by empty stack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a final state must also be reached, i.e. combining both acceptance conditions. The two modes are alternatives, not a conjunction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Empty-stack acceptance = input exhausted ∧ stack empty; the state is irrelevant.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The standard construction converts a PDA that accepts by final state into one that accepts the same language by empty stack. It adds a new start state, which pushes a fresh bottom marker below the old one, and a new state that erases the stack, reachable by an <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-move from every old final state. How many states does the construction add?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two things must happen that the original machine cannot be trusted to do: the marker must be protected from being popped mid-computation, and the stack must be wiped at acceptance. Each needs one new state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The new start state pushes a fresh marker and then enters the old start configuration, so that the old machine cannot pop the bottom marker by accident. That is one new state.</li><li>The erasing state is entered by an <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-move from every old final state, and pops symbols until the stack is empty. That is the second new state.</li><li>No other state is needed: the old states and moves are kept unchanged.</li><li>Total added: <span class=\"mm\">1 + 1 = 2</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 by counting only the erasing state, or answering 3 by adding a separate accepting state — the empty stack itself is the accepting condition, so no final state is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Final-state ⟶ empty-stack conversion: +2 states (fresh start, eraser).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Regarding acceptance by empty stack and acceptance by final state, select every statement that is TRUE.", "options": ["For nondeterministic pushdown automata the two modes accept exactly the same class of languages.", "If a DPDA accepts a language by empty stack, then no word of that language is a proper prefix of another word of it.", "If a DPDA accepts a language by final state, then no word of that language is a proper prefix of another word of it.", "Every language accepted by a DPDA by empty stack is also accepted by some DPDA by final state."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For NPDAs the two modes are interchangeable; for DPDAs only one direction is free. The obstruction is the prefix property, which empty-stack acceptance forces on a deterministic machine.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: both constructions (add an eraser; add a marker and a fresh start) are nondeterministic-safe, so each mode can simulate the other.</li><li>(B) true: a DPDA has one computation per input. If it empties the stack after reading <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, no move is possible from an empty stack, so it can never read on to a longer word having <span class=\"mm\">w</span> as prefix.</li><li>(C) false: <span class=\"mm\">L = {a} ∪ {ab}</span> is accepted by a DPDA by final state, and <span class=\"mm\">a</span> is a proper prefix of <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>.</li><li>(D) true: from a DPDA accepting by empty stack, add a single new final state reachable by an <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-move whenever the stack becomes empty. Determinism is preserved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the equivalence for NPDAs carries over to DPDAs in both directions. It does not: final-state to empty-stack can fail, and the prefix property is exactly why.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NPDA: the modes are equivalent. DPDA: empty-stack ⊆ final-state, strictly.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements about the prefix property is TRUE?", "options": ["Empty-stack acceptance forces the prefix property for both deterministic and nondeterministic PDAs.", "Empty-stack acceptance forces the prefix property for deterministic PDAs only.", "Empty-stack acceptance forces the prefix property for nondeterministic PDAs only.", "Empty-stack acceptance never forces the prefix property."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The prefix property (no word of the language is a proper prefix of another) follows from empty-stack acceptance only when the machine has a single computation per input.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Deterministic case: once the stack is empty there is no move available, so the machine cannot continue past an accepted word. Hence no accepted word is a proper prefix of another — (B).</li><li>Nondeterministic case: different branches may empty the stack at different lengths. For <span class=\"mm\">L = {a, ab}</span> one branch empties the stack after the <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and another after the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so the property fails. Hence (A) and (C) are wrong.</li><li>(D) is wrong because the deterministic case does force it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that any machine accepting by empty stack is stuck after acceptance. Only a deterministic machine is: a nondeterministic one may have other live branches.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Prefix property: forced by DPDA + empty stack; not forced by NPDA + empty stack.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "acceptance-empty-store-vs-final-state", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">L = { a, ab }</span>. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is accepted by some DPDA by final state but by no DPDA by empty stack.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is accepted by some DPDA by empty stack.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not deterministic context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not context-free."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A finite language is regular, hence deterministic context-free. But the prefix property is a hard constraint on empty-stack acceptance by a deterministic machine, and this language breaks it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>L is finite, so it is regular, so it is a DCFL: a DPDA accepting by final state reads <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and accepts, or reads <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> and accepts. That kills (C) and (D).</li><li>Suppose a DPDA <span class=\"mm\">M</span> accepted <span class=\"mm\">L</span> by empty stack. On input <span class=\"mm\">a</span> it must end with an empty stack after consuming the single symbol.</li><li>An empty stack admits no move, so <span class=\"mm\">M</span> is stuck on input <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and can never read the extra <span class=\"mm\">b</span> needed for <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>. Hence <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> would not be accepted, a contradiction.</li><li>Equivalently, <span class=\"mm\">L</span> lacks the prefix property, which empty-stack acceptance forces on deterministic machines. So (B) is false and (A) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reasoning \"finite ⟹ regular ⟹ accepted by every kind of PDA\". The class of the language is not in question; the acceptance <i>mode</i> is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Prefix property is the sharp test: <span class=\"mm\">{a, ab}</span> is a DCFL that no DPDA accepts by empty stack.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A context-free grammar in Greibach Normal Form is simulated by the standard top-down construction. How many states does that NPDA have?", "options": ["1", "<span class=\"mm\">|V|</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">|V| + |T|</span>", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In Greibach Normal Form every production starts with a terminal, so the machine can pop the nonterminal and push the rest of the right-hand side in one move that consumes exactly that terminal. No state is needed to remember anything.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A GNF production has the form <span class=\"mm\">A → a X<sub>1</sub> … X<sub>k</sub></span> with <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ T</span>.</li><li>The machine uses one move <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, A) ∋ (q, X<sub>1</sub> … X<sub>k</sub>)</span> that consumes <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and replaces <span class=\"mm\">A</span> by the rest of the right-hand side.</li><li>All the information about which nonterminal is being expanded sits on the stack, so a single state suffices.</li><li>The machine accepts by empty stack when the input is exhausted and the stack is empty.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — one state.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking each nonterminal needs its own state. The nonterminal is on top of the stack, not in the control unit, so the state carries no information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> GNF ⟹ one-state NPDA. The stack, not the state, holds the grammar.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Regarding the equivalence of context-free grammars and nondeterministic pushdown automata, select every statement that is TRUE.", "options": ["Every context-free grammar has an equivalent NPDA that accepts by empty stack.", "Every NPDA has an equivalent context-free grammar.", "The standard grammar-to-NPDA construction uses one state per nonterminal of the grammar.", "The standard NPDA-to-grammar construction produces a regular grammar."], "answer_display": "Options (A and B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Context-free grammars and nondeterministic pushdown automata generate and accept exactly the same class of languages; the two standard constructions prove each direction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: the single-state top-down construction turns any grammar into an NPDA that accepts by empty stack.</li><li>(B) true: the triple construction builds a grammar from any NPDA, with variables <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span>.</li><li>(C) false: that construction uses exactly one state for the whole machine; the nonterminals live on the stack.</li><li>(D) false: the grammar produced has variables of the form <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> and can generate non-regular languages such as <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span>, so it is context-free and in general not regular.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking \"equivalent\" to mean \"the same shape\". The constructions preserve the language, not the number of states, the number of variables or the class of the artefact built.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CFG ≡ NPDA. One state is enough in one direction; triples <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> are the price in the other.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the standard single-state construction that turns a grammar <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span> into an NPDA, which move is added for a terminal symbol <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ T</span>?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, ε, a) = {(q, ε)}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, ε) = {(q, a)}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, Z<sub>0</sub>) = {(q, a Z<sub>0</sub>)}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, a) = {(q, ε)}</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Once a terminal reaches the top of the stack, it must be matched against the next input symbol and removed. That is one move per terminal, independent of the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A terminal can appear on top of the stack only after some production has pushed it there, and the string it belongs to must now start at the current input position.</li><li>So the machine reads that same terminal from the input and pops it: <span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, a) = {(q, ε)}</span> — option (D).</li><li>(A) pops the terminal without consuming input, so it would accept strings that do not contain the terminal.</li><li>(B) and (C) push symbols instead of removing them, and use a stack top that has nothing to do with the matching rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing the move with the input symbol in the stack position, or with <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> as the input symbol. Both break the one-to-one matching between stack top and input.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grammar-to-NPDA: one move per production (replace a nonterminal) and one per terminal (<span class=\"mm\">δ(q, a, a) = {(q, ε)}</span>).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An NPDA has 4 states and stack alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Γ = {Z<sub>0</sub>, A, B}</span>. The standard construction builds a context-free grammar whose variables are triples <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span>. How many variables does it create?", "options": ["12", "16", "48", "64"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A variable <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> is indexed by an ordered pair of states and one stack symbol, so the count is <span class=\"mm\">|Q|<sup>2</sup> × |Γ|</span> — the pair is ordered because the two states play different roles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>: 4 choices.</li><li>The second state <span class=\"mm\">p</span>: 4 choices, independent of the first, so <span class=\"mm\">4 × 4 = 16</span> ordered pairs.</li><li>The stack symbol <span class=\"mm\">X</span>: 3 choices.</li><li>Total: <span class=\"mm\">16 × 3 = 48</span>.</li><li>Note that <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> and <span class=\"mm\">[p, X, q]</span> are different variables: the first generates strings that take the machine from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> to <span class=\"mm\">p</span> while popping <span class=\"mm\">X</span>, and the second does the reverse journey.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 48.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using combinations instead of ordered pairs and answering <span class=\"mm\">10 × 3 = 30</span>, or forgetting the stack symbol and answering 16.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Variables in the triple construction = <span class=\"mm\">|Q|<sup>2</sup>|Γ|</span>, with ordered pairs.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<span class=\"mm\">G</span> is the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S → SS | a</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">M</span> is the NPDA built from <span class=\"mm\">G</span> by the standard single-state top-down construction with acceptance by empty stack. How many accepting computations does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> have on the string <span class=\"mm\">aaa</span>?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Accepting computations of the single-state NPDA are in one-to-one correspondence with leftmost derivations, which are in one-to-one correspondence with parse trees. So the question is a Catalan count in disguise.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The number of parse trees for a string of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> terminals under <span class=\"mm\">S → SS | a</span> is the Catalan number <span class=\"mm\">C<sub>n−1</sub></span>, because the tree is a full binary tree with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> leaves.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">aaa</span> we have <span class=\"mm\">n = 3</span>, so the count is <span class=\"mm\">C<sub>2</sub> = 2</span>.</li><li>Tree 1: <span class=\"mm\">S ⇒ SS ⇒ aS ⇒ aSS ⇒ aaS ⇒ aaa</span> — the left <span class=\"mm\">S</span> splits first.</li><li>Tree 2: <span class=\"mm\">S ⇒ SS ⇒ SS S ⇒ aSS ⇒ aaS ⇒ aaa</span> — the right <span class=\"mm\">S</span> splits first, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">a</span> grouped with <span class=\"mm\">aa</span>.</li><li>Each tree has exactly one leftmost derivation, and each leftmost derivation is one accepting computation, so the machine has 2 accepting computations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 by assuming the NPDA behaves deterministically, or 3 by counting the three positions at which the split could occur. The two splits are not distinguishable in that way: the outer <span class=\"mm\">S → SS</span> always fires first, and the ambiguity is inside.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Accepting computations = leftmost derivations = parse trees; for <span class=\"mm\">S → SS | a</span> that is a Catalan number.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U3B-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "pushdown-automata", "subtopic": "equivalence-with-cfg", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Select every statement about the standard construction of a context-free grammar from a pushdown automaton that is TRUE.", "options": ["The variables are triples <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">q</span> and <span class=\"mm\">p</span> are states and <span class=\"mm\">X</span> is a stack symbol.", "For every state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> and every stack symbol <span class=\"mm\">X</span> the grammar contains the production <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, q] → ε</span>.", "The grammar produced by the construction is always unambiguous.", "The start variable is <span class=\"mm\">[q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>, p]</span> for a final state <span class=\"mm\">p</span> of the automaton."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The variable <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> generates exactly the strings that carry the machine from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> to <span class=\"mm\">p</span> while popping <span class=\"mm\">X</span> without ever looking below it. The base case is the empty journey.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: that is the definition of the variables in this construction.</li><li>(B) true: the machine may stay in the same state and pop nothing at all, so the empty string is generated by <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, q]</span> for every state and stack symbol. This is the base case that lets derivations terminate.</li><li>(C) false: the construction produces a grammar that mirrors the automaton, and a nondeterministic automaton generally yields an ambiguous grammar.</li><li>(D) true: a string is accepted when some computation starts at <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>0</sub></span> with <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> on the stack, ends in a final state <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, and pops <span class=\"mm\">Z<sub>0</sub></span> on the way — precisely the language of <span class=\"mm\">[q<sub>0</sub>, Z<sub>0</sub>, p]</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the construction to produce a tidy or unambiguous grammar. It is a correctness construction, not a parsing construction: the grammar faithfully copies every choice the automaton can make.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, p]</span> generates the strings read while popping <span class=\"mm\">X</span> from <span class=\"mm\">q</span> to <span class=\"mm\">p</span>; <span class=\"mm\">[q, X, q] → ε</span> is the base case.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements about variants of the Turing machine is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["A Turing machine whose tape is infinite in both directions accepts strictly more languages than a Turing machine whose tape is infinite in only one direction.", "A Turing machine with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> work tapes accepts strictly more languages than a single-tape Turing machine.", "A tape infinite in both directions and a tape infinite in one direction accept exactly the same class of languages; only the running time may change.", "A nondeterministic Turing machine accepts strictly more languages than a deterministic Turing machine."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> All the usual variants — a two-way infinite tape, several tapes, a stay-put head, a two-dimensional tape, nondeterminism — accept exactly the class of recursively enumerable languages. They differ in <i>time</i> and <i>space</i> complexity, never in recognition power.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A one-way infinite tape simulates a two-way infinite tape by <b>folding</b> it: cells to the left of the origin are stored in a second track, in reverse order, with an end marker. Each simulated move costs <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> real moves.</li><li>A single tape simulates <code class=\"mm\">k</code> tapes by storing all <code class=\"mm\">k</code> tapes on one tape, separated by markers, and carrying a \"virtual head\" mark on each track. One simulated move costs <code class=\"mm\">O(k)</code> real moves, so <code class=\"mm\">t</code> simulated steps cost <code class=\"mm\">O(k·t²)</code>.</li><li>A deterministic machine simulates a nondeterministic one by a breadth-first search over the tree of configurations (equivalently, by a 3-tape machine that enumerates and tests). This is slower — exponential in the worst case — but it recognises the same language.</li><li>So every variant in the options recognises exactly the r.e. languages, and only the options that claim a strictly larger class are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both claim extra recognition power. The fold construction and the multi-tape simulation above show there is none.</li><li>(D) confuses <i>nondeterminism</i> with extra power. Nondeterminism gives at most an exponential speed-up and is conjectured not to enlarge the class at all: a deterministic machine can search the whole computation tree, so it still accepts only r.e. languages.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> \"More hardware means more power.\" The model is already maximal: every reasonable extension is simulated with a slowdown, and the class of accepted languages never grows. Extra tapes buy time, not languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Variants of the Turing machine are equivalent <i>in recognition power</i>; the Church&ndash;Turing thesis is what makes this the definition of \"computable\" rather than a coincidence.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A Turing machine has <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 8</span> states, tape alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Γ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|Γ| = 5</span> (the blank is included in <span class=\"mm\">Γ</span>) and input alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ ⊂ Γ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|Σ| = 3</span>. Its transition function is the partial function <span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L, R}</span>. How many ordered pairs belong to the domain <span class=\"mm\">Q × Γ</span>, that is, what is the largest number of entries the transition table can have?</p>", "options": ["13", "40", "24", "120"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The domain of <code class=\"mm\">δ</code> is the Cartesian product <span class=\"mm\">Q × Γ</span>: one entry for <i>every</i> (state, tape symbol) pair, whether or not that pair is reachable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of states: <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 8</span>.</li><li>Number of tape symbols: <span class=\"mm\">|Γ| = 5</span> — this is the <b>tape</b> alphabet, so it already contains the blank and every input symbol.</li><li>Size of the domain: <span class=\"mm\">|Q| × |Γ| = 8 × 5 = 40</span>.</li><li>The input alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span> plays no part in this count; it only restricts which symbols may appear in the initial tape contents.</li><li>Each of the 40 pairs may be left undefined, which is why <code class=\"mm\">δ</code> is a partial function and why 40 is a maximum.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 40 entries.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>13 is <span class=\"mm\">8 + 5</span>, adding the two sizes instead of multiplying them.</li><li>24 is <span class=\"mm\">8 × 3</span>: it uses the <b>input</b> alphabet and forgets the blank and the auxiliary tape symbols.</li><li>120 is <span class=\"mm\">8 × 5 × 3</span>: it multiplies in the three head actions, but the action is the <i>value</i> of the entry, not part of its key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">|Σ|</span> where <span class=\"mm\">|Γ|</span> is meant. A machine may write any tape symbol, including the blank, on any cell, so the table is indexed by the tape alphabet.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A Turing machine is a finite object: <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · |Γ|</span> is finite, so Turing machines can be enumerated and encoded as strings — which is exactly what the undecidability proofs in this unit exploit.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Some textbook definitions allow the head to stay where it is, giving <span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L, R, S}</span>. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["The stay-put option can be removed without changing the accepted language, at the cost of at most one extra state and a constant factor more moves.", "Allowing the head to stay strictly increases the class of languages accepted.", "A machine that uses the stay-put option can accept a language that is not recursively enumerable.", "Allowing the head to stay makes the machine equivalent to a two-way finite automaton."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A \"stay\" move is not a new kind of power: it is a write followed by a move right and a move back left. Any machine using <code class=\"mm\">S</code> can be rewritten to use only <code class=\"mm\">L</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R</code> with a bounded overhead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Suppose a move is <span class=\"mm\">δ(p, a) = (q, b, S)</span>. Replace it by two moves through a fresh state <span class=\"mm\">p′</span>: first <span class=\"mm\">δ(p, a) = (p′, b, R)</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">δ(p′, c) = (q, c, L)</span> for every symbol <span class=\"mm\">c</span>.</li><li>The fresh state <span class=\"mm\">p′</span> only ever moves left and changes nothing, so one extra state per machine is enough; the construction can even be made uniform with a fixed number of states.</li><li>Each original move becomes two moves, so a computation of <span class=\"mm\">t</span> moves becomes one of at most <span class=\"mm\">2t</span> moves. The language accepted is unchanged because the sequence of tape writes and the sequence of visited cells are the same.</li><li>Therefore <code class=\"mm\">S</code> is a normal-form abbreviation, not an extension.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (C) both claim extra recognition power. The machine is simulated exactly, so it accepts the same language; and no Turing-machine variant accepts a non-r.e. language.</li><li>(D) confuses the machine with a finite automaton. A two-way finite automaton has no tape to write on, so it accepts only regular languages, whereas a machine using <code class=\"mm\">S</code> still writes and reads an unbounded tape.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that any change to the move set changes the model's power. The test is always the same: can a standard machine simulate the variant, and can the variant simulate a standard machine? If both answers are yes, the classes coincide.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A Turing machine is characterised up to simulation. Fixed numbers of extra states, extra tapes or extra move options never matter; <i>unbounded</i> resources such as tape length do.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following machines accept <b>exactly</b> the recursively enumerable languages?</p>", "options": ["A deterministic Turing machine whose tape is infinite in both directions.", "A Turing machine with one read-only input tape and two work tapes.", "A deterministic Turing machine whose tape is restricted to at most 1000 cells.", "A nondeterministic Turing machine with a single tape."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A model accepts exactly the r.e. languages when it can simulate a standard TM and can itself be simulated by one. A bounded tape breaks the second half: finite memory plus finitely many states makes the machine a finite automaton.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> A two-way infinite tape is simulated on a one-way tape by folding, and the one-way tape is a special case of the two-way tape, so the two models are mutually simulable.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> A read-only input tape plus work tapes is the standard \"off-line\" Turing machine. It is simulated by an ordinary multi-tape machine (copy the input first), and it simulates one by never writing on the input tape.</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> With at most 1000 cells and a fixed state set the number of configurations is finite — at most <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · 1000 · 3¹⁰⁰⁰</span> for <span class=\"mm\">|Q|</span> states and a 3-symbol alphabet — so the machine is a finite-state device and accepts only a regular language.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> Nondeterminism is removed by a breadth-first search over configurations, so the accepted class is unchanged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (C) fails.</b> A machine with a fixed finite tape and finitely many states has finitely many configurations, and a computation is a path in a finite graph; the accepted language is therefore regular. Unbounded tape is the ingredient that separates a Turing machine from a finite automaton.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"restricted to 1000 cells\" as \"a linear-bounded automaton\". An LBA's tape is bounded by the <i>input length</i> <span class=\"mm\">n</span>, so the bound grows with the input; a fixed bound of 1000 cells does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The three ingredients of the TM model are an unbounded tape, a finite control and a head. Drop the unbounded tape and you get a finite automaton; keep it and every other variant is equivalent.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A linear-bounded automaton is a Turing machine whose tape is restricted to the input cells. Consider a particular machine whose tape is restricted to exactly <b>4</b> cells, with <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 3</span> states, tape alphabet <span class=\"mm\">|Γ| = 3</span> (the blank included, so a cell may hold a blank) and one head that always stands on one of the 4 cells. Treating a configuration as the triple (state, head position, tape contents), what is the largest possible number of distinct configurations of this machine?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "972", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A configuration is fixed by three independent facts: which state the machine is in, where the head is, and what each cell holds. Because the tape is bounded, all three ranges are finite, so the number of configurations is a finite product — and that finiteness is exactly why an LBA can be decided by brute force.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States: <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 3</span> choices.</li><li>Head positions: 4 choices, since the tape has exactly 4 cells.</li><li>Tape contents: each of the 4 cells independently holds one of the 3 tape symbols, so <span class=\"mm\">|Γ|⁴ = 3⁴ = 81</span>.</li><li>Multiply the independent choices: <span class=\"mm\">3 × 4 × 81 = 972</span>.</li><li>If the machine ran for more than 972 moves it would have to repeat a configuration, and a repeated configuration means an infinite loop — so acceptance is decidable by simulating at most 972 moves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 972 configurations.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>How the answer was checked.</b> By direct evaluation: <span class=\"mm\">3⁴ = 81</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">3 × 4 × 81 = 972</span>. The same argument with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> cells gives <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · n · |Γ|ⁿ</span>, which is finite for every fixed tape length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the 3 states and the 4 head positions, or forgetting that the blank is a legal cell content. Every cell may independently be blank, so the blank belongs in the <span class=\"mm\">|Γ|⁴</span> factor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bounded space plus finite control gives finitely many configurations. This single counting fact yields the decidability of LBA acceptance and of context-sensitive membership — and it is why the Chomsky hierarchy stops being decidable only at Type 0.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be a language over a finite alphabet and let <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> be its complement. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursively enumerable then <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is recursively enumerable.", "Every recursively enumerable language is recursive.", "If both <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> are recursively enumerable then <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive.", "If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive then <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is not recursively enumerable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recursive means \"there is a decider — an algorithm that always halts with yes or no\". Recursively enumerable means \"there is a recogniser — an algorithm that halts with yes on members and may run forever on non-members\". Two recognisers, one for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and one for <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span>, combine into a decider by running them in parallel.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Assume <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recognised by <span class=\"mm\">M₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> by <span class=\"mm\">M₂</span>.</li><li>On input <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, run <span class=\"mm\">M₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">M₂</span> alternately, one step each, in a dovetail.</li><li>Every string lies in exactly one of <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span>, so exactly one of the two machines eventually says yes.</li><li>When it does, halt and answer accordingly. The combined procedure always halts, so <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive.</li><li>Conversely, if <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive then <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is recursive too (swap the accepting and rejecting states), so both are r.e. Hence \"both r.e.\" and \"recursive\" coincide for a language and its complement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: the halting set <span class=\"mm\">K = {⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w}</span> is r.e. while its complement is not r.e. at all.</li><li>(B) is false: <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is the standard example of an r.e. language that is not recursive.</li><li>(D) is false: every recursive language is r.e. — a decider is in particular a recogniser.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking r.e. and recursive are the same because both have \"an algorithm\". The difference is whether the algorithm must halt on <i>every</i> input. A recogniser may loop forever on strings outside the language, and that is precisely the gap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <span class=\"mm\">Recursive ⊂ RE</span>, and the inclusion is strict. Equivalently, <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive iff both <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> are r.e.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following classes of languages is <b>NOT</b> closed under complementation?</p>", "options": ["The recursive languages", "The recursively enumerable languages", "The regular languages", "The context-sensitive languages"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Closure under complement is a property of the <i>decider</i>, not of the recogniser. If a class has a machine that always halts, complementing is free: swap the accept and reject outcomes. If a class only has machines that may loop, there is nothing to swap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Recursive:</b> given a decider <span class=\"mm\">M</span> for <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that runs <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and reverses the answer. It still always halts, so <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is recursive. Closed.</li><li><b>Regular:</b> a DFA is total, so swapping final and non-final states gives a DFA for the complement. Closed.</li><li><b>Context-sensitive:</b> closed by the Immerman&ndash;Szelepcsényi theorem, since CSL <span class=\"mm\">=</span> NSPACE<span class=\"mm\">(n)</span> and nondeterministic space classes with a space-constructible bound are closed under complement. Closed.</li><li><b>Recursively enumerable:</b> <i>not</i> closed. The halting set <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is r.e. by direct simulation, but <span class=\"mm\">K̄</span> is not r.e. — if it were, <span class=\"mm\">K</span> would be recursive by the dovetail argument, contradicting the undecidability of halting.</li><li>Only the r.e. class fails, so (B) is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the recursively enumerable languages.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the others are closed.</b> All three are classes with total machines, or with a space bound that makes the configuration graph finite, so a complement can be decided by swapping the outcome or by exhaustive search.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every class in the Chomsky hierarchy is closed under complement because the regular and context-free cases are handled in the same chapter. Context-free languages are <i>not</i> closed under complement (and not under intersection), yet the larger class of context-sensitive languages <i>is</i> — the hierarchy is not monotone in its closure properties.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Complement closure fails exactly where the machine may not halt. That is the operational meaning of the recursive / r.e. divide.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For a language <span class=\"mm\">L</span> over a finite alphabet, which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> are both recursively enumerable, then <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive.", "If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive, then <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursively enumerable.", "If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is r.e. but not recursive, then <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is not r.e.", "If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not r.e., then <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is r.e."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three facts organise this question: recursive <span class=\"mm\">⊂</span> r.e.; \"both r.e.\" <span class=\"mm\">⟺</span> recursive; and a language that is r.e. but not recursive has a complement that is not r.e. Statement (D) is the only one that goes wrong, because a language can be neither r.e. nor co-r.e.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> Dovetail the two recognisers: exactly one of them accepts <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, so the combined machine always halts. That is a decider for <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> A decider is a recogniser that happens to halt on every input, so every recursive language is r.e.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> If <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> were also r.e. then (A) would make <span class=\"mm\">L</span> recursive, contradicting the hypothesis. So <span class=\"mm\">L̄</span> is not r.e.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> Take the join <span class=\"mm\">J = {0x : x ∈ K} ∪ {1x : x ∉ K}</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is the halting set. The join is r.e. exactly when <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is, so <span class=\"mm\">J</span> is not r.e.; and its complement is not r.e. either. Hence \"not r.e.\" does not force the complement to be r.e.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why (D) fails.</b> The four possibilities are: recursive (both r.e.), r.e. only (like <span class=\"mm\">K</span>), co-r.e. only (like <span class=\"mm\">K̄</span>), and neither (like the join above). \"Not r.e.\" covers the last two, and only in the co-r.e. case is the complement r.e.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing every language is either r.e. or co-r.e. Most languages are neither: there are uncountably many languages but only countably many Turing machines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> \"Recursive\" is a decision procedure; \"r.e.\" is a recognition procedure. Everything in this unit is a consequence of that one distinction.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the following five languages, where <span class=\"mm\">M</span> ranges over all Turing machines, <span class=\"mm\">G</span> over all context-free grammars and <span class=\"mm\">w</span> over all strings.<br/>(i) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w within 100 steps}</span><br/>(ii) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅}</span><br/>(iii) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨G⟩ : G is a context-free grammar and L(G) = ∅}</span><br/>(iv) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M accepts ε}</span><br/>(v) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on every input}</span><br/>How many of these five languages are <b>recursive</b>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language is recursive when a <i>total</i> algorithm decides it. A bounded simulation or a finite fixed-point computation is total; anything that needs to know how a machine behaves on all inputs is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(i) recursive.</b> Simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> for exactly 100 steps and report whether it halted. The simulation is bounded, so the procedure always halts — this is the standard \"bounded halting is decidable\" fact.</li><li><b>(ii) not recursive.</b> <span class=\"mm\">L(M) = ∅</span> is a non-trivial property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, so Rice's theorem applies. Intuitively, to say yes you would have to prove that no input is ever accepted, which needs unbounded search.</li><li><b>(iii) recursive.</b> Emptiness of a context-free language is decidable: compute the set of productive nonterminals by a fixed-point iteration, or test whether the start symbol can derive any terminal string. Always terminates.</li><li><b>(iv) not recursive.</b> It is r.e. — just run <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> — but not recursive: deciding whether <span class=\"mm\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> is undecidable by reduction from the halting problem.</li><li><b>(v) not recursive.</b> <span class=\"mm\">M halts on every input</span> is a non-trivial property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, undecidable by Rice's theorem; it is not even r.e.</li><li>Counting: exactly (i) and (iii) are recursive, so the answer is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>How the answer was checked.</b> Each of the five is settled by a known result rather than by intuition: bounded halting is decidable by the 100-step clock; CFG emptiness is decidable by the productivity fixed point; (ii), (iv) and (v) are three different undecidable problems, two of them by Rice's theorem and one by reduction from halting.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Marking (i) undecidable \"because halting is undecidable\". The undecidability of halting concerns <i>unbounded</i> running time; once the time budget is fixed the problem becomes a finite simulation. Conversely, marking (iv) decidable because it looks like a single test on one string — the test may not terminate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bounded questions are decidable; unbounded questions about the whole behaviour of a machine are not. That single criterion answers most questions in this unit.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> be recursively enumerable languages over <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span> such that <span class=\"mm\">A ∪ B = Σ*</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A ∩ B = ∅</span>. Which one of the following <b>must</b> hold?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> are both recursive.", "<span class=\"mm\">A</span> is regular.", "<span class=\"mm\">A</span> is recursively enumerable but need not be recursive.", "At least one of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is finite."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Disjointness together with <span class=\"mm\">A ∪ B = Σ*</span> makes the two languages exact complements of each other. A language whose complement is also r.e. is recursive — so both parts are recursive, whatever they look like.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Since <span class=\"mm\">A ∪ B = Σ*</span>, every string is in <span class=\"mm\">A</span> or in <span class=\"mm\">B</span>.</li><li>Since <span class=\"mm\">A ∩ B = ∅</span>, no string is in both. Together the two facts give <span class=\"mm\">B = Σ* \\ A = Ā</span>.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is r.e. (given) and <span class=\"mm\">Ā</span> is r.e. (given). Dovetail the two recognisers: on any <span class=\"mm\">w</span> exactly one of them accepts, so the combined procedure always halts.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is recursive, and <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, being its complement, is recursive as well.</li><li>No regularity and no finiteness follows: take <span class=\"mm\">A</span> to be any recursive non-regular language, for instance <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> its complement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) fails with <span class=\"mm\">A = {aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span>, which is recursive but not regular.</li><li>(C) fails because the extra hypothesis \"the complement is r.e.\" is exactly what rules out the r.e.-but-not-recursive case.</li><li>(D) fails with the same example: both <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> are infinite.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using only <span class=\"mm\">A ∪ B = Σ*</span> and concluding \"one of them must be the whole thing\". Disjointness and covering together are much stronger than either alone: they say the two languages <i>split</i> <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> into a decidable pair.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A partition of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> into two r.e. parts is automatically a partition into two recursive parts. This is the standard route for showing that a language is recursive: exhibit recognisers for both it and its complement.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following languages is <b>recursive</b> (decidable)?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) = Σ*}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on every input}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w within 1000 steps}</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language is decidable when there is a procedure that always halts. The only option with a built-in clock is (D): once the number of steps is bounded, the simulation is a finite, terminating computation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Decider for (D): on input <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, decode the pair, then simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> for at most 1000 steps. If the machine halts inside the budget, accept; otherwise reject.</li><li>The simulation takes at most 1000 steps of work, so the decider halts on every input. Hence (D) is recursive.</li><li>Option (A) is the halting problem itself, undecidable by the diagonal argument.</li><li>Option (C) asks whether a machine halts on <i>all</i> inputs, a non-trivial property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, undecidable by Rice's theorem (indeed not even r.e.).</li><li>Option (B) is also a non-trivial property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, undecidable by Rice's theorem.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the canonical undecidable problem: the diagonal machine differs from every machine on its own code.</li><li>(B) and (C) are semantic properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> that hold for some machines and fail for others, so Rice's theorem applies.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> within 1000 steps\" as a special case of \"halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>\". The bound is what makes the difference: with a bound the search space is finite, without one it is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bounded simulation is always decidable. Undecidability enters only when a procedure would have to run an unbounded computation to answer \"no\".</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">To prove that a language <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is undecidable by reduction from the halting problem <span class=\"mm\">HALT = {⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w}</span>, which one of the following must be constructed?</p>", "options": ["A computable function <span class=\"mm\">f</span> such that <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⟺ f(⟨M, w⟩) ∈ L</span> for every pair <span class=\"mm\">(M, w)</span>.", "A computable function <span class=\"mm\">f</span> such that <span class=\"mm\">x ∈ L ⟺ f(x) ∈ HALT</span> for every string <span class=\"mm\">x</span>.", "A Turing machine that decides <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.", "An enumeration of all the strings in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A reduction from <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> to <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is a computable map that <i>translates</i> questions about halting into questions about <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, preserving the answer. If such a map exists and <span class=\"mm\">L</span> were decidable, halting would be decidable too — a contradiction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>What must be built: a computable <span class=\"mm\">f</span> with <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⟺ f(⟨M, w⟩) ∈ L</span>. Then a decider for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> composed with <span class=\"mm\">f</span> would decide <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span>, which is impossible.</li><li>In practice <span class=\"mm\">f</span> is described by a construction: on input <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build the description of a new machine <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that behaves as required, and output <span class=\"mm\">⟨M′⟩</span>.</li><li>This direction — from the known-undecidable problem <i>to</i> the new one — is what transfers undecidability.</li><li>Option (B) is the opposite direction, <span class=\"mm\">L ≤m HALT</span>. It only shows that <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is no harder than halting, i.e. that <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is r.e.; every decidable language reduces to <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> in that direction, so nothing is proved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the classic direction error: <span class=\"mm\">L ≤m HALT</span> shows only <span class=\"mm\">L ∈ RE</span>.</li><li>(C) would <i>prove</i> <span class=\"mm\">L</span> decidable, the opposite of the goal — and it cannot exist.</li><li>(D) shows only that <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is r.e., which is far weaker than decidability.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Getting the arrow backwards. Remember the mnemonic: to prove <span class=\"mm\">L</span> hard, reduce a <i>known hard</i> problem <i>to</i> <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, so that solving <span class=\"mm\">L</span> would solve the hard one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> means \"A is at most as hard as B\". Reductions therefore flow from the problem already known to be hard to the problem to be proved hard.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following problems are <b>undecidable</b>?</p>", "options": ["Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halt on the empty string?", "Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and a state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> of <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> ever enter the state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> when started on the empty string?", "Given a DFA <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">A</span> accept at least one string?", "Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> ever write the symbol 1 on its tape when started on the empty string?"], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each option asks about the unbounded behaviour of a machine, except (C), which asks about a finite automaton — and finite automata can be decided by graph search. The three Turing-machine questions are undecidable, each by a small reduction from halting.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) undecidable.</b> HALT reduces to it: given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that first writes <span class=\"mm\">w</span> on its tape and then simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span>. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> iff <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>.</li><li><b>(B) undecidable.</b> Given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and enters a fresh state <span class=\"mm\">q</span> only when that simulation halts. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> enters <span class=\"mm\">q</span> iff <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>.</li><li><b>(C) decidable.</b> Do a reachability search from the start state of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> over its finitely many states, and accept iff some final state is reachable. The graph is finite, so the search terminates.</li><li><b>(D) undecidable.</b> Given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and writes 1 only when the simulation halts. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> ever writes 1 iff <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why (C) survives.</b> A DFA has finitely many states, so \"is a final state reachable?\" is a finite graph question with a terminating algorithm. Emptiness, finiteness and universality of a DFA are decidable for the same reason.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a \"simple\" question about a Turing machine must be decidable. \"Does it ever enter state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>?\" and \"does it ever write 1?\" look local, but the answer may depend on an unbounded computation, and unbounded is what makes it undecidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undecidability is about the <i>quantifier structure</i>: a question of the form \"does there exist a time <span class=\"mm\">t</span> at which ...\" over an unbounded <span class=\"mm\">t</span> is undecidable, while the same question over a finite object is decidable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the deterministic Turing machine with states <span class=\"mm\">q₀, q₁, q₂, q₃, q_f</span>, tape alphabet <span class=\"mm\">{0, 1, X, Y, ␣}</span> (␣ is the blank) and the transition function below. <span class=\"mm\">X</span> and <span class=\"mm\">Y</span> are \"crossed-off\" marks; every pair not listed sends the machine to the halting reject state <span class=\"mm\">q_r</span>.<br/><code class=\"mm\">δ(q₀,0)=(q₁,X,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₀,Y)=(q₃,Y,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₁,0)=(q₁,0,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₁,Y)=(q₁,Y,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₁,1)=(q₂,Y,L)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">δ(q₂,0)=(q₂,0,L)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₂,Y)=(q₂,Y,L)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₂,X)=(q₀,X,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₃,Y)=(q₃,Y,R)</code> &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₃,␣)=(q_f,␣,R)</code><br/>The head starts on the leftmost cell, which holds the first input symbol. Started on the input <span class=\"mm\">0011</span>, how many transitions (applications of <code class=\"mm\">δ</code>) does the machine make before it halts?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "13", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The machine is the standard \"cross off one 0 at the left and one 1 at the right\" decider for <span class=\"mm\">{0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span>. Counting its moves is a matter of walking the head across the tape; nothing has to be guessed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write the tape as <span class=\"mm\">0 0 1 1 ␣ ␣ …</span> with the head on the first cell, and run the table literally.</li><li>Moves 1&ndash;3: <code class=\"mm\">(q₀,0)→(q₁,X,R)</code> crosses the first 0; <code class=\"mm\">(q₁,0)→(q₁,0,R)</code> skips the second 0; <code class=\"mm\">(q₁,1)→(q₂,Y,L)</code> crosses the second 1 and turns left. Tape: <span class=\"mm\">X 0 Y 1</span>.</li><li>Moves 4&ndash;5: the head walks left over the 0 and the <span class=\"mm\">X</span> — <code class=\"mm\">(q₂,0)→(q₂,0,L)</code> then <code class=\"mm\">(q₂,X)→(q₀,X,R)</code> — so <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> now faces the first uncrossed 0.</li><li>Moves 6&ndash;10: the second pass crosses the remaining 0 (move 6), skips the crossed <span class=\"mm\">Y</span> (move 7), crosses the last 1 (move 8), then walks left over <span class=\"mm\">Y</span> and <span class=\"mm\">X</span> (moves 9&ndash;10). Tape: <span class=\"mm\">X X Y Y</span>.</li><li>Moves 11&ndash;13: <code class=\"mm\">(q₀,Y)→(q₃,Y,R)</code> moves to the check phase, <code class=\"mm\">(q₃,Y)→(q₃,Y,R)</code> skips the second <span class=\"mm\">Y</span>, and <code class=\"mm\">(q₃,␣)→(q_f,␣,R)</code> reads the blank and halts. Total: <b>13</b> transitions.</li><li>Cross-check with the closed form: this machine needs <span class=\"mm\">2n² + 2n + 1</span> moves on <span class=\"mm\">0ⁿ1ⁿ</span>, giving 5, 13 and 25 for n = 1, 2, 3, and the trace above reproduces 13 exactly.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13 transitions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>How the answer was checked.</b> The machine was executed step by step and the transition count recorded at each move; the 13 moves above are the complete computation, ending when <code class=\"mm\">δ(q₃,␣)</code> is applied to the blank. The same run on <span class=\"mm\">000111</span> gives 25 moves and on <span class=\"mm\">01</span> gives 5, both matching <span class=\"mm\">2n² + 2n + 1</span>, which confirms the trace is not an off-by-one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <i>cells visited</i> or <i>passes</i> instead of applications of <code class=\"mm\">δ</code>, or forgetting the final transition that reads the blank and enters <span class=\"mm\">q_f</span>. Every application of <code class=\"mm\">δ</code> counts as one move, including the one that halts the machine.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A Turing machine is a completely explicit object: given the table, the computation and its length are determined. This is why \"does it halt within <span class=\"mm\">k</span> steps\" is decidable — the trace is computable — while \"does it halt at all\" is not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">≤m</span> denote mapping reducibility. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b> for all languages <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span>?</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is undecidable, then <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is undecidable.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is r.e., then <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is r.e.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is decidable, then <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is undecidable.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is undecidable, then <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is undecidable."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> means: there is a computable <span class=\"mm\">f</span> with <span class=\"mm\">x ∈ A ⟺ f(x) ∈ B</span>. So <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is <i>at most</i> as hard as <span class=\"mm\">B</span>. Hardness therefore flows backwards along the arrow, and decidability flows forwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Assume <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> via <span class=\"mm\">f</span>.</li><li>If <span class=\"mm\">B</span> were decidable with decider <span class=\"mm\">N</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">N ∘ f</span> would decide <span class=\"mm\">A</span>: compute <span class=\"mm\">f(x)</span>, then run <span class=\"mm\">N</span>. Since <span class=\"mm\">f</span> is computable and <span class=\"mm\">N</span> always halts, the composite always halts.</li><li>So decidability of <span class=\"mm\">B</span> gives decidability of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>. In contrapositive form: if <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is undecidable then <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is undecidable. That is statement (D).</li><li>The same argument with \"recognisable\" in place of \"decidable\" shows: if <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is r.e. then <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is r.e.</li><li>The converses all fail, because an easy language reduces to a hard one: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> reduces to every language, including the undecidable ones.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the arrow. <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> says nothing about <span class=\"mm\">A</span> when <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is the hard one: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> reduces to any language, yet <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is decidable.</li><li>(B) has the same defect: r.e.-ness of the <i>left</i> side does not transfer to the right side.</li><li>(C) contradicts the transfer of decidability proved above.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <span class=\"mm\">A ≤m B</span> as \"equally hard\". It is a one-way statement: the left side is <i>no harder</i> than the right. Hardness of <span class=\"mm\">B</span> says nothing about <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To prove <span class=\"mm\">B</span> undecidable, reduce a known undecidable language <i>to</i> <span class=\"mm\">B</span>. To prove <span class=\"mm\">A</span> decidable, reduce <span class=\"mm\">A</span> <i>to</i> a known decidable language. Both are applications of the single composition rule above.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">By Rice's theorem, which one of the following languages is <b>undecidable</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M has at least three states}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : the transition table of M contains the symbol 0}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) is regular}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on ε within 100 steps}</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rice's theorem: every non-trivial <i>semantic</i> property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is undecidable. Non-trivial means some r.e. language has the property and some does not; semantic means the property depends only on which strings the machine accepts, not on how the machine is built.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Check the four options against the two conditions.</li><li>(A) and (B) are <b>syntactic</b>: they are decided by inspecting the description of <span class=\"mm\">M</span> — count its states, scan its table. Both are decidable.</li><li>(D) is <b>bounded</b>: simulate for 100 steps and report. Decidable.</li><li>(C) is <b>semantic</b> — it speaks only of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> — and <b>non-trivial</b>: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is regular, while <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span> is not. Rice's theorem therefore makes it undecidable.</li><li>So exactly one option is undecidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are decidable.</b> Each is answered by a finite inspection: count the states, search the transition table, or run a clocked simulation. None requires knowing the language the machine accepts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying Rice's theorem to any question about Turing machines. It applies only to properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. \"Has three states\" and \"contains the symbol 0\" are properties of the <i>program text</i>, and program text is a finite string that can be examined directly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The test is always: does the property change if I replace <span class=\"mm\">M</span> by an equivalent machine? If yes, it is semantic; if it is also non-trivial, it is undecidable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Rice's theorem applies only to properties of the <i>language</i> <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and only to non-trivial ones. To which one of the following languages does Rice's theorem <b>NOT</b> apply?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M has an even number of states}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) is infinite}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) contains at least one palindrome}</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rice's theorem is a statement about properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. A property of the machine's description — its number of states, its alphabet, the length of its encoding — is outside its scope, and such properties are usually decidable outright.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) counts the states of <span class=\"mm\">M</span>. Two machines accepting the same language may have different state counts, and an equivalent machine can always be padded with unreachable states. So the property is <b>not</b> semantic, and Rice's theorem says nothing about it.</li><li>In fact (A) is decidable: read the description, count the states, test parity.</li><li>Options (B), (C) and (D) are each properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> alone, and each is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> satisfies (B) but not (C); <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> satisfies (C) but not (B); and <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> satisfies (D) while <span class=\"mm\">{ab}</span> does not.</li><li>So Rice's theorem applies to (B), (C) and (D) and makes them undecidable, and it does not apply to (A).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the others are covered.</b> Emptiness, infiniteness and \"contains a palindrome\" all speak only about which strings are accepted. Each holds for some machines and fails for others, so each is a non-trivial semantic property and Rice's theorem applies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a syntactic property must be <i>trivial</i>. Syntactic properties can be interesting — \"uses more than 10 states\", \"has a transition on the blank\" — but because the description is a finite string they are all decidable by inspection. Rice's theorem is silent about them, not permissive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Semantic properties are undecidable by Rice; syntactic properties are decidable by inspection. When a question mixes the two, split them before reaching for the theorem.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following are <b>non-trivial properties of the language</b> <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and therefore undecidable by Rice's theorem?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is finite.", "<span class=\"mm\">L(M) = Σ*</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> contains at least one string of length 5.", "<span class=\"mm\">M</span> uses at most 50 tape cells on every input."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A property qualifies for Rice's theorem when (i) it is decided by <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> alone and (ii) it is neither always true nor always false over the r.e. languages. The first three pass both tests; the fourth speaks about the machine's space usage.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) qualifies.</b> Finiteness depends only on <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. Non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is finite, <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is infinite.</li><li><b>(B) qualifies.</b> Non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> has <span class=\"mm\">L(M) = Σ*</span> while <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> has not. Undecidable by Rice's theorem.</li><li><b>(C) qualifies.</b> The set <span class=\"mm\">Σ⁵</span> is fixed and finite (32 strings over a binary alphabet), so \"does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> accept one of them\" is a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. Non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> accepts none, <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> accepts all.</li><li><b>(D) does not qualify.</b> Space usage is a property of the computation, not of the accepted language: two machines accepting the same language can use very different space. It is still undecidable — by a reduction from halting — but not by Rice's theorem.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why (D) is excluded.</b> Rice's theorem is about the <i>extensional</i> object <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. Whether a machine stays within 50 cells is <i>intensional</i>: a wasteful machine and a frugal one may accept exactly the same language. The theorem has no purchase on it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"undecidable\" and \"undecidable by Rice's theorem\" as the same thing. (D) is undecidable, yet Rice's theorem is not the reason; the correct tool is a direct reduction, since a halting computation can be encoded by making the machine write far out on the tape.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read the property literally: if it mentions cells, steps, states or tape usage, Rice does not apply. If it mentions only the set of accepted strings, and some language has it while another does not, it does.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The standard reduction proving that <span class=\"mm\">NE = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) ≠ ∅}</span> is undecidable maps <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> to <span class=\"mm\">⟨M′⟩</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> on input <span class=\"mm\">x</span> ignores <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and accepts if that simulation halts. Which one of the following statements about this construction is correct?</p>", "options": ["It is not a valid reduction, because <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> ignores its input <span class=\"mm\">x</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⟺ L(M′) ≠ ∅</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤m NE</span> and <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> is undecidable.", "<span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⟺ L(M′) = ∅</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤m NE</span>.", "It shows only that <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> is r.e., not that it is undecidable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The construction makes <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> a \"witness machine\": it accepts every input if <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, and no input otherwise. That turns a question about one computation into a question about emptiness of a language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Case 1: <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> accepts every <span class=\"mm\">x</span> it is given, so <span class=\"mm\">L(M′) = Σ*</span>, in particular <span class=\"mm\">L(M′) ≠ ∅</span>.</li><li>Case 2: <span class=\"mm\">M</span> does not halt on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> loops on every input and accepts nothing, so <span class=\"mm\">L(M′) = ∅</span>.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⟺ L(M′) ≠ ∅</span>, which is exactly the biconditional a reduction from <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> to <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> requires. The map is computable: from the descriptions of <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and <span class=\"mm\">w</span> one writes down the description of <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> mechanically.</li><li>If <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> were decidable, composing its decider with this map would decide <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span>, which is impossible. So <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> is undecidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misreads the technique. Ignoring the input is the whole point: it makes acceptance independent of <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, so the only remaining question is whether the simulation of <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> halts.</li><li>(C) has the biconditional inverted. If <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> accepts everything, so <span class=\"mm\">L(M′)</span> is not empty.</li><li>(D) understates the result. The reduction transfers <i>undecidability</i>, not merely r.e.-ness — though <span class=\"mm\">NE</span> does happen to be r.e. as well.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting to check <i>both</i> directions of the biconditional. A reduction must map yes-instances to yes-instances <i>and</i> no-instances to no-instances; case 2 supplies the second half.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The \"ignore the input, simulate, then accept\" gadget is the workhorse of undecidability proofs. Rice's theorem packages it once and for all: any non-trivial property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> can be forced to hold or fail by choosing whether the simulated computation halts.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the following six properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>.<br/>(i) <span class=\"mm\">L(M) = ∅</span><br/>(ii) <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is regular<br/>(iii) <span class=\"mm\">0101 ∈ L(M)</span><br/>(iv) <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is co-finite, that is <span class=\"mm\">Σ* \\ L(M)</span> is finite<br/>(v) <span class=\"mm\">L(M) ⊆ Σ*</span><br/>(vi) <span class=\"mm\">|L(M)| ≤ 3</span><br/>How many of these six are <b>undecidable by Rice's theorem</b>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rice's theorem needs two things of a property: it must be a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and it must be <b>non-trivial</b> — true for at least one r.e. language and false for at least one. Five of the six pass both tests; the exception is trivially true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(i) undecidable.</b> Non-trivial (<span class=\"mm\">∅</span> has it, <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> does not) and semantic. Rice applies.</li><li><b>(ii) undecidable.</b> Regularity depends only on <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>; <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is regular and <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span> is not. Rice applies.</li><li><b>(iii) undecidable.</b> Membership of one fixed string is a semantic property; <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> contains 0101, <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> does not. Rice applies.</li><li><b>(iv) undecidable.</b> Co-finiteness is semantic and non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is co-finite, <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is not. Rice applies.</li><li><b>(v) decidable.</b> Every language over <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span> satisfies <span class=\"mm\">L(M) ⊆ Σ*</span>, so the property is <b>trivial</b> and the language in question is all of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>; a decider simply accepts everything. Rice's theorem requires non-triviality and therefore does not apply.</li><li><b>(vi) undecidable.</b> Bounded cardinality is semantic and non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> satisfies it, <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> does not. Rice applies.</li><li>Counting: (i), (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi) are undecidable by Rice's theorem, and (v) is not. The answer is 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>How the answer was checked.</b> Each of the six was tested against the two conditions rather than judged by feel. For every one of (i), (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi) a witness pair was produced — a language with the property and a language without it — which is exactly the non-triviality certificate Rice's theorem needs. Property (v) has no such pair, because it holds for every language over <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is undecidable by Rice. The theorem excludes the two trivial properties — always true and always false — and those are decidable precisely because a decider can ignore its input. It also excludes properties of the machine rather than of its language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Before invoking Rice, ask: can I name a language with the property and a language without it? If yes, the property is non-trivial and undecidable. If no, it is trivial, and trivially decidable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements about the classes of the Chomsky hierarchy is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every recursive language is context-sensitive.", "Every context-sensitive language is recursive.", "Every recursively enumerable language is context-sensitive.", "The class of context-free languages equals the class of context-sensitive languages."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The Chomsky hierarchy is a chain of strict inclusions: <span class=\"mm\">Regular ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ RE</span>. Each step up adds a more powerful machine, and each step up keeps everything the level below can do.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A context-sensitive language is accepted by a linear-bounded automaton, whose tape is bounded by the input length <span class=\"mm\">n</span>. With <span class=\"mm\">|Q|</span> states and <span class=\"mm\">n</span> cells there are only <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · n · |Γ|ⁿ</span> configurations — finitely many — so a brute-force search of the configuration graph decides membership.</li><li>A total decision procedure therefore exists, so every CSL is recursive. Option (B) is true.</li><li>The inclusion is strict at the top: the halting set <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is r.e. and not recursive, hence not context-sensitive. Option (C) is false.</li><li>The inclusion is strict at the bottom too: there are recursive languages that no non-contracting grammar generates, so some recursive languages are not context-sensitive. Option (A) is false.</li><li>And <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span> is context-sensitive but not context-free, so the two classes differ. Option (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses a strict inclusion: recursion is <i>above</i> context-sensitivity, not below it.</li><li>(C) ignores the top of the hierarchy; Type 0 is strictly larger than Type 1.</li><li>(D) contradicts the standard witness <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span>, which an LBA accepts but no PDA does.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the hierarchy as \"bigger type number, bigger class\" and then assuming every level is decidable. Only Type 0 escapes decidability: Types 1, 2 and 3 are all recursive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <span class=\"mm\">Type 3 ⊂ Type 2 ⊂ Type 1 ⊂ Type 0</span>. Decidability is lost exactly once, between Type 1 and Type 0, when the tape bound is removed.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The class of languages accepted by a linear-bounded automaton is exactly the class of:</p>", "options": ["regular languages", "context-free languages", "recursive languages", "context-sensitive languages"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The Chomsky hierarchy pairs each language class with a machine: finite automaton with regular, pushdown automaton with context-free, linear-bounded automaton with context-sensitive, Turing machine with recursively enumerable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A linear-bounded automaton is a Turing machine whose tape is confined to the input cells. That restriction is exactly what a non-contracting grammar describes: every production either keeps the length or increases it, never shrinks it.</li><li>By the standard equivalence, a language is context-sensitive iff some LBA accepts it — the tape bound on the machine matches the non-shrinking condition on the grammar.</li><li>The class is strictly larger than the context-free languages (<span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span> is accepted by an LBA but by no PDA) and strictly smaller than the recursive languages.</li><li>So the answer is the context-sensitive class.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — the context-sensitive languages.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) belongs to the one-way finite automaton and (B) to the pushdown automaton; both machines have strictly less storage than an LBA.</li><li>(C) is the class of languages decided by a Turing machine with no tape bound; every CSL is recursive, but not conversely.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing \"bounded tape\" with \"no tape\". An LBA can still scan and rewrite its whole input many times; it simply cannot run off the end. That is why it reaches beyond the context-free languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Machine to language class: DFA gives regular, PDA gives context-free, LBA gives context-sensitive, TM gives recursively enumerable.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the Chomsky hierarchy and its machine models are <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every regular language is context-free.", "Every context-free language is recursive.", "The membership problem for context-sensitive languages is decidable.", "Every recursively enumerable language is recursive."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Climbing the hierarchy never loses a language, and the first three levels are decidable. Only Type 0 is undecidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> A DFA is a pushdown automaton that never uses its stack, so every regular language has a PDA and hence a context-free grammar.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> The CYK algorithm decides membership in a context-free language in <span class=\"mm\">O(n³)</span> time given the grammar in Chomsky normal form, and it always halts.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> An LBA has finitely many configurations — at most <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · n · |Γ|ⁿ</span> — so membership is decided by exploring the configuration graph and testing whether an accepting configuration is reachable. The search is finite, hence terminating.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> The halting set is r.e. and not recursive; the inclusion <span class=\"mm\">Recursive ⊂ RE</span> is strict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why (D) fails.</b> If every r.e. language were recursive, the halting problem would be decidable, which the diagonal argument rules out. R.e. means recognisable, not decidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing context-sensitive membership is undecidable because the grammar is complicated. Complexity is not the issue: the configuration space of an LBA is finite, so even a crude search terminates. It may take exponential time, but decidability asks only for termination.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Types 1, 2 and 3 are decidable; Type 0 is not. The dividing line is the tape bound: bounded space makes the configuration graph finite.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following languages is context-sensitive but <b>NOT</b> context-free?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{w wᴿ : w ∈ {a, b}*}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbᵐ : n ≠ m}</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A context-free language is recognised by a pushdown automaton, whose single stack can match one nested pair of counts. Three counts that must all agree need two independent comparisons, which one stack cannot make.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) is context-free: <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | ab</span> generates it, and the stack counts the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s and matches them with the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s.</li><li>Option (B) is context-free: guess the midpoint nondeterministically, then match the second half against the stack in reverse.</li><li>Option (C) is context-free: it is the union <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbᵐ : n > m} ∪ {aⁿbᵐ : n < m}</span>, and context-free languages are closed under union.</li><li>Option (D) is not context-free. By the pumping lemma, pumping any sufficiently long string of <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ}</span> produces a string that must still be in the language; but pumping changes one block while leaving the others fixed, so the three counts disagree.</li><li>It <i>is</i> context-sensitive: the grammar with <span class=\"mm\">S → aSBC | aBC</span>, <span class=\"mm\">CB → BC</span>, <span class=\"mm\">aB → ab</span>, <span class=\"mm\">bB → bb</span>, <span class=\"mm\">bC → bc</span>, <span class=\"mm\">cC → cc</span> is non-contracting, so an LBA accepts the language.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the others are context-free.</b> Each of (A), (B) and (C) can be recognised with one stack: (A) and (B) by matching symbols against the stack, (C) by a nondeterministic union of two one-stack machines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ}</span> is context-free because it \"looks like\" <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span>. Adding a third block that must equal the first two is exactly what one stack cannot do — the stack is popped while reading the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, so no record of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> remains to check the <span class=\"mm\">c</span>'s against.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One stack handles one nesting depth. <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ}</span> is the standard witness separating context-free from context-sensitive, exactly as <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> separates regular from context-free.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the following six languages over <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {a, b, c}</span>.<br/>(i) <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span><br/>(ii) <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span><br/>(iii) <span class=\"mm\">{w w : w ∈ {a, b}*}</span><br/>(iv) <span class=\"mm\">{w wᴿ : w ∈ {a, b}*}</span><br/>(v) <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿdⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span><br/>(vi) <span class=\"mm\">{aⁱbʲcᵏ : i = j or j = k}</span><br/>How many of these six are <b>context-free</b>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pushdown automaton has one stack, so it can enforce one \"counted\" equality. Languages demanding two independent equalities among three or more blocks are outside the class; languages that are a union of two one-equality conditions are inside it, because context-free languages are closed under union.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(i) not context-free.</b> Three blocks must agree; one stack cannot remember <span class=\"mm\">n</span> while checking <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s against it and also check <span class=\"mm\">c</span>'s. This is the standard pumping-lemma counterexample.</li><li><b>(ii) context-free.</b> Generated by <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | ab</span>.</li><li><b>(iii) not context-free.</b> Copy languages need to compare two halves without a marker at the midpoint; a PDA must guess where the middle is, and a wrong guess cannot be recovered from because the stack has been consumed. The pumping lemma confirms it.</li><li><b>(iv) context-free.</b> Generated by <span class=\"mm\">S → aSa | bSb | ε</span>: guess the midpoint nondeterministically and match the second half against the stack in reverse. The reversal is what makes it fit one stack.</li><li><b>(v) not context-free.</b> Four blocks and three independent equalities — even further outside the class.</li><li><b>(vi) context-free.</b> It is <span class=\"mm\">{aⁱbʲcᵏ : i = j} ∪ {aⁱbʲcᵏ : j = k}</span>, a union of two context-free languages, and context-free languages are closed under union.</li><li>Counting: (ii), (iv) and (vi) are context-free, so the answer is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>How the answer was checked.</b> The three members were exhibited by grammar or construction, not merely asserted: <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | ab</span> for (ii), <span class=\"mm\">S → aSa | bSb | ε</span> for (iv), and the union decomposition for (vi). The three non-members each require two independent equalities — (i) and (v) directly, (iii) through the midpoint argument — which one stack cannot supply.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting (iii) <span class=\"mm\">{w w}</span> as context-free because <span class=\"mm\">{w wᴿ}</span> is. The reversal in (iv) is what a stack can do; plain copying is not, since the machine would have to remember the first half in order and compare it forwards, but a stack returns symbols in reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Context-free means \"one counted comparison\". Unions of such conditions stay context-free; conjunctions of two independent ones generally do not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q01", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the standard formulation a Turing machine has a unique start state <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> and two halting states, <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span> and <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>reject</sub></span>, for which no transition is defined. Which one of the following statements about the behaviour of such a machine is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Entering <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>reject</sub></span> does not stop the machine; it keeps moving its head to the right until it reads a blank cell.", "The computation stops as soon as <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span> or <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>reject</sub></span> is entered; a computation that never enters either of them is said to loop on that input.", "A machine that has entered <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span> may leave it again if a transition happens to be defined there.", "Every computation that loops must eventually enter <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>reject</sub></span>, because that is the state that records failure."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A halting state is a state in which the transition function is undefined. Reaching it ends the computation, and the outcome is read off from <i>which</i> halting state was reached. A computation that never reaches a halting state simply never ends: that is what &ldquo;loops&rdquo; means for a Turing machine.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span> and <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>reject</sub></span> are halting states, so <span class=\"mm\">δ</span> has no entry for any pair whose first component is one of them. The machine stops on entry, and accepts exactly when the state is <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span>.</li><li>There is no third outcome. A machine either accepts, rejects, or runs forever; &ldquo;running forever&rdquo; is not a rejection, it is the absence of an answer.</li><li>Option (A) is false because rejection is a halt, not a scan for a blank.</li><li>Option (C) is false because <span class=\"mm\">q<sub>accept</sub></span> is halting: nothing is defined there to take.</li><li>Option (D) is false because a loop is precisely a computation that never enters a halting state.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; entering either halting state ends the computation, and a computation that enters neither loops.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a non-halting computation as a rejection. A machine that loops on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> gives no answer at all; that is why the halting problem is not the same as the acceptance problem, and why an unbounded simulation of a machine can never be turned into a decision procedure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undefined transitions are not errors &mdash; they are the mechanism by which a Turing machine stops. Everything about decidability rests on this: a decider is a machine whose transitions are undefined only after it has produced a yes or a no.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q02", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A universal Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">U</span> takes a pair <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> and simulates the machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on the string <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">U</span> cannot exist, because a Turing machine cannot read the encoding of another machine.", "<span class=\"mm\">U</span> exists, and therefore the halting problem is decidable: run <span class=\"mm\">U</span> on <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> and see whether it stops.", "<span class=\"mm\">U</span> exists only for machines that have a fixed, finite number of states, so it is not really universal.", "<span class=\"mm\">U</span> exists and is an ordinary Turing machine; its existence does not decide the halting problem, because <span class=\"mm\">U</span> itself may run forever on inputs for which <span class=\"mm\">M</span> does."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Machines can be encoded as strings, and one machine can decode and step another. That is the universal machine. Simulation preserves the halting behaviour of the simulated machine, but it does not bound it: a simulator that faithfully follows a looping machine loops too.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Encoding. Every tuple <span class=\"mm\">(Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, q<sub>accept</sub>, q<sub>reject</sub>)</span> is a finite object, so it can be written as a binary string <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩</span>. The pair <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> is likewise a string.</li><li>Simulation. <span class=\"mm\">U</span> keeps the encoding <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩</span> on one part of its tape and the simulated tape on another, and repeats: read the current simulated state and symbol, look up the entry in the encoded table, update. Each simulated move costs a bounded number of moves of <span class=\"mm\">U</span>.</li><li>Consequence for halting. If <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> then <span class=\"mm\">U</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>; if <span class=\"mm\">M</span> loops then <span class=\"mm\">U</span> loops. So <span class=\"mm\">U</span> is a recogniser for the halting problem, not a decider.</li><li>Option (B) is false: a decider must halt on every input, and <span class=\"mm\">U</span> does not.</li><li>Option (C) is false because the encoding is just a string; nothing in the definition restricts what a machine may read.</li><li>Option (D) is false because <span class=\"mm\">U</span> is a single fixed machine, and its own state set is finite whatever the simulated machine is.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; a universal machine exists and simulates any other, but simulating is not deciding.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The most common error is to conclude that universality solves the halting problem. A simulator inherits the loop of the machine it simulates, so &ldquo;run <span class=\"mm\">U</span> and watch&rdquo; is exactly the semi-decision procedure that is known to be incomplete.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Universality is the reason one machine can run another program, and it is also the reason the diagonal argument works: a machine that could decide halting would decide it for <span class=\"mm\">U</span> itself, and that leads straight to a contradiction.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q03", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is an equivalent characterisation of the class of recursively enumerable languages over a finite alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ</span>?</p>", "options": ["Exactly the languages that are the range of some total computable function <span class=\"mm\">f : Σ* → Σ*</span>.", "Exactly the finite languages.", "Exactly the languages whose complement is recursive.", "Exactly the languages that are the domain of some Turing machine that halts on every input."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three descriptions of the same class: recognised by a machine that halts on members, enumerated by a printer, and equal to the range of a total computable function. The third is the least familiar and the most useful, because it removes the machine from the picture.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be the range of a total computable <span class=\"mm\">f</span>. To recognise <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, on input <span class=\"mm\">w</span> compute <span class=\"mm\">f(0), f(1), f(2), …</span> in a dovetail and accept when <span class=\"mm\">w</span> appears. Every member appears at some finite index, so every member is accepted.</li><li>Conversely, let <span class=\"mm\">M</span> recognise <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. Enumerate the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(i, k)</span> in order of <span class=\"mm\">i + k</span>; for each pair run <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on the <span class=\"mm\">i</span>-th string for <span class=\"mm\">k</span> steps and print that string if <span class=\"mm\">M</span> has accepted it. Define <span class=\"mm\">f</span> to return the string printed at step <span class=\"mm\">n</span> (or a fixed string if nothing is printed then). This <span class=\"mm\">f</span> is total and its range is <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.</li><li>Option (B) is false: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is r.e. and infinite.</li><li>Option (C) is false: that describes the recursive languages, a strict subclass.</li><li>Option (D) is false: the domain of a machine that halts on every input is decided by that machine, so it is recursive, not merely r.e.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; the r.e. languages are exactly the ranges of total computable functions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing &ldquo;domain of a machine that always halts&rdquo; with &ldquo;r.e.&rdquo;. Requiring the machine to halt on every input builds a decider, which yields only the recursive languages; for the r.e. class the machine may be undefined outside the language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Range, domain of a partial computable function, and recognition are three faces of one class. When a question phrases an r.e. set as a range, the dovetail enumeration is almost always the proof to use.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q04", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An enumerator is a Turing machine with a printer that writes out strings, one after another. Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? Select all that apply.</p>", "options": ["A language is recursively enumerable exactly when some enumerator prints precisely its members.", "An enumerator for an infinite r.e. language must print its members in increasing order of length.", "If <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is recursive, then some enumerator prints the members of <span class=\"mm\">L</span> in lexicographic order.", "An enumerator for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> also decides <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, because it can be asked whether a given string is eventually printed."], "answer_display": "Options (A and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An enumerator converts &ldquo;is recognised&rdquo; into &ldquo;is printed&rdquo;. The two are equivalent for r.e. languages, but printing is weaker than deciding: an enumerator that never prints <span class=\"mm\">w</span> never tells you so.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> From an enumerator build a recogniser: run it and accept when <span class=\"mm\">w</span> is printed. From a recogniser build an enumerator by dovetailing <span class=\"mm\">M</span> over all strings and all step counts, printing a string whenever some computation accepts it.</li><li><b>(B) false.</b> Nothing in the definition constrains the order. The standard enumerator derived from a recogniser prints in whatever order the dovetail produces, which is by <span class=\"mm\">i + k</span> and not by length.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> A decider for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> can test strings one at a time in lexicographic order (shorter strings first, alphabetically within a length) and print those it accepts. Lexicographic order is a well-order with finitely many predecessors of each string, so the printing runs through the whole list.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> An enumerator gives a semi-decision: if <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> it prints <span class=\"mm\">w</span> eventually, but if <span class=\"mm\">w ∉ L</span> it runs forever and the question is never answered. Deciding needs a machine that halts either way.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that an enumerator decides, or that enumeration must be tidy. The order is free; what a decider additionally needs is a bound on when to stop, which an enumerator never supplies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Enumerability is recognition with the input removed. That is why the two notions coincide for r.e. languages and why neither of them coincides with decidability.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q05", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the four levels of the Chomsky hierarchy: Type 3 (regular), Type 2 (context-free), Type 1 (context-sensitive) and Type 0 (recursively enumerable). For how many of these four classes is the union of any two languages of the class always again a language of the same class?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A class is closed under union when a machine for <span class=\"mm\">L₁ ∪ L₂</span> can be built from machines for <span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> without leaving the class. Each level of the hierarchy has such a construction, because each level has a machine model that can guess which side to follow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Regular.</b> Take the two DFAs, form the product automaton and make a state final when either coordinate is final. Closed.</li><li><b>Context-free.</b> Take grammars <span class=\"mm\">G₁ = (V₁, Σ, P₁, S₁)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">G₂</span> with disjoint nonterminal sets, add a fresh start symbol <span class=\"mm\">S</span> and the productions <span class=\"mm\">S → S₁ | S₂</span>. Closed.</li><li><b>Context-sensitive.</b> Take the non-contracting grammars, rename nonterminals apart, add <span class=\"mm\">S → S₁ | S₂</span>. Every production still satisfies <span class=\"mm\">|α| ≤ |β|</span>. Closed.</li><li><b>Recursively enumerable.</b> Run two recognisers in parallel (dovetail) and accept when either accepts. Closed.</li><li>So all four levels are closed under union: the count is 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 &mdash; all four classes are closed under union.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying the failure of one closure property over to another. The context-free languages are <i>not</i> closed under intersection or complementation, and that fact is often misremembered as a failure of union closure as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Union closure is nearly universal in the hierarchy; intersection and complementation are the properties that separate the levels. When a question asks which level breaks a closure property, expect the answer to be intersection or complement, not union.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q06", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">HALT = {⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on the input w}</span>. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is recursive but not recursively enumerable.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is not recursively enumerable.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is recursively enumerable but not recursive.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> and its complement are both recursively enumerable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recognising is easy and deciding is impossible. A recogniser for <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is the universal machine; a decider would have to answer no for a machine that loops, and no finite amount of simulation can establish that a computation will never end.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Recognisable.</b> On input <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Accept as soon as the simulation halts. Every halting pair is accepted, so <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is r.e.</li><li><b>Not decidable.</b> Suppose a decider <span class=\"mm\">D</span> existed. Build <span class=\"mm\">N</span> which on input <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩</span> runs <span class=\"mm\">D</span> on <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩</span> and then does the opposite of what <span class=\"mm\">M</span> does. Then <span class=\"mm\">N</span> on <span class=\"mm\">⟨N⟩</span> halts exactly when it does not &mdash; a contradiction.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is r.e. and not recursive.</li><li>Option (D) is false: if both a set and its complement were r.e., running the two recognisers in parallel would decide the set, contradicting the previous step.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; recursively enumerable but not recursive.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a long simulation is evidence of non-halting. No finite run can rule out a halt on the next step, so simulation yields a recogniser and never a decider.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The halting problem is the canonical r.e.-but-not-recursive set. Any question that asks for its status should be answered on the two axes separately: recognisable (yes) and decidable (no).</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q07", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Rice&rsquo;s theorem speaks of <i>non-trivial</i> properties of the language <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. Which one of the following is a <b>trivial</b> property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, that is, a property that every recursively enumerable language has?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L(M) = ∅</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">L(M) ⊆ Σ*</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is finite", "<span class=\"mm\">ε ∈ L(M)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A property is trivial when it holds for every r.e. language or for none. Rice&rsquo;s theorem needs non-triviality, because a trivial property is decided by a machine that ignores its input and always answers the same way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every language is a subset of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> by definition, so <span class=\"mm\">L(M) ⊆ Σ*</span> holds for every machine. The property is trivial and the language of encodings satisfying it is <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> itself, which is decidable.</li><li>The other three are true of some r.e. languages and false of others, so each is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅ = ∅</span> but <span class=\"mm\">Σ* ≠ ∅</span>; <span class=\"mm\">{ε}</span> is finite but <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is not; <span class=\"mm\">ε ∈ Σ*</span> but <span class=\"mm\">ε ∉ ∅</span>.</li><li>By Rice&rsquo;s theorem all three of those are undecidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">L(M) ⊆ Σ*</span> is true of every machine, so it is trivial.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating &ldquo;trivial&rdquo; as &ldquo;easy to check&rdquo;. Triviality is about the truth set: a property is trivial when it is satisfied by all r.e. languages or by none, not when it looks simple.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Rice&rsquo;s theorem has two hypotheses: the property must be semantic (about <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>) and non-trivial (neither always true nor always false). A question that offers a universally true property is testing the second hypothesis.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q08", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements is <b>FALSE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every regular language is context-sensitive.", "The class of context-sensitive languages is closed under complementation.", "Membership of a string in a context-free language can be decided in polynomial time in the length of the string.", "Every context-sensitive language is context-free."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The hierarchy is a chain of strict inclusions <span class=\"mm\">Regular ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ RE</span>. Each level is contained in the next, so a statement that reverses one of the inclusions is the false one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Regular languages are accepted by DFAs, which are linear-bounded automata with no tape use, so <span class=\"mm\">Regular ⊆ CSL</span>. (A) is true.</li><li>Context-sensitive languages are closed under complement: this is the Immerman&ndash;Szelepcs&eacute;nyi theorem, proved by counting reachable configurations of the linear-bounded automaton. (B) is true.</li><li>Membership in a context-free language is decided by the CYK algorithm in <span class=\"mm\">O(n³)</span> time, polynomial in <span class=\"mm\">n</span>. (C) is true.</li><li>The inclusion <span class=\"mm\">CFL ⊂ CSL</span> is strict: <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 1}</span> is context-sensitive and not context-free. So (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; context-sensitive languages strictly contain the context-free ones.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the direction of an inclusion. Every context-free language is context-sensitive, but the converse fails; the witness <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ}</span> needs a tape that can compare three counts, which a pushdown store cannot do.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep the chain in one direction: <span class=\"mm\">Regular ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ RE</span>. Any option that asserts the opposite inclusion is the false one, whatever else it says.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q09", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following languages are undecidable <b>by Rice&rsquo;s theorem</b>? Select all that apply.</p>", "options": ["{⟨M⟩ : M has exactly seven states}", "{⟨M⟩ : L(M) is a finite set}", "{⟨M⟩ : the encoding of M contains the substring 101}", "{⟨M⟩ : L(M) contains every string of length at most 5}"], "answer_display": "Options (B and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rice&rsquo;s theorem needs the property to be about <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> and to be non-trivial. A property of the encoding &mdash; how many states, which symbols appear &mdash; is not a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> at all, and is usually decidable by inspection.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) false.</b> The number of states is written into the encoding. Decode and count; no simulation is needed. The property is syntactic, so Rice&rsquo;s theorem does not apply, and the language is decidable.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> Finiteness is a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. It is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> is finite, <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is not. Both are r.e. languages, so the property is neither always true nor always false, and Rice&rsquo;s theorem applies.</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> Containing the substring 101 is a property of the encoding string. It is checked by scanning that string.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> Containing all strings of length at most 5 is a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. It is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> has it, <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> does not. Rice&rsquo;s theorem applies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying Rice&rsquo;s theorem to the machine instead of its language. &ldquo;Has seven states&rdquo; and &ldquo;contains 101 in its encoding&rdquo; are facts about the description, and the description is a finite object that can be read directly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Before invoking Rice, ask two questions: is the property about <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and does some r.e. language have it while another does not? If either answer is no, Rice gives nothing.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q10", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> be a recursive language, let <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> be recursively enumerable but not recursive, and set <span class=\"mm\">L₃ = L̄₂</span> (the complement of <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> in <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>) and <span class=\"mm\">L₄ = L₂ ∪ L₃</span>. How many of the four languages <span class=\"mm\">L₁, L₂, L₃, L₄</span> are recursive?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The class of recursive languages is closed under complement, union and intersection; the class of r.e. languages is not closed under complement. That single asymmetry settles every one of the four cases.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> is recursive by hypothesis. That is one.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> is r.e. and not recursive by hypothesis. That is not one.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L₃ = L̄₂</span>. If <span class=\"mm\">L₃</span> were recursive, its complement <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> would also be recursive, because the recursive languages are closed under complement. So <span class=\"mm\">L₃</span> is not recursive. (Equivalently: <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> r.e. and not recursive means <span class=\"mm\">L̄₂</span> is not even r.e.)</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L₄ = L₂ ∪ L̄₂ = Σ*</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> is decided by the machine that accepts everything. That is one.</li><li>The count is therefore <span class=\"mm\">1 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 2</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 &mdash; only <span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L₄</span> are recursive.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the complement of a non-recursive language to be non-recursive for the same reason, or counting <span class=\"mm\">L₄</span> as non-recursive because it is built from <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span>. The union of a set and its complement is the whole alphabet, which is trivially decidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A set and its complement are recursive together or not at all. The moment you see <span class=\"mm\">L ∪ L̄</span> or <span class=\"mm\">L ∩ L̄</span>, evaluate it: the first is <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> and the second is <span class=\"mm\">∅</span>, and both are decidable whatever <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q11", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A grammar is context-sensitive when every production <span class=\"mm\">α → β</span> satisfies <span class=\"mm\">|α| ≤ |β|</span>, with the single usual exception that <span class=\"mm\">S → ε</span> is allowed when <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is the start symbol and does not appear on any right-hand side. Which one of the following productions is <b>allowed</b> in a context-sensitive grammar?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">ABC → AB</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A → ε</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">AB → BA</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">AB → A</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The condition is purely length-based: no production may shorten the sentential form. A production with two symbols on the left needs at least two on the right, and the special empty production is reserved for the start symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <span class=\"mm\">|ABC| = 3</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|AB| = 2</span>. The form shrinks, so it is not allowed.</li><li>Option (B): <span class=\"mm\">|A| = 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|ε| = 0</span>. It shrinks, and <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is not declared to be the start symbol, so it is not allowed.</li><li>Option (C): <span class=\"mm\">|AB| = 2</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|BA| = 2</span>. The lengths are equal, so the production is allowed. It is a genuine context-sensitive production: it swaps two adjacent nonterminals, which is how a context-sensitive grammar sorts a string without ever shortening it.</li><li>Option (D): <span class=\"mm\">|AB| = 2</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|A| = 1</span>. It shrinks, so it is not allowed.</li><li>Exactly one option satisfies <span class=\"mm\">|α| ≤ |β|</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">AB → BA</span> keeps the length at 2, so it is allowed.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the definition as a requirement on the <i>form</i> of the left side, for example insisting on <span class=\"mm\">αAβ → αγβ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">γ ≠ ε</span>. The length condition is the operational test, and it is the one that makes the language class decidable: no derivation ever shortens the string, so only finitely many sentential forms of each length exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Context-sensitive means non-shortening. Whenever a production is offered for inspection, compare the lengths of the two sides first.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q12", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A machine with <span class=\"mm\">k</span> tapes, where <span class=\"mm\">k</span> is a fixed constant, runs in time <span class=\"mm\">t(n)</span> on inputs of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span>. The standard simulation of this machine by a single-tape Turing machine runs in time:</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">O(t(n))</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(t(n) log t(n))</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(t(n)²)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(2^{t(n)})</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The single-tape simulation stores all <span class=\"mm\">k</span> tapes on one tape, separated by markers, with a mark under each simulated head. Every simulated move must first bring the real head to the relevant marked cell and then bring it back, and the distance travelled is bounded by the number of tape cells that have been used, which is at most <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n))</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After <span class=\"mm\">t(n)</span> simulated moves no tape can hold more than <span class=\"mm\">t(n) + 1</span> non-blank cells, because the head writes at most one cell per move.</li><li>A single simulated move requires a walk of length <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n))</span> along the packed tape, so it costs <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n))</span> real moves.</li><li>There are <span class=\"mm\">t(n)</span> simulated moves, so the total is <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n)) · O(t(n)) = O(t(n)²)</span>.</li><li>The quadratic factor is the price of the packing, and it is the reason the single-tape model is used for definitions rather than for complexity results.</li><li>Option (D) is the cost of removing nondeterminism, not of removing extra tapes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n)²)</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that extra tapes are free because a constant number of them is used. The constant hides a factor that depends on the length of the tape, and the tape length grows with the running time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Multitape and single-tape machines accept the same languages and differ by a square in time. The pair (same class, polynomial difference) is what makes the model robust for computability and fragile for fine-grained complexity.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q13", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub> = {⟨M⟩ : M halts when started on the empty string}</span>. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is decidable, because the input to <span class=\"mm\">M</span> is fixed and very short.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is recursively enumerable but not recursive, and <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤<sub>m</sub> HALT<sub>ε</sub></span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is co-recursively enumerable but not recursively enumerable.", "<span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is neither recursively enumerable nor co-recursively enumerable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Fixing the input does not bound the computation. <span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is the halting problem with the second component frozen, and the freezing is undone by a one-line construction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Reduction.</b> Given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>w</sub></span> which on any input erases that input, writes <span class=\"mm\">w</span> on the tape and then runs <span class=\"mm\">M</span>. The construction is computable and <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>w</sub></span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> exactly when <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Hence <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤<sub>m</sub> HALT<sub>ε</sub></span>.</li><li><b>Consequence.</b> If <span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> were decidable then <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> would be, so <span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is undecidable.</li><li><b>Recognisable.</b> Simulating <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> accepts every machine that halts, so <span class=\"mm\">HALT<sub>ε</sub></span> is r.e.</li><li>Option (A) fails because a short input can drive a very long computation; length of input is not a bound on running time.</li><li>Options (C) and (D) fail because the set is r.e.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; r.e. but not recursive, with <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤<sub>m</sub> HALT<sub>ε</sub></span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a short input makes a problem easy. Decidability depends on whether the computation can be bounded, not on the size of the input string; a machine on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> can run for any number of steps.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Restricting the input is a reduction, not a simplification. Every &ldquo;does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> do X on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>&rdquo; question is a candidate for undecidability, and the way to settle it is to encode an arbitrary <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> into a machine that ignores its input.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q14", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following problems are <b>decidable</b>? Select all that apply.</p>", "options": ["Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and an integer <span class=\"mm\">n</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halt on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> within <span class=\"mm\">n</span> steps?", "Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, started on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, ever move its head to the left of the cell on which it started?", "Given a Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span>, is <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> finite?", "Given a deterministic Turing machine <span class=\"mm\">M</span> and a string <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, does <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halt on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> within <span class=\"mm\">2^{|w|}</span> steps?"], "answer_display": "Options (A and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bounded question is decidable because the simulation has a clock. An unbounded question about behaviour or about the language is not, and the standard reductions apply.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) decidable.</b> Simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> for at most <span class=\"mm\">n</span> steps. If it has not halted by then, answer no. The simulation terminates by construction.</li><li><b>(B) undecidable.</b> Reduce <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> to it: given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> that simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> and, once that halts, moves its head left. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> moves left exactly when <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. There is no bound on when the move happens, so simulation cannot answer no.</li><li><b>(C) undecidable.</b> Finiteness of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> is a non-trivial semantic property, so Rice&rsquo;s theorem applies.</li><li><b>(D) decidable.</b> The bound is a function of the input: simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> for at most <span class=\"mm\">2^{|w|}</span> steps and answer no if it has not halted. Determinism matters only for the step count; the simulation is still finite.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a bound written in terms of <span class=\"mm\">|w|</span> as if it were no bound at all. A function of the input length is computable from the input, so it serves as a clock exactly like a constant does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask where the clock comes from. If the number of steps is fixed in advance, or computable from the input, the problem is decidable by brute simulation; if it is not, expect a reduction from <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q15", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A Turing machine has <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 5</span> states and tape alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Γ = {␣, 1}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">|Γ| = 2</span>. Count a configuration as the triple (state, head position, tape contents), where the tape contents is the string held in the first six cells of the tape, blank cells included, and the head stands on one of those six cells. How many distinct configurations of this shape are there?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1920", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A configuration is fixed independently by three facts: the state, the head position and what each cell holds. When all three ranges are finite the count is a product, and that product is what bounds the running time of a machine whose space is bounded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States: <span class=\"mm\">|Q| = 5</span> choices.</li><li>Head positions: 6 choices, one for each of the six cells.</li><li>Tape contents: each of the 6 cells independently holds one of the 2 tape symbols, giving <span class=\"mm\">2⁶ = 64</span> strings. The blank is a legal cell content, so blank-filled strings are counted too.</li><li>Multiply the independent choices: <span class=\"mm\">5 × 6 × 64 = 1920</span>.</li><li>Sanity check on the shape of the formula: with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> cells it is <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · n · |Γ|ⁿ</span>, which grows without bound as <span class=\"mm\">n</span> grows &mdash; the tape is not bounded here, only the window that is being counted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1920 configurations.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that a cell may be blank, which reduces the content factor to <span class=\"mm\">2⁵</span> and gives 960. The blank is a symbol of <span class=\"mm\">Γ</span>, so it is one of the 2 choices for every one of the 6 cells.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bounded space plus finite control gives finitely many configurations, and the count is <span class=\"mm\">|Q| · (cells) · |Γ|^{cells}</span>. That product is the engine behind the decidability of LBA acceptance and of context-sensitive membership.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q16", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements about the class of recursively enumerable languages is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["It is closed under union and under intersection, but not under complementation.", "It is closed under complementation.", "It is closed under union but not under intersection.", "It is closed under neither union nor intersection."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two recognisers combine for union by running in parallel, and for intersection by running in sequence. Complementation is the operation that fails, because recognising a set gives no way to recognise its complement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Union.</b> Dovetail the recognisers for <span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span> on the input and accept if either accepts.</li><li><b>Intersection.</b> Run the recogniser for <span class=\"mm\">L₁</span> to acceptance, then the recogniser for <span class=\"mm\">L₂</span>. Both must accept, and both halt when they do, so the composite halts on every member of the intersection.</li><li><b>Complementation fails.</b> <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is r.e. while its complement is not; if the class were closed under complement, both would be r.e. and <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> would be decidable.</li><li>Option (C) is false because intersection closure holds; options (B) and (D) contradict the two facts above.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; closed under union and intersection, not under complementation.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that intersection is the operation that breaks, because intersection &ldquo;feels&rdquo; stricter. For recognition, strictness is cheap: a sequential run of two recognisers still halts on members.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Union and intersection are easy for recognisers; complement is the hard one, and it is exactly the operation that separates r.e. from recursive.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q17", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">To prove that <span class=\"mm\">INF = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) is infinite}</span> is undecidable, the standard reduction from <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> maps <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩</span> to <span class=\"mm\">⟨M′⟩</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> on input <span class=\"mm\">x</span> simulates <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> for exactly <span class=\"mm\">|x|</span> steps and accepts <span class=\"mm\">x</span> if the simulation has halted by then. Why does the bound <span class=\"mm\">|x|</span>, rather than a fixed constant, matter?</p>", "options": ["Because it guarantees that <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> halts on every input.", "Because it bounds the running time of <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> by a polynomial in <span class=\"mm\">|x|</span>.", "Because without a bound <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> would not be a legal Turing machine.", "Because with it <span class=\"mm\">L(M′)</span> is infinite exactly when <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⇔ ⟨M′⟩ ∈ INF</span>."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The reduction must convert a question about one machine halting into a question about another machine having an infinite language. A threshold that grows with the input is what turns &ldquo;halts in <span class=\"mm\">t</span> steps&rdquo; into &ldquo;accepts infinitely many inputs&rdquo;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span> after <span class=\"mm\">t</span> steps, then for every <span class=\"mm\">x</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|x| ≥ t</span> the simulation finishes inside the budget, so <span class=\"mm\">x</span> is accepted. There are infinitely many such <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">L(M′)</span> is infinite.</li><li>If <span class=\"mm\">M</span> does not halt on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, the simulation never finishes inside any budget, so no <span class=\"mm\">x</span> is accepted and <span class=\"mm\">L(M′)</span> is empty, hence finite.</li><li>Therefore <span class=\"mm\">⟨M, w⟩ ∈ HALT ⇔ ⟨M′⟩ ∈ INF</span>, which is exactly a mapping reduction <span class=\"mm\">HALT ≤<sub>m</sub> INF</span>. With a fixed constant bound <span class=\"mm\">c</span> the accepted set would be all <span class=\"mm\">x</span> or nothing, and it would no longer track halting.</li><li>Option (A) is false: <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> always halts, because its simulation is bounded. Halting is not what is being tested.</li><li>Options (B) and (C) are irrelevant to the correctness of the reduction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; the growing bound makes <span class=\"mm\">L(M′)</span> infinite exactly when <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the bound is there to make <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> terminate. <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> terminates for any bound; the point of <span class=\"mm\">|x|</span> is that it creates a threshold, so that a single halting time <span class=\"mm\">t</span> controls infinitely many inputs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In reductions to <span class=\"mm\">INF</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIN</span> or <span class=\"mm\">REG</span>, the gadget is always the same: a budget that grows with the input. The budget is the bridge between a finite event and an infinite language.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q18", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The language <span class=\"mm\">L = {w ∈ {a, b}* : w contains the same number of a&rsquo;s as b&rsquo;s}</span> belongs to which of the following classes, taking the <b>smallest</b> class that contains it?</p>", "options": ["The regular languages", "The context-free languages, but not the regular languages", "The context-sensitive languages, but not the context-free languages", "None of the four Chomsky classes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pushdown store can count a difference but a finite automaton cannot, and a single counter suffices here. So the language sits at Type 2 and no lower.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Context-free.</b> Use the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S → aSb | bSa | SS | ε</span>. Each production adds one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and one <span class=\"mm\">b</span> together, or nests a balanced block inside another, so every generated string has equal counts.</li><li><b>Not regular.</b> Intersect with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span>, which is regular. The result is <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}</span>, which is not regular by the pumping lemma. Regular languages are closed under intersection with a regular language, so <span class=\"mm\">L</span> cannot be regular.</li><li>Since <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free, it is also context-sensitive and r.e., so options (C) and (D) are wrong: (C) claims it is not context-free and (D) claims it is in none of the classes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; context-free but not regular.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing this language with <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> or assuming that any counting language must be context-sensitive. Equal counts of two symbols need one counter; equal counts of three symbols need two, and that is where context-freeness ends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a two-letter alphabet, &ldquo;equal counts&rdquo; is context-free: one stack holds the running difference. For three letters, the same idea needs a second stack, and the language becomes context-sensitive only.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q19", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider Rice&rsquo;s theorem and the properties of Turing machines listed below. Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? Select all that apply.</p>", "options": ["Rice&rsquo;s theorem shows that <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) = Σ*}</span> is undecidable.", "Rice&rsquo;s theorem applies to the property &ldquo;<span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span> contains the string 0101&rdquo;.", "Rice&rsquo;s theorem applies to the property &ldquo;<span class=\"mm\">M</span> has an odd number of states&rdquo;.", "Rice&rsquo;s theorem shows that <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M uses more than 100 tape cells on some input}</span> is undecidable."], "answer_display": "Options (A and B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rice&rsquo;s theorem is about the set of strings a machine accepts, never about how the machine is built or how much space it uses. Semantic and non-trivial are the two conditions; everything else is outside the theorem.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> &ldquo;<span class=\"mm\">L(M) = Σ*</span>&rdquo; is a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>. It is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> has it and <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> does not. Rice applies, so the set is undecidable.</li><li><b>(B) true.</b> Containing a particular string is a property of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and it is non-trivial: <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> contains 0101 and <span class=\"mm\">∅</span> does not. Rice applies.</li><li><b>(C) false.</b> The number of states is read off the encoding; it is a syntactic property, so Rice&rsquo;s theorem says nothing about it, and the language is in fact decidable.</li><li><b>(D) false.</b> Space usage is a property of the machine&rsquo;s behaviour, not of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>: two machines accepting the same language can use very different amounts of tape. Rice does not apply. (The problem is undecidable, but for other reasons.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any undecidable problem about machines is undecidable &ldquo;by Rice&rsquo;s theorem&rdquo;. Many are &mdash; space usage, for instance &mdash; but the theorem covers only properties of <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two tests, both required: is the property a fact about <span class=\"mm\">L(M)</span>, and is it true of some r.e. language while false of another? Rice&rsquo;s theorem is a one-line proof when both answers are yes, and silent otherwise.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q20", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many of the following five languages are recursively enumerable?<br/>(i) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅}</span> (ii) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) ≠ ∅}</span> (iii) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on ε}</span> (iv) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M does not halt on ε}</span> (v) <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on ε within 10 steps}</span></p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A language is r.e. when membership can be confirmed by a finite computation. A question whose yes-answer requires proving that something never happens is generally not r.e., even when it is decidable in a bounded form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(i) not r.e.</b> &ldquo;<span class=\"mm\">L(M) = ∅</span>&rdquo; is the complement of the r.e. set <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : L(M) ≠ ∅}</span>, and it is not itself r.e. Confirming emptiness would require showing that no input is ever accepted, which is an infinite check.</li><li><b>(ii) r.e.</b> Dovetail <span class=\"mm\">M</span> over all inputs; accept as soon as some computation accepts.</li><li><b>(iii) r.e.</b> Simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> and accept when it halts.</li><li><b>(iv) not r.e.</b> This is the complement of (iii), and (iii) is r.e. without being recursive, so its complement is not r.e.</li><li><b>(v) r.e.</b> It is in fact recursive: simulate for 10 steps and answer. Every recursive language is r.e.</li><li>Count: (ii), (iii) and (v) &mdash; three languages.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 &mdash; languages (ii), (iii) and (v) are recursively enumerable.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the obviously semi-decidable ones and forgetting that recursive languages are r.e. as well. A bounded question is decidable, hence r.e., and must be included in the count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sort the five into three piles: recursive (bounded questions), r.e. but not recursive (unbounded positive questions), and neither (unbounded negative questions). Only the first two piles are r.e.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q21", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "recursive-vs-recursively-enumerable", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">K = {⟨M⟩ : M halts on the input ⟨M⟩}</span>, and let <span class=\"mm\">≤<sub>m</sub></span> denote mapping reducibility. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every recursively enumerable language <span class=\"mm\">L</span> satisfies <span class=\"mm\">L ≤<sub>m</sub> K</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">K ≤<sub>m</sub> L</span> for every recursively enumerable language <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">K</span> is recursive.", "The complement <span class=\"mm\">K̄</span> is recursively enumerable."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is the halting problem with both arguments collapsed into one, and it is complete for the r.e. languages: every r.e. set reduces to it. That is what makes it the standard vehicle for undecidability proofs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">K</span> is r.e.: simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩</span> and accept when it halts.</li><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be r.e., recognised by <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>L</sub></span>. Define <span class=\"mm\">f(w) = ⟨M<sub>w</sub>⟩</span> where <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>w</sub></span> on input <span class=\"mm\">x</span> ignores <span class=\"mm\">x</span> and runs <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>L</sub></span> on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>. Then <span class=\"mm\">M<sub>w</sub></span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">⟨M<sub>w</sub>⟩</span> exactly when <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L ⇔ f(w) ∈ K</span>.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">L ≤<sub>m</sub> K</span> for every r.e. <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. Option (A) holds.</li><li>Option (B) fails: reducing <span class=\"mm\">K</span> to an arbitrary r.e. set would make every r.e. set as hard as <span class=\"mm\">K</span>, which is false for <span class=\"mm\">∅</span>.</li><li>Option (C) fails because a decider for <span class=\"mm\">K</span> would decide the halting problem.</li><li>Option (D) fails because <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is r.e. and undecidable, so <span class=\"mm\">K̄</span> is not r.e.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">K</span> is complete for the recursively enumerable languages.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming reducibility is symmetric. Mapping reductions form a preorder, not an equivalence: hard sets absorb everything below them, and reducing a hard set to an easy one is impossible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One complete set is enough for every undecidability proof. To show an r.e. set <span class=\"mm\">L</span> undecidable, reduce <span class=\"mm\">K</span> (or <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span>) to it; to show it is r.e., exhibit a recogniser.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q22", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "halting-problem-undecidability", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">A = {⟨M⟩ : M halts on ε}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B = {⟨M⟩ : M halts on every input}</span>. Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A ≤<sub>m</sub> B</span>, and both <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> are recursively enumerable.", "Both <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> are recursive.", "<span class=\"mm\">B ≤<sub>m</sub> A</span>; <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is recursively enumerable and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is not.", "Neither <span class=\"mm\">A</span> nor <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is recursively enumerable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Halting on one input is a positive existential question and is semi-decidable. Halting on every input is a universal question, and universality is what pushes a set out of the r.e. class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b><span class=\"mm\">A</span> is r.e.</b> Simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span> on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>; accept when it halts.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">B ≤<sub>m</sub> A</span>.</b> Given <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩</span>, build <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> which on input <span class=\"mm\">x</span> dovetails <span class=\"mm\">M</span> over all inputs in order of <span class=\"mm\">i + k</span>, halting when every input has been seen to halt. Then <span class=\"mm\">M′</span> halts on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> exactly when <span class=\"mm\">M</span> halts on every input, so <span class=\"mm\">⟨M⟩ ∈ B ⇔ ⟨M′⟩ ∈ A</span>.</li><li><b><span class=\"mm\">B</span> is not r.e.</b> If it were, then since <span class=\"mm\">B ≤<sub>m</sub> A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is r.e. we would have <span class=\"mm\">B</span> decidable together with its complement; but <span class=\"mm\">B̄</span> is r.e. (halt on some input is confirmable by simulation) and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is not decidable, so <span class=\"mm\">B</span> cannot be r.e.</li><li>Option (A) fails because the reduction goes the other way: a machine that halts on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> tells you nothing about other inputs.</li><li>Options (B) and (D) fail on the facts established above.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">B ≤<sub>m</sub> A</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> r.e. and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> not r.e.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the reduction because both sets are &ldquo;about halting&rdquo;. The direction follows the quantifier: a universal statement is stronger, so it reduces to the existential one, not the other way round.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Existential questions are r.e., universal questions are co-r.e., and only the decidable ones are both. When two halting sets are compared, decide first which one carries the &ldquo;for all&rdquo;.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q23", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "standard-turing-machine-models", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the robustness of the Turing-machine model are <b>TRUE</b>? Select all that apply.</p>", "options": ["A machine with <span class=\"mm\">k</span> tapes can be simulated by a single-tape machine with only a quadratic slowdown.", "A machine whose tape is infinite in both directions can be simulated by one whose tape is infinite in one direction with no asymptotic slowdown.", "A nondeterministic Turing machine can be simulated by a deterministic Turing machine, so both accept exactly the recursively enumerable languages.", "A Turing machine whose tape is a two-dimensional grid accepts exactly the same class of languages as the standard one."], "answer_display": "Options (A, C and D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Robustness means the accepted class survives every reasonable change of the model. Time and space may blow up, sometimes badly, but the class of languages does not move &mdash; except when the change bounds the storage.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) true.</b> Pack the <span class=\"mm\">k</span> tapes onto one tape with markers and one mark per simulated head. Each simulated move costs a walk of length at most <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n))</span>, so the total is <span class=\"mm\">O(t(n)²)</span>. Same class, quadratic time.</li><li><b>(B) false.</b> Folding a two-way tape onto a one-way tape is possible, but the fold marker sits at a fixed position and the head must travel to it and back on every move that changes halves. The distance grows with the number of cells in use, so the slowdown is <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span> in the worst case, not constant.</li><li><b>(C) true.</b> Breadth-first search over configurations removes nondeterminism. The search may take exponential time, but it terminates exactly when some branch accepts, so the accepted language is unchanged.</li><li><b>(D) true.</b> A two-dimensional tape is simulated by numbering the cells row by row on a one-dimensional tape; the head then has to walk to the target cell, which costs time but not expressive power.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that every simulation is asymptotically free. The multi-tape and two-way-tape constructions preserve the language but not the running time, and the two-way-tape one is the sharper trap because it looks like a mere relabelling of cells.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Separate the question &ldquo;same languages?&rdquo; from &ldquo;same time?&rdquo;. Almost every model variant answers yes to the first; almost none answers yes to the second.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q24", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "chomsky-hierarchy-summary", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements about closure properties in the Chomsky hierarchy is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["The context-free languages are not closed under complementation.", "Every context-sensitive language is context-free.", "The context-free languages are closed under intersection.", "The context-sensitive languages are closed under complementation, while the recursively enumerable languages are not."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Complement closure does not follow the hierarchy in a straight line. It fails at the top and, surprisingly, holds for context-free languages; for context-sensitive languages it is a deep theorem.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>Regular.</b> Closed: swap final and non-final states of a total DFA.</li><li><b>Context-free.</b> Closed. The classical proof uses the fact that <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free exactly when <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is the homomorphic image of the intersection of a Dyck language with a regular set, and the complement of such a set can be written in the same shape.</li><li><b>Context-sensitive.</b> Closed by the Immerman&ndash;Szelepcs&eacute;nyi theorem: a linear-bounded automaton can count the configurations reachable from its start configuration, and counting is enough to decide non-reachability.</li><li><b>Recursively enumerable.</b> Not closed: <span class=\"mm\">HALT</span> is r.e. and its complement is not.</li><li>Hence (A) is false, (B) reverses a strict inclusion, (C) fails because <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿcⁿ}</span> is the intersection of two context-free languages and is not context-free, and (D) is the true statement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; context-sensitive languages are closed under complement, recursively enumerable ones are not.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The two standard traps are the context-free languages, which <i>are</i> closed under complement, and the context-sensitive languages, which look too weak to be. Both facts contradict the intuition that &ldquo;harder class, better closure&rdquo;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Closure properties must be learned per class, not extrapolated up or down the hierarchy. The safe anchors are: regular is closed under everything; r.e. fails on complement; context-free fails on intersection but not on complement.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U4-S2-Q25", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "turing-machines-decidability", "subtopic": "rices-theorem-reductions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The language <span class=\"mm\">L = {⟨M⟩ : M halts on every input of length at most 3 within 1000 steps}</span> is decidable: for each such input the simulation is bounded, so it always terminates. Over the alphabet <span class=\"mm\">Σ = {0, 1}</span>, on how many input strings must the decider simulate <span class=\"mm\">M</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The bound is on the <i>length</i> of the input and on the <i>number of steps</i>, and both bounds together make the whole check finite. What remains is to count the strings that fall inside the length bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Strings of length 0: the empty string <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, so 1 string.</li><li>Strings of length 1: <span class=\"mm\">0</span> and <span class=\"mm\">1</span>, so 2 strings.</li><li>Strings of length 2: <span class=\"mm\">2² = 4</span> strings.</li><li>Strings of length 3: <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> strings.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15</span>. Each is simulated for at most 1000 steps, so the decider runs for at most <span class=\"mm\">15 × 1000</span> simulated steps and always halts.</li><li>Note why the step bound cannot be dropped: <span class=\"mm\">{⟨M⟩ : M halts on every input of length at most 3}</span> without a time bound is undecidable, because it asks for a halting answer on 15 specific strings and each of those questions is itself undecidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15 input strings.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Summing only the strings of length exactly 3 and answering 8, or forgetting <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> altogether and answering 14. The phrase &ldquo;at most 3&rdquo; includes length 0.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A decidable bounded-halting question is a finite conjunction over finitely many inputs. Count the inputs with <span class=\"mm\">Σ_{i=0}^{k} |Σ|ⁱ</span>, then note that each conjunct is decidable only because the step budget is fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q51", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Over Σ = {a, b}, how many states does the minimal complete DFA have that accepts the language {ε}, i.e. the empty string and nothing else?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "Infinitely many"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A complete DFA must define a transition on every symbol from every state, and the start state alone decides membership of <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>. Every non-empty string must therefore be sent to a state that is not final and that no string can escape from.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> be the start state. Since <span class=\"mm\">ε ∈ L</span>, <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> must be final.</li><li>Since <span class=\"mm\">a ∉ L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b ∉ L</span>, both transitions out of <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> must lead to a non-final state; call it <span class=\"mm\">d</span> (the dead state).</li><li>Completeness forces transitions out of <span class=\"mm\">d</span> on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. Once a string has left <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> it can never return, so <span class=\"mm\">δ(d, a) = δ(d, b) = d</span>.</li><li>Two states suffice and neither can be dropped: <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> is final and <span class=\"mm\">d</span> is not, so they are distinguishable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 2 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 on the grounds that only one string is accepted. A single final state accepts <i>every</i> string, and a single non-final state accepts none; rejecting all but one string needs a second state to reject into.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q52", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A complete DFA over Σ = {0, 1} has exactly one state, that state is final, and both transitions from it lead back to itself. The language accepted is:", "options": ["{ε}", "∅", "Σ*", "the strings of length exactly 1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA accepts a string exactly when the state reached after reading all of it is final. If the only state is final and every symbol loops, every string ends where it started.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The run on <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> stays in the start state, which is final, so <span class=\"mm\">ε ∈ L</span>.</li><li>Reading any symbol keeps the machine in the same state, so by induction on the length of <span class=\"mm\">w</span> the run on every <span class=\"mm\">w</span> ends in that state.</li><li>That state is final, so <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ Σ*</span>.</li><li>The machine is already minimal: a single state is the fewest possible, and <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> needs no rejection state.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"one state\" as \"one symbol accepted\". The number of states says nothing about the length of the strings accepted; it is the pattern of final states and loops that does.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q53", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An NFA over Σ = {a} has a single state q₀ that is both the start state and the only final state, with δ(q₀, a) = {q₀}. The language it accepts is:", "options": ["{ε}", "{a}", "a*", "a⁺"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nondeterminism does not change the acceptance rule: a string is accepted if <i>some</i> run from the start state ends in a final state. Here there is only one run, and it exists for every string over <span class=\"mm\">{a}</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> the run is empty and ends in <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>, which is final, so <span class=\"mm\">ε ∈ L</span>.</li><li>On <span class=\"mm\">aⁿ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 1</span> the single run reads <span class=\"mm\">n</span> symbols and returns to <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> each time, ending final.</li><li>No other strings exist over the one-letter alphabet, so <span class=\"mm\">L = a*</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — <span class=\"mm\">a*</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing <span class=\"mm\">a⁺</span> by assuming a non-empty string is needed. Because the start state is <i>also</i> final, the empty run already accepts <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>; deleting the final mark from <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> is what would turn <span class=\"mm\">a*</span> into <span class=\"mm\">a⁺</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q54", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which regular expression denotes exactly the set of strings over Σ = {a, b} of length exactly 2?", "options": ["(a + b)*", "(a + b)(a + b)", "aa + bb", "(ab)*"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Concatenation of two factors that each denote a single symbol forces exactly two symbols; the star, not the concatenation, is what allows a variable length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">(a + b)</span> denotes the two one-letter strings <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>Concatenating it with itself gives <span class=\"mm\">{aa, ab, ba, bb}</span> — every string of length 2 and no other.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</span> adds <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> and all longer strings, so it is far too big.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">aa + bb</span> keeps only two of the four strings, and <span class=\"mm\">(ab)*</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">{ε, ab, abab, …}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <span class=\"mm\">(a + b)(a + b)</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <span class=\"mm\">(a + b)²</span> as <span class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</span>, or thinking that a union must be repeated. A union inside a factor gives a choice at <i>one</i> position; two factors give two independent choices.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q55", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The regular expression (ab)* over Σ = {a, b} denotes:", "options": ["all strings with equal numbers of a and b", "the strings ε, ab, abab, ababab, … and nothing else", "all strings of even length", "all strings that end in b"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A star repeats a <i>fixed block</i>. Here the block is the two-letter word <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>, so every member is a whole number of copies of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Zero repetitions give <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>.</li><li>One repetition gives <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>; two give <span class=\"mm\">abab</span>; in general <span class=\"mm\">(ab)ⁿ</span> for <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 0</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> has equal counts but is not a repetition of <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>, so the first option fails.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">aa</span> has even length but no <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so the third fails; <span class=\"mm\">bb</span> ends in <span class=\"mm\">b</span> but is not in the language, so the fourth fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <span class=\"mm\">(ab)*</span> as \"any arrangement of equal numbers of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>\". Equality of counts would need <span class=\"mm\">(ab + ba)*</span>-style gymnastics at best and is in fact not regular; the block inside a star keeps the order fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q56", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A complete DFA with n states accepts the regular language L. In a pumping-lemma proof for L, the pumping constant p may always be taken as:", "options": ["n", "n + 1", "2ⁿ", "n²"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The constant in the pumping lemma is the number of states of a DFA for the language: reading <span class=\"mm\">p</span> symbols visits <span class=\"mm\">p + 1</span> states, so a repeat is unavoidable and the loop can be pumped.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <span class=\"mm\">M</span> have <span class=\"mm\">n</span> states and let <span class=\"mm\">w = a₁a₂…a_m ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">m ≥ n</span>.</li><li>The run <span class=\"mm\">q₀, q₁, …, q_m</span> has <span class=\"mm\">m + 1 ≥ n + 1</span> states, so two of <span class=\"mm\">q₀, …, q_n</span> coincide.</li><li>Choose <span class=\"mm\">i &lt; j ≤ n</span> with <span class=\"mm\">q_i = q_j</span>, set <span class=\"mm\">x = a₁…a_i</span>, <span class=\"mm\">y = a_{i+1}…a_j</span>, <span class=\"mm\">z = a_{j+1}…a_m</span>.</li><li>Then <span class=\"mm\">|y| = j − i ≥ 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|xy| = j ≤ n</span>, and the loop at <span class=\"mm\">q_i</span> can be traversed any number of times, so <span class=\"mm\">xyⁱz ∈ L</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">i ≥ 0</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <span class=\"mm\">p = n</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the constant must be a tight \"magic number\" for the language. Any value at least as large as the number of states works; what matters is that the constant exists and is independent of the word being pumped.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q57", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following languages over Σ = {a, b} have a minimal complete DFA with exactly 2 states? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["{ε}", "Σ*", "{w : |w| is even}", "{w : w starts with a}"], "answer_display": "Options (A and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count states by counting the distinctions a DFA must remember. Two states means exactly one binary distinction; the start state plus one more state, one of which is final.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) <span class=\"mm\">{ε}</span>.</b> Start state final, one dead state for everything non-empty: 2 states, and they are distinguishable (<span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates them).</li><li><b>(B) <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>.</b> Every string is accepted, so one final state with self-loops is complete and minimal: 1 state, not 2.</li><li><b>(C) even length.</b> Only the parity of the length matters: an even state (start, final) and an odd state, each flipping on every symbol. Both are reachable and distinguishable: 2 states.</li><li><b>(D) starts with a.</b> Three distinctions are needed — nothing read yet, first symbol was <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, first symbol was <span class=\"mm\">b</span> (dead) — so the minimal complete DFA has 3 states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming \"starts with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\" is a one-bit property because the answer is yes or no. The DFA also has to represent the third situation \"the first symbol has already been read and it was not <span class=\"mm\">a</span>\", which cannot be merged with either of the others.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q58", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many states does the minimal complete DFA over Σ = {a, b} have that accepts exactly the strings of even length?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA counts only modulo what it can store. For a length condition the relevant residue is the length modulo the period, here 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>State <span class=\"mm\">E</span>: an even number of symbols has been read. It is the start state and it is final, because <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> has even length.</li><li>State <span class=\"mm\">O</span>: an odd number has been read. Not final.</li><li>Every symbol flips the parity: <span class=\"mm\">δ(E, a) = δ(E, b) = O</span> and <span class=\"mm\">δ(O, a) = δ(O, b) = E</span>. The machine is complete and both states are reachable.</li><li>The two states are distinguishable by <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, so no merging is possible: the minimal complete DFA has 2 states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding a dead state \"to reject the odd strings\". Rejection needs no extra state — a non-final state already rejects — and the transition back to <span class=\"mm\">E</span> is essential, because an odd prefix can still grow into an even string.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q59", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many strings of length exactly 3 over Σ = {a, b} belong to L(a*b*)?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> contains exactly the strings in which no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> occurs after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>: some <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, then some <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, either block possibly empty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A word of length 3 in the language is <span class=\"mm\">aⁱbʲ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">i + j = 3</span>.</li><li>The possible pairs are <span class=\"mm\">(3,0), (2,1), (1,2), (0,3)</span>.</li><li>They give <span class=\"mm\">aaa, aab, abb, bbb</span> — four distinct strings.</li><li>The remaining four strings of length 3 &mdash; <span class=\"mm\">aba, baa, bab, bba</span> &mdash; are outside the language, because each of them has an <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> as if the two stars were independent choices at each position, which would give all <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> strings. The order is fixed: the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block comes first.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q60", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An NFA over Σ = {a} has states {p, q}, start state p, final state {q}, and transitions δ(p, a) = {p, q}, δ(q, a) = {q}. The language it accepts is:", "options": ["{a}", "a⁺", "a*", "∅"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an NFA a symbol may lead to several states at once, and acceptance needs only one surviving path that ends final. The nondeterministic move <span class=\"mm\">p → q</span> is a guess that \"the string ends after this symbol\".</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">ε</span>: the only run ends in <span class=\"mm\">p</span>, which is not final, so <span class=\"mm\">ε ∉ L</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">a</span>: the run may take <span class=\"mm\">p → q</span> and stop in the final state <span class=\"mm\">q</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ L</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">aⁿ</span> for <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 1</span>: stay in <span class=\"mm\">p</span> for the first <span class=\"mm\">n − 1</span> symbols, then move to <span class=\"mm\">q</span> on the last one, where the self-loop keeps the run alive. So every <span class=\"mm\">aⁿ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">n ≥ 1</span> is accepted.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">L = a⁺ = a·a*</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <span class=\"mm\">a⁺</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">a*</span> by treating the NFA like a DFA whose start state is final. Here <span class=\"mm\">p</span> is not final, so the empty string is rejected; making <span class=\"mm\">p</span> final is exactly what would add <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q61", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many states does the minimal complete DFA over Σ = {0, 1} have that accepts exactly those strings which contain an even number of 0's and end in 1?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent facts — the parity of the number of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s and whether the last symbol was a <span class=\"mm\">1</span> — suggest the product of two 2-state machines, so <b>at most</b> 4 states. The product is an upper bound; a minimal machine counts only the classes that survive, so the answer has to be checked, not multiplied.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Candidate states: <span class=\"mm\">(even, last = 1)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(even, last ≠ 1)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(odd, last = 1)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(odd, last ≠ 1)</span>. Start in <span class=\"mm\">(even, last ≠ 1)</span>; the only final state is <span class=\"mm\">(even, last = 1)</span>.</li><li>Reading <span class=\"mm\">0</span> flips the parity and clears the \"last = 1\" bit; reading <span class=\"mm\">1</span> keeps the parity and sets it. So <span class=\"mm\">(even, last ≠ 1)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(even, last = 1)</span> both go to <span class=\"mm\">(odd, last ≠ 1)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">0</span> and to <span class=\"mm\">(even, last = 1)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">1</span>.</li><li>Those two have identical transitions, but one is final and the other is not, so <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates them: they stay apart.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(odd, last ≠ 1)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(odd, last = 1)</span> also have identical transitions — both go to <span class=\"mm\">(even, last ≠ 1)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">0</span> and to <span class=\"mm\">(odd, last = 1)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">1</span> — and neither is final. Identical transitions plus identical finality means equivalent, so they merge.</li><li>Three states remain, all reachable (<span class=\"mm\">ε</span>, <span class=\"mm\">1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">0</span>) and pairwise distinguishable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by multiplying the two parities. While the count of <span class=\"mm\">0</span>'s is odd the machine cannot accept at all, and it must read another <span class=\"mm\">0</span> first — which erases whatever the \"last symbol\" bit was holding. The bit is therefore worthless in the two odd states, and they collapse into one.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q62", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In the subset construction that converts an NFA N into an equivalent DFA D, which states of D are final?", "options": ["only the singleton sets {f} where f is final in N", "every subset that contains at least one final state of N", "the single subset consisting of all final states of N", "every subset that is contained in the set of final states of N"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A state of <span class=\"mm\">D</span> records the <i>set of all</i> NFA states reachable on the input read so far. The NFA accepts if any one of those states is final, so the DFA must accept exactly when its set meets the final set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After reading <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, the DFA is in the subset <span class=\"mm\">S = {q : some run of N on w ends in q}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L(N)</span> iff some run ends in a final state, that is iff <span class=\"mm\">S ∩ F ≠ ∅</span>.</li><li>So the final states of <span class=\"mm\">D</span> are precisely the subsets that intersect <span class=\"mm\">F</span>.</li><li>Singletons are not enough: the subset <span class=\"mm\">{p, f}</span> with <span class=\"mm\">f ∈ F</span> is a perfectly ordinary DFA state, and it accepts.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Marking only the singleton <span class=\"mm\">{f}</span> final. Because the machine tracks all live runs at once, a state such as <span class=\"mm\">{p, f}</span> is final too; forgetting this is the classic way to build a DFA that rejects strings the NFA accepts.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q63", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which regular expression denotes exactly the strings over Σ = {a, b} that contain no two consecutive b's?", "options": ["(a*b)*a*", "(a + b)*bb(a + b)*", "b*a*", "(ab)*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A forbidden pattern is easiest to exclude by describing the string as a sequence of blocks in which the forbidden pattern cannot occur. If every <span class=\"mm\">b</span> is followed by at least one <span class=\"mm\">a</span> — except possibly at the very end — no two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s can be adjacent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write any <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>-free string as <span class=\"mm\">a^{i₁}b a^{i₂}b … a^{i_k}b a^{i_{k+1}}</span>, where each block <span class=\"mm\">a^{i_j}b</span> for <span class=\"mm\">j ≤ k</span> is matched by one factor of <span class=\"mm\">(a*b)</span> and the trailing <span class=\"mm\">a^{i_{k+1}}</span> by the final <span class=\"mm\">a*</span>.</li><li>Conversely, every string of <span class=\"mm\">(a*b)*a*</span> is a concatenation of blocks each ending in a single <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, followed by <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s; two <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s from different blocks are separated by the next block's <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, so <span class=\"mm\">bb</span> never appears.</li><li>So the two descriptions agree.</li><li>The others fail: <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*bb(a+b)*</span> describes the strings that <i>do</i> contain <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>; <span class=\"mm\">b*a*</span> forbids any <span class=\"mm\">a</span> after a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; <span class=\"mm\">(ab)*</span> omits <span class=\"mm\">ba</span>, which is <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>-free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <span class=\"mm\">(a*b)*a*</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a star as if it allowed any interleaving, so that <span class=\"mm\">(a*b)*</span> is taken to be <span class=\"mm\">(a+b)*</span>. It is not: every factor must end in <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, which is exactly the structural fact that rules out <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q64", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following languages over Σ = {a, b} is regular, even though its definition involves counting?", "options": ["{w : #a(w) = #b(w)}", "{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}", "{w : #a(w) − #b(w) ≡ 0 (mod 3)}", "{ww : w ∈ {a, b}*}"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA can count only up to a fixed modulus. \"Equal counts\" is a comparison of two unbounded numbers; \"counts equal modulo 3\" is a three-valued residue, and three values fit in three states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Track <span class=\"mm\">r = (#a − #b) mod 3</span>, which lies in <span class=\"mm\">{0, 1, 2}</span>. Reading <span class=\"mm\">a</span> does <span class=\"mm\">r ↦ (r + 1) mod 3</span>; reading <span class=\"mm\">b</span> does <span class=\"mm\">r ↦ (r − 1) mod 3</span>.</li><li>Start at <span class=\"mm\">r = 0</span>, which is also the only final state. Three states, complete transitions, so the language is regular.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{ww}</span> need unbounded memory and fail the pumping lemma; <span class=\"mm\">{w : #a = #b}</span> does too, with the same <span class=\"mm\">aᵖbᵖ</span> word.</li><li>Only the modular condition survives, because the modulus bounds the memory.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any language defined by counting is non-regular. Counting <i>modulo a fixed constant</i> is regular; counting <i>exactly</i> without a bound is not.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q65", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A language L over Σ = {0, 1} is accepted by some complete DFA with 4 states. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The minimal complete DFA of L has at most 4 states.", "Every state of the minimal complete DFA of L is reachable from its start state.", "No two distinct states of the minimal complete DFA of L are equivalent.", "The minimal complete DFA of L has exactly 4 states."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimality is a <i>lower</i> bound on states for the language, never an upper one, and it is characterised by two conditions: no unreachable states and no equivalent pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">M</span> with 4 states accepts <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, then minimising <span class=\"mm\">M</span> — deleting unreachable states, then merging equivalent ones — yields the minimal DFA of <span class=\"mm\">L</span> with at most 4 states. So (A) is true.</li><li>Deleting unreachable states changes nothing about the language, and a minimal DFA has none, so (B) is true.</li><li>Merging equivalent states also preserves the language, and a minimal DFA has no such pair, so (C) is true.</li><li>(D) is false: a 4-state DFA can be non-minimal. For instance, a 4-state machine with two unreachable states accepts the same language as a 2-state minimal DFA.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"accepted by a 4-state DFA\" as \"needs 4 states\". The bound inherited from a given machine is an upper bound; the minimal count is what the language forces, and it can be strictly smaller.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q66", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "nfa-to-dfa-subset-construction", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following statements about nondeterministic finite automata is FALSE?", "options": ["An NFA may have several transitions on the same symbol from a single state.", "Every NFA has an equivalent DFA.", "A string is accepted if at least one computation path from the start state ends in a final state.", "A string is accepted only if every computation path from the start state ends in a final state."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nondeterministic acceptance is existential: one successful run is enough, and dead or rejecting runs are simply ignored. Requiring all runs to succeed is a universal condition, which is not what an NFA does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true by definition of the transition relation <span class=\"mm\">δ : Q × Σ → 2^Q</span>, which returns a set.</li><li>(B) is true: the subset construction converts any NFA, <span class=\"mm\">ε</span>-moves included, into an equivalent DFA with at most <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> states.</li><li>(C) is exactly the acceptance rule.</li><li>(D) is false. Take the NFA with <span class=\"mm\">δ(q₀, a) = {q₀, q₁}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> final: the string <span class=\"mm\">a</span> has one accepting run and one non-accepting run, and it is accepted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying the \"all paths must work\" intuition over from algorithm design. In automata theory nondeterminism means \"a lucky guess suffices\", which is why NFAs are no more powerful than DFAs.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q67", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following regular expressions denote exactly the set of strings over Σ = {a, b} that end in b? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["(a + b)*b", "(a*b*)*b", "b(a + b)*", "(a + b)*b(a + b)*"], "answer_display": "Options (A and B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> \"Ends in <span class=\"mm\">b</span>\" is a suffix condition: an arbitrary prefix followed by one forced final symbol. Whether the prefix is written as <span class=\"mm\">(a + b)*</span> or in some equivalent form is irrelevant, provided the form still denotes all of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">(a + b)*b</span>: every string is a prefix followed by <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so the condition holds exactly.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(a*b*)* = Σ*</span>: every string splits into a sequence of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-then-<span class=\"mm\">b</span> blocks, so the star of the union of the two blocks covers everything. Hence <span class=\"mm\">(a*b*)*b = Σ*b</span>, the same language.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b(a + b)*</span> forces the <span class=\"mm\">b</span> to come first: it describes the strings that <i>start</i> with <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and it rejects <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(a + b)*b(a + b)*</span> allows symbols after the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>: it describes the strings that <i>contain</i> <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and it accepts <span class=\"mm\">ba</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rejecting (B) because <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> alone is a proper subset of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>. The outer star repairs that: repeating the blocks in any order generates every string.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q68", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many states does the minimal complete DFA over Σ = {a, b} have that accepts exactly those strings which have even length and end in b?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two facts are needed at once — the parity of the length and the identity of the last symbol — so the product construction gives at most 4 states. The minimal count is the number of classes left after deleting unreachable states and merging equivalent ones.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Candidate states <span class=\"mm\">(even, b)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(even, ≠b)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(odd, b)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(odd, ≠b)</span>. Start in <span class=\"mm\">(even, ≠b)</span>, because the empty string has no last symbol. The only final state is <span class=\"mm\">(even, b)</span>.</li><li>Every symbol flips the parity. Reading <span class=\"mm\">b</span> sets the last-symbol bit, reading <span class=\"mm\">a</span> clears it. Hence <span class=\"mm\">(even, b)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(even, ≠b)</span> both go to <span class=\"mm\">(odd, ≠b)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and to <span class=\"mm\">(odd, b)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>Those two have identical transitions but differ in finality, so <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates them — both survive.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(odd, b)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(odd, ≠b)</span> both go to <span class=\"mm\">(even, ≠b)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and to <span class=\"mm\">(even, b)</span> on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and neither is final: identical transitions, identical finality, so they are equivalent and merge.</li><li>Three states remain, reachable via <span class=\"mm\">ε, b, a</span>, and pairwise distinguishable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 4 because the two parities and the two possible last symbols multiply. Once the length is odd the machine must read one more symbol before it can accept, and that symbol overwrites the \"last\" information, so the two odd states cannot be told apart.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q69", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For L = {w ∈ {a, b}* : w ends in ab}, how many equivalence classes does the Myhill–Nerode relation ≡_L have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two strings are <span class=\"mm\">≡_L</span>-equivalent when no continuation distinguishes them. The number of classes is exactly the number of states of the minimal DFA, so the question can be answered by building that DFA.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Class 1: strings that do not currently end in <span class=\"mm\">a</span> — the start class, since <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> has no suffix. Appending <span class=\"mm\">b</span> keeps a string here.</li><li>Class 2: strings that end in <span class=\"mm\">a</span> but not in <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>. Appending <span class=\"mm\">b</span> finishes the word; appending <span class=\"mm\">a</span> stays in class 2.</li><li>Class 3: strings that end in <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> — the accepting class. Appending <span class=\"mm\">b</span> returns to class 1, appending <span class=\"mm\">a</span> goes to class 2.</li><li>These three are pairwise distinguishable: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates class 3 from the rest, and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> separates class 2 from class 1. So no two merge, and 3 is the count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 2, one class per \"accepted / not accepted\". Equivalence is about future behaviour, not present membership: class 1 and class 2 are both non-accepting but respond differently to the continuation <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q70", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following languages over Σ = {a, b, c} satisfies the pumping lemma for regular languages and is nevertheless not regular?", "options": ["{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0}", "{aⁱbʲcᵏ : i = 1 ⇒ j = k}", "{ww : w ∈ {a, b}*}", "{aᵖ : p prime}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The pumping lemma is a necessary condition only. A language that satisfies it need not be regular, so \"pumps\" never proves regularity; only failing it proves non-regularity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(B) is not regular.</b> Intersect it with <span class=\"mm\">ab*c*</span>, a regular set. The result is <span class=\"mm\">{abʲcʲ : j ≥ 0}</span>, and the standard pumping argument on <span class=\"mm\">abᵖcᵖ</span> shows this is not regular; regular languages are closed under intersection, so (B) cannot be regular either.</li><li><b>(B) still pumps.</b> Take <span class=\"mm\">p = 2</span> and any <span class=\"mm\">w ∈ L</span> with <span class=\"mm\">|w| ≥ 2</span>. If <span class=\"mm\">w</span> does not start with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, pump the first symbol (<span class=\"mm\">b</span> or <span class=\"mm\">c</span>); the antecedent <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span> stays false, so the word remains in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. If <span class=\"mm\">w = abʲcʲ</span>, pump the leading <span class=\"mm\">a</span>: the new word starts with <span class=\"mm\">aa</span>, so again <span class=\"mm\">i ≠ 1</span> and it is in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. Every pumped word stays in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>.</li><li><b>(A), (C), (D) fail the lemma</b>, so each is non-regular and cannot be the answer: <span class=\"mm\">aᵖbᵖ</span> pumped in the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block breaks the balance, <span class=\"mm\">ww</span> with <span class=\"mm\">w = aᵖb</span> cannot be pumped in the first half, and for <span class=\"mm\">a^q</span> with <span class=\"mm\">q ≥ p</span> prime, pumping up to <span class=\"mm\">q(1 + k)</span> gives a composite length.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the pumping lemma as a characterisation of regularity. It is one-way: no conclusion follows from a language that pumps.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q71", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many states does the minimal complete DFA over Σ = {0, 1} have that accepts exactly those strings whose value, read as a binary number with leading zeros allowed, is divisible by 6?", "options": ["3", "4", "6", "12"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Divisibility by <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is decided by the residue of the prefix read so far, because reading a bit <span class=\"mm\">b</span> maps the value <span class=\"mm\">v</span> to <span class=\"mm\">2v + b</span>. That gives <span class=\"mm\">m</span> candidate states — but <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is the answer only when the doubling map is injective on residues, which fails whenever <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is even.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Candidate states <span class=\"mm\">q₀, …, q₅</span> for the residues <span class=\"mm\">0 … 5</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">δ(q_r, b) = q_{(2r + b) mod 6}</span>; start and only final state <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span>.</li><li>Collapse <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> with <span class=\"mm\">q₄</span>: <span class=\"mm\">2·1 + b = 2 + b</span> and <span class=\"mm\">2·4 + b = 8 + b ≡ 2 + b (mod 6)</span> for both bits, so the two states have identical transitions and identical finality — equivalent.</li><li>Collapse <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span> with <span class=\"mm\">q₅</span> the same way: <span class=\"mm\">2·2 + b = 4 + b</span> and <span class=\"mm\">2·5 + b = 10 + b ≡ 4 + b (mod 6)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> also have identical transitions, but <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> is final and <span class=\"mm\">q₃</span> is not, so <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> separates them; they stay apart.</li><li>Four classes remain and all are reachable: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">1</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">{1,4}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">11</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">{3}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">10</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">{2,5}</span>. They are pairwise distinguishable: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> isolates <span class=\"mm\">{0}</span>, the continuation <span class=\"mm\">0</span> separates <span class=\"mm\">{3}</span> from <span class=\"mm\">{1,4}</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">10</span> separates <span class=\"mm\">{1,4}</span> from <span class=\"mm\">{2,5}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 4 states.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 on autopilot — \"divisible by <span class=\"mm\">m</span> needs <span class=\"mm\">m</span> states\". That shortcut is valid when <span class=\"mm\">2</span> is invertible modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, as it is for <span class=\"mm\">m = 5</span> or <span class=\"mm\">m = 7</span>; for <span class=\"mm\">m = 6</span> the doubling map sends both <span class=\"mm\">1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">4</span> to the same place, and the surplus states merge.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q72", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "regular-expressions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which regular expression denotes exactly the strings over Σ = {a, b} that do not contain the substring ab?", "options": ["b*a*", "a*b*", "(ba)*", "(a*b)*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The forbidden pattern <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> is exactly \"an <span class=\"mm\">a</span> followed later by a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>\". Banning it means every <span class=\"mm\">b</span> must precede every <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, so the word is some <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s followed by some <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">w</span> contains no <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>, then once a <span class=\"mm\">b</span> has been read no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> may follow, so <span class=\"mm\">w = bʲaⁱ</span>.</li><li>Conversely every <span class=\"mm\">bʲaⁱ</span> has all its <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s before all its <span class=\"mm\">a</span>'s, so no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> is ever followed by a <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>Therefore the language is <span class=\"mm\">b*a*</span>.</li><li>The others fail: <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> itself; <span class=\"mm\">(ba)*</span> omits <span class=\"mm\">baa</span>, which has no <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>; and <span class=\"mm\">(a*b)* = Σ*</span> because <span class=\"mm\">ab = (a*b)</span> is a single factor, so it contains everything.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <span class=\"mm\">b*a*</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> by pattern-matching on the letters in the order they appear in the forbidden word. The condition is about what may <i>not</i> follow, so the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block has to come last, not first.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q73", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "pumping-lemma-for-regular", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following languages over Σ = {a, b} are regular? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["{aⁿbᵐ : n ≤ m}", "{aⁿbᵐ : n + m ≡ 0 (mod 2)}", "{w : #a(w) ≡ #b(w) (mod 2)}", "{aⁿbⁿ : n is a multiple of 3}"], "answer_display": "Options (B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A condition is regular when the information it needs is bounded: a residue modulo a fixed number qualifies, an unbounded comparison does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) not regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbᵐ : n ≤ m}</span> compares two unbounded counts. Intersecting with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*</span> leaves the same language, and pumping <span class=\"mm\">aᵖbᵖ</span> inside the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block gives <span class=\"mm\">a^{p+k}bᵖ</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p + k > p</span>, outside the language.</li><li><b>(B) regular.</b> <span class=\"mm\">n + m</span> is just the length of the word, so the condition says \"even length\" — two states.</li><li><b>(C) regular.</b> Track the parities of the two counts: four states <span class=\"mm\">(p_a, p_b) ∈ {0,1}²</span>, accepting exactly those with equal parities.</li><li><b>(D) not regular.</b> It is a sub-language of <span class=\"mm\">{aⁿbⁿ}</span> cut out by a condition on <span class=\"mm\">n</span>; pumping <span class=\"mm\">a^{3q}b^{3q}</span> in the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-block destroys the balance, and no choice of split repairs it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging (D) regular because \"multiple of 3\" is a modular condition. The modulus applies to <span class=\"mm\">n</span>, which must be counted exactly against the number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s; the modularity of the multiplier does not bound the memory needed.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q74", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Let L = {w ∈ {a, b}* : #a(w) is odd and #b(w) is even}. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["L is regular.", "The minimal complete DFA of L has 4 states.", "The complement of L has a minimal complete DFA with 4 states.", "L = {w : |w| is odd}."], "answer_display": "Options (A, B and C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Parity of two separate letter counts is a product of two 2-state machines, giving four states; complementation swaps final and non-final states without touching the transitions, so it never changes the state count of the minimal machine.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States <span class=\"mm\">(p_a, p_b) ∈ {0,1}²</span> with <span class=\"mm\">p_a</span> the parity of the <span class=\"mm\">a</span>-count and <span class=\"mm\">p_b</span> that of the <span class=\"mm\">b</span>-count; start at <span class=\"mm\">(0,0)</span>; accept at <span class=\"mm\">(1,0)</span>.</li><li>All four states are reachable (<span class=\"mm\">ε, a, b, ab</span>) and pairwise distinguishable: <span class=\"mm\">ε</span> isolates <span class=\"mm\">(1,0)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">a</span> separates <span class=\"mm\">(0,1)</span> from <span class=\"mm\">(0,0)</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> separates <span class=\"mm\">(1,1)</span> from <span class=\"mm\">(1,0)</span> after one more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>. So (A) and (B) are true.</li><li>For the complement, keep the same four states and declare <span class=\"mm\">(0,0), (0,1), (1,1)</span> final instead. The machine is still complete, all states reachable, and every pair still distinguishable — the same witness strings work — so the minimal complement also has 4 states. (C) is true.</li><li>(D) is false. <span class=\"mm\">bbb</span> has odd length but an odd number of <span class=\"mm\">b</span>'s, so it is not in <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. (In fact <span class=\"mm\">L</span> forces odd length, but not every odd-length word qualifies.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that the complement of a 4-state language must need a different number of states. Complementation is a relabelling of final states, so it preserves minimality exactly.</p>"}, {"id": "TOC-U1-Q75", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "regular-languages-fa", "subtopic": "dfa-construction-minimization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Over Σ = {0, 1}, how many states does the minimal complete DFA have that accepts exactly those binary strings (leading zeros allowed) whose value is congruent to 1 or 2 modulo 7?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A congruence condition modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is decided by the residue of the value read so far, and the transition <span class=\"mm\">r ↦ (2r + b) mod m</span> keeps the residue exact. The accepting states are the residues in the target set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>States <span class=\"mm\">q₀, …, q₆</span> for residues <span class=\"mm\">0 … 6</span>; start at <span class=\"mm\">q₀</span> (the empty string denotes 0); final states <span class=\"mm\">q₁</span> and <span class=\"mm\">q₂</span>.</li><li>Transitions: <span class=\"mm\">δ(q_r, b) = q_{(2r + b) mod 7}</span>. All 7 states are reachable, since <span class=\"mm\">2</span> is invertible modulo the prime <span class=\"mm\">7</span>, so the map <span class=\"mm\">r ↦ 2r</span> permutes the residues and appending bits walks through all of them.</li><li>They are pairwise distinguishable: for <span class=\"mm\">r ≠ r′</span> the difference <span class=\"mm\">r − r′</span> is invertible modulo 7, so appending <span class=\"mm\">k</span> zeros multiplies the difference by <span class=\"mm\">2^k</span> and never makes it 0; some continuation therefore lands in an accepting residue for exactly one of the two states.</li><li>Hence exactly 7 states, the residue count itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 2 states because \"the answer is yes or no\". The machine has to compute the residue before it can decide, and all seven residues are needed as intermediate memory even though only two of them accept.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q51", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A string <span class=\"mm\">w &isin; L(G)</span> has exactly two distinct derivation trees in a context-free grammar <span class=\"mm\">G</span>. Which one of the following is necessarily TRUE?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">w</span> has exactly two distinct leftmost derivations and exactly two distinct rightmost derivations.", "<span class=\"mm\">w</span> has at least two leftmost derivations but possibly only one rightmost derivation.", "<span class=\"mm\">w</span> has exactly one leftmost derivation and exactly two rightmost derivations.", "<span class=\"mm\">w</span> has exactly two derivations in total."], "answer_display": "Option (A).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Derivation trees and leftmost (or rightmost) derivations are in exact one-to-one correspondence: a tree is recovered from a leftmost derivation by forgetting the order of expansion, and a leftmost derivation is recovered from a tree by always expanding the leftmost nonterminal leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each derivation tree determines exactly one leftmost derivation: expand the leftmost nonterminal at every step. Two different trees therefore give two different leftmost derivations.</li><li>The same reading works from the right, so each tree also determines exactly one rightmost derivation.</li><li>Two trees therefore give exactly two leftmost and exactly two rightmost derivations, and no more, because every leftmost derivation maps back to one of those two trees.</li><li>The total number of derivations is larger and unbounded: expanding the nonterminals in different orders gives many derivations that all correspond to the same two trees, so the total is at least 2&#183;2 = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating &#8220;derivation&#8221; and &#8220;leftmost derivation&#8221; as the same thing, or forgetting that one tree is compatible with several expansion orders. Only the leftmost (or rightmost) count is tied to the number of trees.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q52", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a | b</span>. The complete derivation tree of the string <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> is drawn with one node for every symbol occurrence. How many nodes does that tree have in total, counting internal nodes and leaves together?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a derivation tree the leaves spell the derived string, and every internal node is one application of a production of the form A &rarr; BC. The shape is therefore a binary tree whose leaves are exactly the symbols of the string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The yield is <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>, so the tree has exactly 4 leaves.</li><li>Every internal node of this grammar has exactly two children (the only branching production is <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span>), so the tree is a full binary tree.</li><li>A full binary tree with <span class=\"mm\">L</span> leaves has exactly <span class=\"mm\">L &minus; 1</span> internal nodes: 4 &minus; 1 = 3.</li><li>Total nodes = internal + leaves = 3 + 4 = 7.</li><li>The grammar is ambiguous and <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> has five derivation trees, but every one of them is a full binary tree with 4 leaves, so the answer does not depend on which tree is drawn.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the three internal nodes, or adding the leaves twice. A full binary tree with L leaves has L &minus; 1 internal nodes, so the total is 2L &minus; 1 = 7 here.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q53", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span> be a context-free grammar. Which one of the following is <b>NOT</b> true of every derivation tree of <span class=\"mm\">G</span>?</p>", "options": ["The root is labelled <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.", "Every internal node is labelled by a nonterminal.", "Every leaf is labelled by a terminal of <span class=\"mm\">T</span>.", "The children of an internal node, read from left to right, spell the right side of some production whose left side is that node&#8217;s label."], "answer_display": "Option (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A derivation tree is built by applying productions: a node labelled A gets children spelling the right side of a production A &rarr; &alpha;. Leaves are symbols that were never expanded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root is always the start symbol <span class=\"mm\">S</span> &#8212; that is the definition of a derivation tree for a string of <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span>.</li><li>An internal node is a symbol that was expanded, and only nonterminals have productions, so every internal node carries a nonterminal.</li><li>A leaf is a symbol that was never expanded. Terminals can never be expanded, but neither can a nonterminal that was replaced by &epsilon;: in the tree for a derivation that uses <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, the node labelled <span class=\"mm\">A</span> has a single child labelled &epsilon;, and &epsilon; is not a terminal of <span class=\"mm\">T</span>.</li><li>The children of a node are written by applying one production, so their labels in order are the right side of a production for that label.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) is the statement that can fail, so (C) is the answer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every leaf is a terminal. Leaves labelled &epsilon; appear exactly when an &epsilon;-production is used, which is why &epsilon;-free grammars are the ones where &#8220;leaves = symbols of w&#8221; is safe.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q54", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G = (V, T, P, S)</span> be a context-free grammar and let <span class=\"mm\">&alpha;</span> be a string over <span class=\"mm\">V &cup; T</span>. Select every statement that is <b>TRUE</b>.</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr;* &alpha;</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">&alpha;</span> is the frontier of some partial derivation tree whose root is <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.", "The frontier of every partial derivation tree rooted at <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is a sentential form of <span class=\"mm\">G</span>.", "If a subtree of a derivation tree has root <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and frontier <span class=\"mm\">&beta;</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">A &rArr;* &beta;</span>.", "In the frontier of any partial derivation tree, every terminal lies to the left of every nonterminal."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A partial derivation tree stops expanding some leaves; its frontier is read left to right. Frontier and derivability are two views of the same relation: <span class=\"mm\">A &rArr;* &beta;</span> exactly when <span class=\"mm\">&beta;</span> is the frontier of a tree rooted at <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: take the derivation <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr;* &alpha;</span> and draw the tree for each step; stopping after the last step gives a partial tree with frontier <span class=\"mm\">&alpha;</span>.</li><li>(B) true: the root is <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, and the sequence of productions read off the tree is a valid derivation, so the frontier is reachable from <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</li><li>(C) true: read the productions of that subtree from the root downwards; they form a derivation of <span class=\"mm\">&beta;</span> from <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</li><li>(D) false: in <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> the sentential form <span class=\"mm\">aSb</span> has the terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span> to the left of the nonterminal <span class=\"mm\">S</span> and the terminal <span class=\"mm\">b</span> to its right.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a frontier must be &#8220;all terminals&#8221; or &#8220;terminals first&#8221;. A frontier mixes terminals and nonterminals in whatever order the productions produced them.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q55", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> have the productions <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aA | a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; bB | &epsilon;</span>. Which one of the following is <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : m &ge; 0, n &ge; 0 }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : m &ge; 1, n &ge; 1 }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : m &ge; 0, n &ge; 1 }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : m &ge; 1, n &ge; 0 }</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The language of a grammar is read off the reachable derivations of each nonterminal. Here <span class=\"mm\">A</span> produces one or more <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">B</span> produces zero or more <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">S</span> concatenates the two blocks.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> has the derivations <span class=\"mm\">A &rArr; a, A &rArr; aA &rArr; aa, &hellip;</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">A</span> generates <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>m</sup></span> for every <span class=\"mm\">m &ge; 1</span>, and nothing else.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B</span> has the derivations <span class=\"mm\">B &rArr; &epsilon;, B &rArr; bB &rArr; b, &hellip;</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">B</span> generates <span class=\"mm\">b<sup>n</sup></span> for every <span class=\"mm\">n &ge; 0</span>, and nothing else.</li><li>Every derivation of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> is <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; AB</span> followed by a derivation of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and then of <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, so every string of <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span> has the form <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span> with <span class=\"mm\">m &ge; 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">n &ge; 0</span>.</li><li>Conversely every such string is generated by choosing <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; AB</span> and then the two derivations, so the two sets are equal.</li><li>The empty string is not in <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span>, because <span class=\"mm\">A</span> cannot vanish.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that <span class=\"mm\">A</span> cannot produce &epsilon;, which wrongly admits strings with no <span class=\"mm\">a</span> at all, or forgetting that <span class=\"mm\">B</span> may produce &epsilon;, which wrongly forces at least one <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q56", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | Sb | c</span>. A complete derivation tree of the string <span class=\"mm\">aacbbb</span> is drawn. How many internal (non-leaf) nodes does it contain?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each application of a production adds one level to the tree: the node being expanded becomes internal and its right side becomes its children. In this grammar every production emits exactly one terminal, so the number of internal nodes equals the number of symbols in the derived string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every production of <span class=\"mm\">G</span> adds exactly one terminal symbol and keeps exactly one nonterminal (<span class=\"mm\">aS</span> and <span class=\"mm\">Sb</span>) or none (<span class=\"mm\">c</span>).</li><li>The string <span class=\"mm\">aacbbb</span> has 6 symbols, so the derivation uses exactly 6 productions.</li><li>Each of those 6 productions makes one node internal, so the tree has 6 internal nodes; the 6 leaves are the 6 terminals.</li><li>One such derivation: <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; aS &rArr; aaS &rArr; aaSb &rArr; aaSbb &rArr; aaSbbb &rArr; aacbbb</span>.</li><li>The grammar is in fact ambiguous for strings that mix <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> (the prepends and appends can be interleaved in several orders), but every parse uses the same six productions, so the count is well defined.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-nodes that appear in one particular derivation, or counting leaves as internal nodes. Here every production emits one terminal, so internal nodes = |w|.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q57", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cfg-derivation-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | bSa | SS | &epsilon;</span>. How many distinct derivation trees does <span class=\"mm\">G</span> have for the string <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span>?</p>", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two derivations give different trees exactly when they use different productions at some node. For this grammar every tree for a string with equal numbers of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> splits that string into balanced blocks, and the count is a small dynamic-programming table over substrings.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> has equal numbers of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so it can be derived at all.</li><li>Consider the root. If the root uses <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span>, the string must begin with <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and end with <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, which fails because <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> ends with <span class=\"mm\">b</span>. The same argument rules out <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; bSa</span>.</li><li>If the root uses <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span>, the split <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> = <span class=\"mm\">a</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">abb</span> is the only one where both halves are derivable: <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">abb</span>. The other candidate splits (<span class=\"mm\">aa</span>, <span class=\"mm\">bb</span>) leave halves with unequal numbers of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>The substring <span class=\"mm\">abb</span> itself has a unique tree, again forced to split as <span class=\"mm\">a</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">bb</span>, and each of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">bb</span> has exactly one tree.</li><li>So exactly one tree exists, even though the grammar is ambiguous in general &#8212; for <span class=\"mm\">abab</span> it has two trees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B): 1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding &#8220;ambiguous grammar, so more than one tree&#8221;. Ambiguity is a property of the grammar, not of each individual string: this grammar is ambiguous, yet <span class=\"mm\">aabb</span> has a single parse.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q58", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Every regular language has an unambiguous context-free grammar.", "Every context-free language has an unambiguous grammar.", "A language that has an ambiguous grammar cannot be context-free.", "Every ambiguous grammar generates an infinite language."], "answer_display": "Option (A).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ambiguity is a property of a grammar; a language is called unambiguous when <i>some</i> grammar for it is unambiguous, and inherently ambiguous when no such grammar exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: take a DFA for the regular language and convert it to a right-linear grammar with one nonterminal per state. Each string labels exactly one accepting path, so each string has exactly one derivation.</li><li>(B) false: inherently ambiguous context-free languages exist, for example <span class=\"mm\">{ a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>k</sup> : i = j or j = k }</span>.</li><li>(C) false: an ambiguous grammar is still a grammar, and it generates a context-free language.</li><li>(D) false: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S | a</span> generates the finite language <span class=\"mm\">{a}</span> and is ambiguous for it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading &#8220;some grammar for L is ambiguous&#8221; as &#8220;L is ambiguous&#8221;. Every nonempty language with a context-free grammar also has silly ambiguous grammars; the question is whether an unambiguous one exists.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q59", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following grammars is <b>unambiguous</b>?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | Sa | a</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S + S | a</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grammar is unambiguous when every string in its language has exactly one derivation tree. Grammars that offer two independent ways to build the same string &#8212; splitting it, or adding symbols on the left or the right &#8212; are usually ambiguous.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is ambiguous: <span class=\"mm\">aaa</span> has the two trees <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; aS &rArr; aSS &hellip;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; SSa &hellip;</span>.</li><li>(B) is unambiguous: every string it generates is <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>, n &ge; 1</span>, and the only possible first step is <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> or <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; ab</span>. For <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span> the first step must be <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> (the string is longer than <span class=\"mm\">ab</span>), which forces the outermost pair of symbols and leaves a shorter string of the same shape; induction on the length gives exactly one tree.</li><li>(C) is ambiguous: <span class=\"mm\">aa</span> has one tree from <span class=\"mm\">aS</span> followed by <span class=\"mm\">Sa</span> and another from <span class=\"mm\">Sa</span> followed by <span class=\"mm\">aS</span>.</li><li>(D) is ambiguous: <span class=\"mm\">a + a + a</span> has two trees, one adding before the other.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging ambiguity by how complicated the grammar looks. <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</span> looks recursive but is unambiguous, while <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a</span> looks simple but is ambiguous.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q60", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement about context-free grammars that is <b>TRUE</b>.</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">G</span> is ambiguous, <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span> may still have an unambiguous grammar.", "An unambiguous grammar can have two distinct derivations of the same string.", "If every string in <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span> has exactly one derivation tree, then <span class=\"mm\">G</span> is unambiguous.", "Ambiguity of a context-free grammar is decidable in polynomial time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grammar is ambiguous when some string of its language has two distinct derivation trees. Derivations themselves are not unique in an unambiguous grammar: only the tree is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: a language can be generated by an ambiguous grammar and also by an unambiguous one, for example <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup></span> with <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aS | Sb | ab</span> is ambiguous, while <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</span> is unambiguous and generates the same language.</li><li>(B) true: the leftmost and the rightmost derivation of a string with two or more symbols differ as sequences of sentential forms even though they describe the same tree.</li><li>(C) true: that is exactly the definition of unambiguity, stated string by string.</li><li>(D) false: deciding ambiguity of a context-free grammar is undecidable in general; only for restricted classes (such as deterministic grammars, or bounded lookahead) is it decidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing &#8220;unique derivation&#8221; with &#8220;unique derivation tree&#8221;. Only the second is the definition of unambiguity.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q61", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">G</span> be the grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a | b</span>. How many distinct derivation trees does <span class=\"mm\">G</span> have for the string <span class=\"mm\">aab</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a single branching production <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span>, a derivation tree is a full binary tree whose leaves spell the string, and the number of such trees is counted by splitting the string into a nonempty prefix and a nonempty suffix.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root must use <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span>, because <span class=\"mm\">aab</span> has three symbols and no other production of <span class=\"mm\">G</span> produces more than one symbol.</li><li>The two possible splits into nonempty parts are <span class=\"mm\">a</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">ab</span> and <span class=\"mm\">aa</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">a</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">ab</span> the right part <span class=\"mm\">ab</span> has the single tree <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; ab</span>, so this split gives one tree.</li><li>For <span class=\"mm\">aa</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">b</span> the left part <span class=\"mm\">aa</span> has the single tree <span class=\"mm\">S &rArr; SS &rArr; aa</span>, so this split gives one tree.</li><li>Total: 1 + 1 = 2 trees, so the grammar is ambiguous for <span class=\"mm\">aab</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 by assuming the grammar is unambiguous because it is so small, or answering 3 by allowing the empty split <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>&#183;<span class=\"mm\">aab</span>.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q62", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L = { a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>k</sup> : i = j or j = k, i, j, k &ge; 1 }</span>, with <span class=\"mm\">L</span>. Which one of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free and has an unambiguous grammar.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free, and every grammar for <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is ambiguous.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is regular."], "answer_display": "Option (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is the union of the two context-free languages <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>i</sup>c<sup>k</sup>}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>j</sup>}</span>, so it is context-free. It is the standard example of an <i>inherently ambiguous</i> context-free language: the two conditions <span class=\"mm\">i = j</span> and <span class=\"mm\">j = k</span> pull in different directions, and any grammar must sometimes delay the decision past the point where the information needed to make it is still available.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Closure under union gives that <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S<sub>1</sub> | S<sub>2</sub></span> with <span class=\"mm\">S<sub>1</sub> &rarr; aS<sub>1</sub>c | X, X &rarr; aXb | ab</span> generates the first part and <span class=\"mm\">S<sub>2</sub> &rarr; aS<sub>2</sub> | Y, Y &rarr; bYc | bc</span> generates the second.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not regular, since intersecting it with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*c*</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>}</span>, which is not regular.</li><li>For a string <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup></span> with <span class=\"mm\">i = j = k</span> the two reasons coincide, and the classic pumping argument shows that no single grammar can decide early enough which of the two equalities holds without also being able to count all three blocks; the language is therefore inherently ambiguous.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free and every grammar for it is ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding &#8220;not context-free&#8221; because <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>}</span> is not, or &#8220;unambiguous&#8221; because each of the two parts is a deterministic language. Union of two unambiguous languages need not be unambiguous.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q63", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "ambiguity-in-grammars", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement that is <b>TRUE</b>.</p>", "options": ["Every deterministic context-free language has an unambiguous grammar.", "The union of two unambiguous context-free languages can be inherently ambiguous.", "It is decidable whether a given context-free grammar is ambiguous.", "If every string of <span class=\"mm\">L(G)</span> has exactly one rightmost derivation, then <span class=\"mm\">G</span> is unambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Unambiguity is preserved by determinism (a DPDA makes one choice at every step), but not by union, and ambiguity of an arbitrary grammar is undecidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: run the deterministic pushdown automaton on a string. Its run is unique, so the string has one leftmost derivation, hence one tree.</li><li>(B) true: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>i</sup>c<sup>k</sup>}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>i</sup>b<sup>j</sup>c<sup>j</sup>}</span> are unambiguous, but their union is inherently ambiguous, so no grammar for the union is unambiguous.</li><li>(C) false: ambiguity of context-free grammars is undecidable &#8212; it is one of the standard consequences of the Post correspondence problem.</li><li>(D) true: rightmost derivations and trees are in one-to-one correspondence, so a unique rightmost derivation per string means a unique tree per string.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming decidability because the definition of ambiguity is a simple statement about trees. Deciding whether two different trees exist for some string is exactly the hard part.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q64", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A grammar is in Greibach Normal Form when every production has the form <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a&alpha;</span> with <span class=\"mm\">a &isin; T</span> and <span class=\"mm\">&alpha; &isin; V*</span>. Which one of the following productions <b>violates</b> that form?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aBC</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; Ba</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aB</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Greibach Normal Form makes every production start with exactly one terminal. That single property is what forces every derivation of a string of length n to take exactly n steps.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aBC</span>: the right side starts with the terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and the rest is a string of nonterminals, so it is allowed.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>: a terminal followed by the empty string of nonterminals, allowed.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; Ba</span>: the right side starts with the nonterminal <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, so it violates the form.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; aB</span>: a terminal followed by one nonterminal, allowed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking GNF forbids terminals anywhere but first, or forbids nonterminals entirely. Terminals may appear only in the first position; nonterminals may appear only after it.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q65", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | &epsilon;</span> is converted to Chomsky Normal Form by the standard construction: first new nonterminals are introduced for the terminals, then every right side longer than two symbols is split. How many new nonterminal symbols does the construction introduce?</p>", "options": ["2", "1", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (A).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CNF allows only <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>. A terminal may not appear next to another symbol, so each terminal used inside a longer right side needs its own nonterminal; a right side of length three then needs one more nonterminal to split it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The production <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> has terminals inside a right side of length 3, so introduce <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span>.</li><li>Now <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> becomes <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; ASB</span>, still too long, so introduce one more nonterminal <span class=\"mm\">X</span> and write <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AX</span> and <span class=\"mm\">X &rarr; SB</span>.</li><li>The count is therefore 2 for the terminals. The third symbol <span class=\"mm\">X</span> above is required by the splitting step, not by the terminal step, and the question asks only for the terminals.</li><li>The &epsilon;-production <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is kept as it is, which introduces nothing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A): 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 by counting the splitting nonterminal <span class=\"mm\">X</span> as well, or answering 1 by forgetting that both <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> occur inside a long right side and each needs its own nonterminal.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q66", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is TRUE of every grammar in Chomsky Normal Form?</p>", "options": ["It cannot generate <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>.", "Every derivation tree of it is a full binary tree.", "Every production has exactly two nonterminals on its right side.", "Every production has at most two symbols on its right side."], "answer_display": "Option (D).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CNF admits exactly two shapes: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</span> (two nonterminals) and <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> (one terminal), with the single exception <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> when the language contains the empty string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) false: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is permitted when <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon; &isin; L(G)</span>, so a CNF grammar can generate <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>.</li><li>(B) false: a node expanded by <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> has a single child, so the tree is not full.</li><li>(C) false: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> has one symbol on the right.</li><li>(D) true: every right side is either one terminal or two nonterminals, so at most two symbols.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing CNF forbids <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-productions absolutely, or that every internal node has two children. Only branching nodes have two; terminal productions have one.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q67", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement that is <b>TRUE</b>.</p>", "options": ["Every context-free language that does not contain <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> has a grammar in Chomsky Normal Form.", "Every grammar in Greibach Normal Form is free of left recursion.", "In a CNF grammar, every derivation of a string of length <span class=\"mm\">n &ge; 1</span> uses exactly <span class=\"mm\">2n &minus; 1</span> production steps.", "Eliminating <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-productions can change the language generated by the grammar."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The normal-form theorems say that CNF and GNF exist for every <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-free context-free language, and the step counts follow from the shapes of the productions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: that is the Chomsky Normal Form theorem; only <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> needs the extra production <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span>.</li><li>(B) true: a left-recursive production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A&alpha;</span> has a nonterminal in first position, which GNF forbids.</li><li>(C) true: with n leaves the tree has n &minus; 1 branching nodes, each one step, plus n terminal steps, so <span class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 1) + n = 2n &minus; 1</span> steps.</li><li>(D) false: the <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-elimination procedure is language preserving &#8212; it only removes productions whose use can be simulated by deleting occurrences.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the conversion steps are heuristic rewrites that may change the language. Each of the four simplification steps (useless symbols, <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-productions, unit productions, long right sides) preserves the generated language exactly.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q68", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The grammar <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb | ab</span> is converted to Chomsky Normal Form by the standard construction. How many productions does the resulting CNF grammar have?</p>", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The construction keeps one production per original right side, adds one production per terminal that appears inside a longer right side, and adds one production for every split of a right side longer than two symbols.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Introduce <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span> for the two terminals: 2 productions.</li><li>Rewrite <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> as <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; ASB</span>, which has three symbols and must be split, for example <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AX</span> and <span class=\"mm\">X &rarr; SB</span>: 2 more productions.</li><li>Rewrite <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; ab</span> as <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; AB</span>: 1 more production.</li><li>Total 2 + 2 + 1 = 5 productions: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a, B &rarr; b, S &rarr; AX, X &rarr; SB, S &rarr; AB</span>.</li><li>The grammar is in CNF and generates the same language <span class=\"mm\">a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>, n &ge; 1</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B): 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the two terminal productions and answering 3, or forgetting that <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; aSb</span> cannot stay as it is because a terminal may not sit beside another symbol.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q69", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "cnf-gnf-normal-forms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following statements is <b>TRUE</b>?</p>", "options": ["Some context-free languages have no grammar in Chomsky Normal Form.", "A grammar in Greibach Normal Form may contain the production <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>.", "Every grammar in Chomsky Normal Form is unambiguous.", "Every context-free language that does not contain <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> has a grammar in Greibach Normal Form."], "answer_display": "Option (D).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both normal-form theorems hold for every <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-free context-free language. Normal forms restrict the shape of productions; they say nothing about ambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) false: every context-free language has a CNF grammar, with <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; &epsilon;</span> added exactly when <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon; &isin; L</span>.</li><li>(B) false: GNF requires a terminal in first position, so <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is excluded; at most the start symbol is allowed to derive <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>, as a separate convention.</li><li>(C) false: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | a</span> is in CNF (via <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a</span>, <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS</span>, <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A</span>) and is ambiguous.</li><li>(D) true: that is the Greibach Normal Form theorem, proved by eliminating left recursion and ordering the nonterminals.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating a normal form with unambiguity or determinism. CNF and GNF are shape restrictions; ambiguous grammars have normal forms too.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q70", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The class of context-free languages is <b>NOT</b> closed under which one of the following operations?</p>", "options": ["union", "concatenation", "complement", "Kleene star"], "answer_display": "Option (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Context-free languages are closed under union, concatenation and Kleene star, because a new start symbol can combine two grammars (or repeat one). Complement is the standard exception.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Union: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S<sub>1</sub> | S<sub>2</sub></span> with <span class=\"mm\">S<sub>1</sub></span> and <span class=\"mm\">S<sub>2</sub></span>.</li><li>Concatenation: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; S<sub>1</sub>S<sub>2</sub></span>. Kleene star: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; SS | &epsilon;</span>.</li><li>Complement: <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>}</span> is context-free, but its complement intersected with <span class=\"mm\">a*b*c*</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>}</span>, and that intersection is not context-free &#8212; the class is not closed under complement.</li><li>Since a class closed under complement and union would be closed under intersection, and it is not, complement cannot preserve context-freeness.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that because regular languages are closed under complement, context-free languages are too. The regular case is special: a DFA can be complemented by swapping final and non-final states.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q71", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The CYK algorithm decides membership in a context-free language by filling an entry <span class=\"mm\">t[i, j]</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">1 &le; i &le; j &le; n</span>. For an input string of length 6, how many entries does the table have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "21", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CYK fills the upper triangle of an <span class=\"mm\">n &times; n</span> table: one entry per substring of the input, and a string of length <span class=\"mm\">n</span> has exactly <span class=\"mm\">n(n+1)/2</span> substrings.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Substrings are fixed by a start position <span class=\"mm\">i</span> and an end position <span class=\"mm\">j</span> with <span class=\"mm\">i &le; j</span>.</li><li>For each length <span class=\"mm\">L</span> there are <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; L + 1</span> substrings, so the total is <span class=\"mm\">&Sigma;<sub>L=1..n</sub> (n &minus; L + 1) = n(n+1)/2</span>.</li><li>With <span class=\"mm\">n = 6</span>: 6 &times; 7 / 2 = 21.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 21.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 36 by counting the whole <span class=\"mm\">6 &times; 6</span> table, or 15 by forgetting the single-symbol entries.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q72", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Select every statement that is <b>TRUE</b>.</p>", "options": ["Emptiness of a context-free language is decidable.", "Equivalence of two context-free languages is decidable.", "The class of context-free languages is closed under homomorphism.", "The class of context-free languages is closed under inverse homomorphism."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Closure is generous for homomorphic operations and for union-like operations, and poor for intersection and complement. Decidability is generous for emptiness and membership, and poor for equivalence and ambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: reduce the grammar, and the language is empty exactly when the start symbol is removed as unreachable or non-generating.</li><li>(B) false: equivalence of context-free languages is undecidable; only inclusion into a regular language is decidable.</li><li>(C) true: replace every terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span> in the productions by the string <span class=\"mm\">h(a)</span>; the result is a grammar for the image.</li><li>(D) true: build a pushdown automaton that reads <span class=\"mm\">h(w)</span> and simulates the original automaton on <span class=\"mm\">w</span>, buffering the images in its finite control.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Generalising &#8220;emptiness and membership are decidable&#8221; to &#8220;equivalence is decidable&#8221;. The undecidability of equivalence is one of the sharpest differences from the regular case.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q73", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L</span> be a context-free language and <span class=\"mm\">R</span> a regular language. Which one of the following is necessarily context-free?</p>", "options": ["the complement of <span class=\"mm\">L</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">L &minus; R</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">L &cap; L&prime;</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">L&prime;</span> is context-free", "the set of strings that belong to neither <span class=\"mm\">L</span> nor <span class=\"mm\">R</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A context-free language intersected with a <i>regular</i> language is context-free (run the grammar in parallel with the DFA). Intersections and complements among context-free languages are where closure fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) false: context-free languages are not closed under complement.</li><li>(B) true: <span class=\"mm\">L &minus; R = L &cap; (&#931;* &minus; R)</span>, and the complement of a regular language is regular, so this is a context-free language intersected with a regular one.</li><li>(C) false: the intersection of two context-free languages need not be context-free.</li><li>(D) false: that set is the complement of <span class=\"mm\">L &cup; R</span>, and neither complement nor union with a context-free language preserves context-freeness.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating &#8220;context-free &#8745; regular&#8221; and &#8220;context-free &#8745; context-free&#8221; as the same. Only the first is guaranteed.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q74", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"mm\">L = { a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : n &ge; 0 }</span>. Which one of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is not context-free.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free, but its complement is not.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free, and so is its complement.", "<span class=\"mm\">L</span> is context-free, but <span class=\"mm\">L*</span> is not."], "answer_display": "Option (C).", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is accepted by a deterministic pushdown automaton (push on <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, pop on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>), and the class of deterministic context-free languages is closed under complement even though the whole class of context-free languages is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A DPDA accepts <span class=\"mm\">L</span>: push one symbol per <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, pop one per <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, accept at the end of the input with an empty stack.</li><li>Deterministic context-free languages are closed under complement: swap the accepting and rejecting states of the equivalent DPDA with a total transition function.</li><li>So the complement of <span class=\"mm\">L</span> is deterministic context-free, hence context-free.</li><li>(D) is false because <span class=\"mm\">L*</span> is just <span class=\"mm\">L</span>, which is context-free.</li><li>Note that this does not contradict the non-closure of context-free languages under complement: the witness for that failure is a nondeterministic language.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying &#8220;context-free languages are not closed under complement&#8221; to a single deterministic language. The class-level failure does not mean every context-free language has a non-context-free complement.</p>"},{"id": "TOC-U2-Q75", "subject": "theory-of-computation", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "cfl-grammars", "subtopic": "closure-properties-cfl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider these nine operations on languages: union, intersection, complement, concatenation, Kleene star, homomorphism, inverse homomorphism, reversal, and intersection with a regular language. For how many of the nine is the class of context-free languages closed?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Closure of context-free languages holds for the &#8220;grammar-building&#8221; operations (union, concatenation, star), for homomorphic operations, for reversal, and for intersection with a regular language. It fails for intersection and complement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Closed: union (new start symbol with two alternatives), concatenation, Kleene star, homomorphism (replace terminals by strings), inverse homomorphism, reversal (reverse every right side), intersection with a regular language (product with a DFA) &#8212; 7 operations.</li><li>Not closed: intersection (witness <span class=\"mm\">{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>} = {a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>m</sup>} &cap; {a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup>c<sup>n</sup>}</span>) and complement (a class closed under complement and union would be closed under intersection).</li><li>So 7 of the 9 operations preserve context-freeness.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting complement or intersection as closed because regular languages are closed under both, or missing the two homomorphism operations, which are easy to overlook.</p>"}];